      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>~t,14 . ,</head>
        <p>~t,14 . ,</p>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>" Tim Bobbinel I was greatly interested in that Warrington R.F.A. gunner's literary discovery at Lytham. I happen to be familiar- with the book which fcrrms the subject of his welcome communica- tion.. From early youth, when the counter- feit presentment of the doughty " Tim " at- tracted me to a penny waxwork show, I have .eagerly devoured all I could lay hands on respecting this pioneer writer in the Lana- shire dialect. I never go to Rochdale without -visiting the versatile old man's last resting- place, and refreshing my memory with a glance at the characteristic rhyming epitaph beginning: "Here lies Tim and likewise Mary,</p>
        <p>Cheek by jowl they never vary afterwards dropping in at the museum in con- nection with the Free Library and Art Gallery for a glimpse at Tim's quaint coloured sketches; and then crossing the road to a point near the park entrance, where an ap- preciative public some years ago erected a monument to perpetuate the memory of this and three other local dialect writers, of whom Roc.hdale has reason to be proud. I know of onky one other town where anything of that kind has been done, and in that instance I wait present at the unveiling of the memorial. I may justradd that as a young man in Halifax I made the acquaintance of John Hartley, the Yorkshire poet, dr.c., who has published much in the dialect that will endure. When last I hoard of him he was living in Cheshire, a poor, lonely, old man having just lost his Auto—the " Many " of his humorous works. The Author of " Tom Brown."</p>
        <p>"Those who are familiar with this school classic." Thus opened lat week's editorial tribute to the late Mr. RObson, whose death in the plenitude of- his powers we all lament. The reference sent me back in thought to a period when I repeatedly enjoyed the privilege of meeting the late Judge Hughes, the genial creator of the immortal "Tom Brown." I was on the staff of a paper within whose area of circulation was Hebden Bridge, where the wholesale clothing trade flourishes. Judge Hughes, who belonged to the Kingsley school , of Christian Socialists, was actively interested in a co-operative factory there, and, along with the late Marquess of Ripon and other notabilities, was wont periodically to run over a-nd encourage the little band of working men engaged in that industrial enterprise, then in the experimental stages, but long ago well assured of success. Judge Hughes was a most enthusiastic supporter of the venture, and once went so far as to array himself in a suit of fustian manufactured at these works, in which he was a shareholder. Well, and why not? Has the reader ever considered the origin of the word corduroy (corde du roi)?</p>
        <p>Perpetual Motion. It was in. that same region--in the adjacent village of Mytholmroyd--that I cameacross one who claimed to have solved the problem of per- petual motion. I was reminded of him as I read at the foot of page 4 in last week's "Ex- aminer," "When one good turn begets an- other we shall have perpetual motion." It in extremely pathetic to contemplate such characters ; they are so desperately in earnest and so cocksure. I think of another whom I had under observation for some years. As a matter of fact, he was financed by my em- ployer at the time, and in every spare moment came to the office to work at his jealously- guarded secret. Gradually there emerged a wonderful contraption in iron, having securely tied about it, when not in actual process of development under the master hand, a thick wrap of impenetrable material. Poor fellow Yet I hardly knew which was the moie re- markable-='-the persistence of the crazy inven- tor himself, or the childlike innocence of the newspaper proprietor in pouring money into an abyss obviously' bottomless. The in- genious one died befdre he had succeeded in getting the thing to complete a single revolu- tion, and, his patron having also passed away, the puzzled heirs and executors had no alternative but to scrap the costly contriv- ance.</p>
        <p>Those Incorrigible Boys. The venerable Rev. J. Yonge's exceedingly I regrettable mishap while endeavouring to pre- vent mischievous boys from firing ornamental vegetation, reminded me of other experiences in the district just referred to. It lay on the very threshold of the home of the red grouse, whose annual "festival fell due on Thurs- day. Stretching 'away on this side and on that were countless thousands of acres of heather-clad moorlands, and at each recur- ring season, beginning as early as Easter Sun- day, gangs of irresponsible youngsters system- atically engaged in a game which they called "-sniddling." Armed with boxes of matches, they roamed over the hills and moors set- tipg fire to whatever growing wild stuff was found sufficiently combustible, so that the g`enerally sWeet mountain air was for the time being heavily laden with a pungent odour and the horizon obscured with vile smoke for the creation of which the common excuse of com- mercialism was entirely lacking. In those vast spaces the detection of the offenders was, difficult, and these young marauders were alike the ,terror and the despair of all inter- ested in the damaged property. • Occasionally a party happened to be caught in the act; then indeed did outraged authority administer, unto theta several varieties of beans, so to express it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>Hoist With His Own Petard.</head>
        <p>Hoist With His Own Petard.</p>
        <p>The case of the squire who, as narrated by an "Interested Reader," brought a bold " bobber's " rod upon himself, recalled for me an amusing instance occurring in a district in. which I formerly resided. The squire of those parts, long since departed this life, was a director of the big railway company having a monopoly of that district. One night, at a pOint about a_ score miles from home, he missed the last train. A little later he at- tempted to enter the brake van of a goods train that was to travel his way, but was- roughly ejected by the burly guard. He gave the man his ,name, and told him of the high official-position he held, but the former (who knew him quite well, and was aware he had a tartar to deal with) retorted, "I don't care who you are; my instructions are to allow no- body to ride with me in this van, and I'm going to obey 'em, whatever else." It was well for him that he took that stand, for the railway magnate, while evidently ready to avail himself of a breach of discipline, would himself have reported the guard had he broken the rule to accommodate him in his predica- ment; whereas., as it was, he commended the •aotute one to headquarters, and promotion immediately resulted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>NO ELECTIONS.</head>
        <p>NO ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>Co-option To Meet Casual Vacancies.</p>
        <p>The Clerk read a letter at Wednesday's meeting of the Warrington Guardians from the Local Government Board stating that the Elections ' and Registration Act (1915), which received the Royal assent on 29th July last, made pro- vision, amongst other matters, for the post- ponement for the period of a year of the elections of local authorities and other bodies and for the filling of casual vacancies. The ..Mlowing paragraph was wntained in the letter Until the new register min into force any casual vacaticy reqt.nred to be filled by election amonk the members of any County Council, Bormio Council, District Council, Board of Guilt-I-dim:is, Parish Council, instead of being fihtd by election is to be filled bye means of the choice by the Council or Board of a person to fill the vacancy, and the Councillor or Guardian so chosen is to hold office in the same manner, in all respects, as if he had been elected to fill the vacancy-;</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>SOLDIERS AS SCRIBES.</head>
        <p>SOLDIERS AS SCRIBES.</p>
        <p>Economic Suggestion At Board Of Guardians' Meeting. At the fortnightly meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians on Wednesday morning, Mr. A.. J. Jolley presided. In the minutes of the House Committee was a recommendation that the master be author- ised to engage, for the period of the war, an office boy to assist in his office, at a salary of 10s. a week. The Rev. A. M. Mitchell expressed the opinion that the Board ought to be very careful before attempting to make any fresh appointments in- .volving extra exvense.' Could not some of the soldiers be utilised in the master's office? Some of them were good scribes. The Chairman: Are you' objecting to the temporary appointment? The Rev. A. M. Mitchell: No. I only want the matter to be clearly understood so that something may be done to obviate the necessity of the appointment. Mr. J. Flanagan: The appointment is only temporary. The minutes were confirmed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>POOR LAW REGISTRATION.</head>
        <p>POOR LAW REGISTRATION.</p>
        <p>AVhat Workhouse Inmates And Vagrants Will Have To Do.</p>
        <p>A letter from the Local Government Board with reference to the registration of inmates in the Workhouse and in the casual wards, was read by the Clerk at thd meeting of the War- rington Board of Guardians on Wednesday morning. The master or person in charge (the letter said) had to deliver a form to the in- mate who was likely to take his or her dis- charge. With regard to others, nothing was going to be done. Forms would be supplied to the vagrants and they would be sent direct by him (the Clerk) to the Local Government Board. They did not go through the local authority. Mr. Mather said a question in regard to the census at the Cottage Homes at i'adgate had been raised. The enumerators visited him on the previous night. They had seen the super- intendent and matron, (Mrs. Lowrance) and they were not quite sure who was to do the work—the Clerk to the Guardians or the local enumerators at Padgate, who were prepared to do the work. The Clerk said that Mrs. Lowrance would deal with the matter.</p>
        <p>Mr. Mather: I take it that we ought to dea with it.</p>
        <p>The Clerk: Mrs. Lawrance is responsible for the filling in of the necessary particulars relating to the children. If she' requires assist- ance, the enumerators are required to give ,it. Replying to a query from Mr. Mather,</p>
        <p>' The -Cle-rk said that children over 15 were not to be registered unless the superintendent was satisfied that they were likely to be discharged within a short period. They were the people who had to have forms filled up for them, and only them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>LUXURIES OF THE POOR.</head>
        <p>LUXURIES OF THE POOR.</p>
        <p>Chairman Of Guardians Says He Won't Have Them Cut Down.</p>
        <p>At Wednesday's meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians a letter was read from the Local Government ,Board advocating economy in expenditure in every branch, whether capital or ,revenue. The Board suggested that whilst not unduly relaxing the standard of publio health administration in their area, local authorities should, as far as possible, refrain from requiring the execution of work, the cost of which has to be borne by private individuals, unless the work is urgently necessary for the removal of nuisances or the protection of health. Even in eases where some work is essential, it will be found practicable to ar- range for repairs of a temporary character to be carried out by the owners or frontagers. Mr. Mather said he thought that the Guard- ians might have exercised a little more econsamy than they had at the last nine or ten meetings.</p>
        <p>DON'T TOUCH THE SMALL LUXURIES OF</p>
        <p>THE POOR !</p>
        <p>The Chairman (Mr. A. J. Jolley) objected. He considered that the administration of that Board was as economical as it could be, with proper regard for the interests of the poor. If anyone suggested that the small luxuries that were allowed to those under their guardianship should be curtailed, he, for one, would fight against it to the last. (Hear, hear.) The relief business of that Board had been for some years transacted on the best lines, and if any rate- payer or anyone else thought that they ought to cut down the out-relief or the small luxuries of the outdoor poor, he should resent it very strongly, and firmly oppose any such reduction. The Rev. A. M. Mitchell said that he felt sure none of them would want to see the luxurieis of the poor curtailed. He thought that the Chair- man had rather misunderstood Mr. Mather's meaning. He (Mr. Mather) was referring—so the speaker thought—to the expenditure in the salaries of the officials rather than the expendi- ture on actual relief. Mr. Mather confirmed the Rev. A. M. Mitchell's explanation of his meaning, and stated that he .would not suggest the cutting down of the little comforts of the poor in their charge.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>NO ROOM FOR ECONOMY.</head>
        <p>NO ROOM FOR ECONOMY.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said he did not think there was any room for a reduction of expenses in that Union, except in the objectionable way he had suggested or the refusal to do what they had been doing for those who were fighting for them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN _OLD_ JOURNALIST.-</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN _OLD_ JOURNALIST.-</p>
        <p>Continued.</p>
        <p>, Tito Boy on the Farm. , HOW hot to do it, was the lesson of the opening paragraph in last week's." Farm and' Garden" column. It is complained-L.-and there is a good deal of truth in it—that the village lad destined to work upon the soil leaves school without an ounce of knowledge of. the simplest facts relating to rural life. The air, the soil, ~the plant, are unknown quantities. We know full well it is not thus that Denmark made her reputation, and we should take care for the future that we should no more be dependent upon that country for agricultural produce than on Germany for (Ayes. I read that the Secretary:Tor Agricul- ture in the United States recently stated that there should be a hundred thousand students in the fifty colleges of the Union—in which the fees are nominal, as I know at first hand, for a native of a district in which I used to live now holds a good position in one of those same ccilleges and I have kept in touch with him. lilngLrd is the only country in the world where agriculture is a prominent industry in</p>
        <p>Continued in next column.</p>
        <p>which a first-class education is not available to the sons of The smallest farmer at fees within his\ reach. Those already mentiOned and the chief European countries, as well as Canada, are in full enjoyment of such facilities —even Ireland is comparatively well off in this respect. We have it on unquestionable authority that at the Agricultural College at Glasnevin, education in agriculture, horticul- ture and dairy farming is supplied to lads whose parents derive their livelihood mainly, from the cultivation of the land, at a cost of £1,5 per term of eleven months, this sum in- cluding board and lodging, laundry and medical attendance. The result is unqualified sifeeess. There are, too, numerous instances in which boys are taught the theory and prac- tice of agriculture and horticulture in Irish rural schools. This is becoming a matter of extreme importance, and we cannot afford longer to neglect it.</p>
        <p>A woman can say mare in a look than a nom can it a book.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>HOW ALL CAN HELP.</head>
        <p>HOW ALL CAN HELP.</p>
        <p>Why We Should And How We Are Able To Save.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>WAYS AND MEANS.</head>
        <p>WAYS AND MEANS.</p>
        <p>During the past year many of our country- men have come forward to risk their lives in their country's cause. Many more, who are debarred by age oil sex from making this great sacrifice, have asked how they can help. All of us, young and old, rich and poor, can help their country now by saving.</p>
        <p>millions. Now it has to spend about 1,000 millions extra.</p>
        <p>THE FOUR METHODS.</p>
        <p>Let us see what would happen to it if we leave out the millions, and suppose that it is not a nation, but a man, Mr. John Bull, who usually gets an income of £2,300 a year and saves about £3OO, and suddenly finds himself obliged to spend £l,OOO a year on a lawsuit with his aggressive neighbour, Herr Schmidt, of Berlin. How can he find the money? He can do it in four ways : (1) He can sell his investments. (2) He can borrow from other people. (3) He can , draw on money he may have lying idle. (4) He can save out of the .e 2,000 a year that he usually spends. _ _</p>
        <p>If he uses any of the first three ways of raising the money, he .will come out of his lawsuit ranch poorer than he went in. If he sells his investments he is so much the poorer for the future; for he will no longer get in- terest on them. If he borrows from other people he will again be poorer, because he will have to pay interest and pay back his debt. If he draws on idle money his cash is gone, and he cannot meet his debts. The only way for him to find the money without leaving him- self poorer is to spend less on things that he used to enjoy, to eat and drink less expen- sively, smoke less, to keep fewer servants, buy</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>WHAT SAVING IS:</head>
        <p>WHAT SAVING IS:</p>
        <p>Saving means spending less than we get. Whatever we save is something kept back from being used on our present needs and held up to be used some other time, or to be added to our store of capital.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>WHY WE OUGHT .TO SAVE.</head>
        <p>WHY WE OUGHT .TO SAVE.</p>
        <p>At first sight this act of saving looks rather selfish and mean, and some thoughtless people despise those who are thrifty and prefer free- handed ,folk who throw . their money about. Those who are well off, especially,- are. gener- ally convinced that to spend all their money is good for trade and gives employment, and by this belief they jugtify all kinds of self- indulgence. But this is generally wrong at any time, and most of all at the present time. Those who save wisely now benefit themselves, but they also benefit everybody else. Because if no one saved and laid out their savings carefully, there could be no more industry. It is true that spending on luxury gives employment, but it does not give as much or as pod employment as money that is put into</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <p>industries which turn out things that are really necessary. If a man who already has a house builds himself another, he gives em- ployment, but if he puts the moneY into build- ing a cloth factory, he would .give just as Much employment in building the 'factory, he would give more and continuous employment in work- ing the factory, and he would help to make clothes cheaper. If he lent the money to someone to build -u factory, the effect would ;)e the same.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>WHY WE SHOULD SAVE NOW.</head>
        <p>WHY WE SHOULD SAVE NOW.</p>
        <p>. Now, saving is not only a good thing, but a national duty on the nart of all Who have any surplus to spare over what is necessary to their health of mind and body. Saving is a national duty now, because if we spend our money we cannot lend it to the Government which wants it for the costliest war that ever was waged; and because if we spend our Money We make people work for us, and if they work for us they cannot work for the war, and for making goods to send to and sell in foreign countries. , The more goods we can sell in _ foreign countries' the more money we get to buy in those conntriesyfood and goods for ourselves and for 'Our armies.'</p>
        <p>The Government ,needs during the war over 1,000 millions a year, and gets .a revenue, with taxes now running, of less than 270 millions: thus .it has to get over 730 millions by bor- rowing. If we do not save this amount we cannot lend it, for we cannot make money out of nothing. If we cannot lend it, the Govern- ment, which must have the money, will have to take it from us by taxing' or a forced loan, and then we shall be forced to save. Is it not better to do it voluntarily? It is true that the Government can get part of what it wants by borrowing abroad. But it cannot do much in that way, for America is the only country that has any large amount to lend; and every pound that we borrow abroad means, that we are henceforward, poorer, becauSe we have in- terest to pay on a foreign debt. In this matter of spending and borrowing a nation is just like a man. Our nation (not: t4.1.e Government) has a total income of about 2,300 million pounds a year. That is what. we make year by year and get from our in- vestments in other countries. Usually it. saves about 300 millions, and spends 9,000 i,</p>
        <p>fewer new clothes, travel, less, and go tp fewer I theatres and race meetings; and to grow and make at home more of the things that he has formerly b`ought by turning his gamekeeper and groom. and the gardener, who used to grow pretty flowers for Lim, on to the task of grow- ing vegetables, and by using his maidservants for work in the dairy, and for making and washing clothes for the household. If he does all this he pays for the lawsuit out of his own pocket, and can go on with it for years, if necessary, and then, when it is over, he can, if he likes, go back to his old way of life. It is the seine with a nation. We can sell investments and borrow abroad to a certain extent, but not much, for there are not many other people who can buy our stocks and shares, or lend us money; and if we do so we are poorer for the future, for we shall receive less interest „and have to pay interest on what we have borrowed. We also can draw on our stock of money in hand, but that means send- ing our gold abroad, and if we do' too much of that our great world-wide banking business will be in danger, because a pertain store of gold is necessary as its foundation. The right thing for us to do is to spend less ourselves, because if we do that we either use less goods that come from abroad, and so we have less to pay to other countries, or else* we use , less goods that are made at home, and so we have more goods to send abroad to pay for the goods that we have to buy from other coun- tries.</p>
        <p>For instance, if we as individuals buy less food and drink and tobacco and fewer motor cars and clothes from abroad, then the shells and rifles and harness and horses and motor lorries that ,the Government has to buy from other countries for our Army and those of the Allies, take the place of the purchases that we have done Withotit, and sir) do not make the country pooier. V we use less of the things that we make at home, then ,there is more left to be sent abroad to' pay for the things that we have to buy abroad.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <p>our War Loan Stock at the market price</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>FOREIGN PURCHASES.</head>
        <p>FOREIGN PURCHASES.</p>
        <p>During tho first six months of this year we have bought £429,000,000 of goods from other countries and only sold them .235,000,000 worth; that means to say that each day we have been buying from them just over £1,000,000 worth</p>
        <p>of goods more than they have bought from us. We have to set against this difference the earnings of our merchant ships, the interest on our money that has been invested abroad, and banking and other services that we render to foreigners. In time of peace the difference between the la.rge-'amount. of. goods- that we buy abroad and those that we sell is fully met by these earnings and services which are some- times called our "invisible exports." But these services have probably been reduced since the war began, and if we do not buy less from abroad a big hole will be made in the great wealth with which we began the war.</p>
        <p>The only way to stop this ruinous process is to buy less abroad and to sell more. This we can do only by using less ourselves, that is, by saving, and by making for ourselves more of the things that we are obliged to have. For instance, if we all travel by train and omni- bus and tram as little as possible, we set free the labour of those who have to run the trains, &amp;c., enabling them either to go into the Army or to go as munition workers or to help in the fields to grow the food that we need to live on. Thus less food has to come from abroad. We also save the coals and petrol that we needed for the trains and omnibuses, and so coal is cheaper for industry and more can be sent abroad, and less petrol has to be brought from ,abroad. Trams are run by electric power which is chiefly pro- duced by coal, and so we save coal by using them less, as we do also by burning as little electric light and gas as possible. If we have any ground, every foot that we can use for - growing or raising ,food in the form of vegetables, wheat, chickens, rabbits, beef and mutton, &amp;c., is a gain to ourselves and the country.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>EVERYBODY'S BUSINESS.</head>
        <p>EVERYBODY'S BUSINESS.</p>
        <p>What we have to do is to carry out, by individual saving and self-denial, a revolution in our national habits. Some people will suffer by it, but they would suffer more if we were beaten in the war, or if we ended the war an impoverished people and a debtor country. Every consideration must be given to those who suffer, but the nation's existence is at stake and comes first. This is a matter in which everyone can help. If we all save as much as we can, and grow and make as much as we can for our- selves, the collective effect on the nation's financial position will be enormous. We must also do all that we can to check unnecessary spending Whenever we can. Some local bodies are Still indulging in unnecessary building, street improvement or lighting, and other forms of municipal activity which, at this crisis, amount to extravagance. With the ob- ject of stoppinc, this form of Waste, the Local Government Board are refusing to sanction borrowing by local authorities, but where the works are underivsken without recourse to a loan, the Government has no effective control. The ratepayers are the real controlling force, and where the local authority shows signs of extravagance the ratepayers should organise to resist it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>FOOD ECONOMY.</head>
        <p>FOOD ECONOMY.</p>
        <p>Eat Stale Bread And Drink Less Alcohol.</p>
        <p>A booklet issued by the Parliamentary War Savings Committee urges the impor- tance of avoiding waste of food. A big point is the choice of economical food. We cull the appended hints from the pages of the book, which should be widely read : Cheese is one of the best and most useful substitutes for meat. Weight for weight it contains more of the particular kind of nourish- ment which meat supplies than meat supplies itself. Milk and skim milk also contain this form of nourishment in a high degree., Peas, beans and lentils also contain these properties, and after thorough cooking should be freely used where the amount of meat in the diet is reduced.</p>
        <p>• There are .two ways in which bread can be ec9nomised without any real stint (1) By using every crust and crumb of it for food, and throwing none of it away. (2) By only using bread which is at least 24 hours old.</p>
        <p>Fresh bread is not so easily digested as bread which is a day old. The latter is more satisfy- ing and less. of it needs to be eaten. Our patriotic ancestors, in the wars of 100 years ago, Wok advantage of this well-known fact, and were able to make a great saving in their store of wheat by giving up the use of fresh bread and eating only that which was at least 24 hours old. It was estimated that about onc- sixth part of the \supply was saved by this simple means. If this were done at the present day it might enable a ten-months' Supply of wheat to last one year. This would be an enormous gain in our powers of resistance to the enemy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>CUT DOWN THE DRINK BILL.</head>
        <p>CUT DOWN THE DRINK BILL.</p>
        <p>Immense quantities of food materials such as barley, wheat and maize are used in this country for the manufacture of beer and spirits. As beer and spirits are almost valueless as food, and can only be classed as luxuries pure and simple, all this grainNis lost for food purposes. If this grain were available for Sood, both for man and beast, the prices of bread and meat would be lowered. It has been estimated that the average expenditure on alcoholic drinks in this country amounts to something over 6s. 6d. per family per week.</p>
        <p>If every family in the land were to cut their drink bill down by, say, one-half and invest the saving on this one item in the War Loan, the amount would come to £80,000,000 per annum. The waste in lowered ability to work now re- sulting from the consumption of alcohol would be largely removed and the gain in national working capacity Would be even greater than is represented by this monetary gain. What we buy from abroad may be roughly divided into four classes :</p>
        <p>(1) Foodstuffs of first necessity, such as corn and meat. (2) Raw materials of industry, such as iron ore, wood, &amp;c., and articles of necessary consumption in the home. (3) Luxuries, such .as many kinds of fruits; which ' are. not 'home produce, wine; brandy, and tobacco, tea and coffee. (4)* Other luxuries, such as silk goods, motor cars, &amp;c.</p>
        <p>Under the first two headings such economies as are necessary"in meat) are desirable quite as much in the British grown or manufac- tured article as in the imported. As to tea, 28 million pounds might be saved by merely reverting to the standard of consump- tion of ten years ago:</p>
        <p>Although the food value of sugar is admittedly high, especially for children, it seems fairly eel.- fain that there is a great opportunity for economy here, and particularly for less eating of sweets, the most expensive form in which sugar can be taken. A reduction of one ounce a week per head in tobacco would mean a saving of over £2,250,000 in the year. The substitution of margarine for butter from abroad would involve a saving of about £13,400,000 a year, or nearly 6s. per head. • It is impossible to give anything like a complete list of articles, not being articles of . food, upon which saving on a considerable scale without loss of efficiency would be pos- sible. .All. that can be attempted is to give a few illustrative- examples. Motor cars, cycles, motor cycles, and parts thereof, were imported in 1913 to the value of over £8,000,000. motor cars • and parts thereof alone being valued .at nearly 71 millions. Motor spirit• was imported in 1913 to the amount of 100,858,000 gallons, valued at ..t3,803,000. The quantity used for motor omni- buses and other heavy traffic cannot, of course, be distinguished from the quantity used by pleasum cars, but the amount so .used represents, an important possible saving. Silk 'manufacturers again offer a great oppor- tunity for economy. Omitting what is needed for the silk industry of this country, the total importation of manufactured silk goods in 1913 was valued at nearly Hi million pounds. Ornamental feathers were imported to the ' value of 31 million pounds; ostrich feathers (21 million pounds). came almost exclusively from South Africa.</p>
        <p>Skins and furs (not leather), which include seal skins and all ornamental furs, were im- ported to the value of over six million pounds.</p>
        <p>The charity that begins at home is generally too weak to travel.</p>
        <p>CYCLING AND MOTORING.</p>
        <p>Some Splendid Scenery In The</p>
        <p>Emerald Isle.</p>
        <p>0(1</p>
        <p>The renewal of the chains was not on account of their failure, to work well, but purely as precautionary measure. The reliability of tlip. chain drive has been proved, but it must be of first quality, fitted to a nicety, and have atten- tion paid to. it at frequent intervals.</p>
        <p>, q .1 PI • tr. .1.11.. # -1 . ......1 a kik r ' 1 . I = 1 i .•</p>
