      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>HOW TO SEE IT.</head>
        <p>HOW TO SEE IT.</p>
        <p>:Queries and correspondence should be addressed to "The Hub," cio The Editor. Replies can- not be sent by post and all queries will be answered in our columns.)</p>
        <p>The ride from Belfast to Portrush, via the coast, is one of the finest in all Ireland, either for the cyclist or the motorist. The views along the coast are superb, the surface on the whole is good, and the gradients are not too severe. Some of the hills require care in descending, and a careful examination of one's brakes should be made before starting. Belfast—the leading business town in Ireland—contains many interesting features, and some time may even be spent in one's making oneself acquainted with the chief buildings and parks. The town is a comparatively modern one, however. Leav- ing Belfast by the Royal Avenue, keep straight on along the shore road between the Cave Hill and Belfast Lough to Greencastle, Whitehouse • (a village devoted to the cotton trade), White- abbey (where there are some rather good ruins), and Carrickfergus—so-called from the tradition that a King 'Fergus was drowned there about B.C. 300. There is a good castle, built in 1178, and still used as a barracks. At Whitehead some very fine views of the lough can be obtained. Larne, a few 'miles further On, is a pretty situ- ated town, best known as the landing place of the Staniaer boats. Near by are the remains of Olderfleet Castle, built in the 13th century, and at which Edward Bruce landed in 1315. Frotil Larne the read is magnificent, being' one- , continual succession of superb sea views. At one part the road passes through a tunnel made by blasting the cliffs. At Ballygally Head there are some ruins of Carmcastle, which tradition connects with O'Halloran, a rebel chief. Gion- arm is soon reached—a most delightful village, with a castle close by. The road round Garron Point is very fine, the cliffs falling precipitously to the sea. At Red Bay the views are, perhaps, the finest of any On the whole trip, while Water- foot, a village a little farther on,, is situated on the north side of the Vale of Glenariff, the grandest of all the Antrim glens. The road then passes under the ruins of Red Bay Castle, an old stronghold of the MacDonnells, and so to Cushendall, Ballyvog, Ballycastle, Ballintoy, and Bushmills, a "name familiar to whisky drinkers the world over., Dunluce Castle, a few miles farther on, is an interesting ruin, though there is not a great deal standing. Portrush is the chief watering-place in the North of Ire- land. It is well situated, but its chief attrac- tion is its close proximity to the Giants Cause- 'way. The run from Belfast to Portrush is 871 miles, and it is safe to say that never was more superb scenery, or more interesting historical associations met with than in this short tour. The run to Belfast can be made -via the direct road, which is only 581 miles.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>SPINDLE LAMPS.</head>
        <p>SPINDLE LAMPS.</p>
        <p>The regulations as regards rear lamps do not state any exact position for their attachment to the machine. A number of riders, the great majority in fact, fix the lamp to the forks. Such an arrangement .has its drawbacks. - The lamp is apt to slip down, or the fork-may be damaged if the machine is dropped on to the lamp side.: One would hardly have thought this possible, but it is a fact all the same. The best arrange- ment I have used is dne which allows the lamp to be fixed on the spindle. As far as I can see, there are no disadvantages by attaching the lamp in this position. Spindle brackets can be obtained at most shops, and they are very easily fixed. This is another of the small things that 'matter. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>FREAK CYCLE.</head>
        <p>A FREAK CYCLE.</p>
        <p>A short time ago I saw an illustration of a freak cycle, which was brought out in 1896- The driving wheel was fitted centrally on the crank axle, and the tyre on the back wheel was specially made with a deep groove right round the tread. The two were joined by a band •of catgut. One who tested this novel machine, writing in a contemporary, says: "It was neces- sary to get slowly off the mark and (rather speed gradually. Up, to eight or nine. miles an hour there was nothing to complain of in the bicycle's behaviour—indeed, it ran up to this rate with a peculiar sense of stability and economy of labour. A higher speed, however, was next to impossible on account of the band slipping. Strangs to say, this fault was not in evidence in climbing a hill, and ,the machine was easier than anything I have tried for hill climbing. Several well-known Birmingham racing men tested its merits on the track, but found it im- possible to develop and sustain a racing pace upon it, whilst it made sprinting out of the question." STRETCH IN MOTOR CYCLE CHAINS.</p>
        <p>I have never been able to ascertain how many miles A. chain on a motor cycle is .supposed to last. There is no doubt, however, that if a goqd make is fitted to the machine, .the life of the chain is a- long one. I heard particulars of a chain- the other day that had covered. over 5,000 miles, and the' stretch only amounted to 0.375 of an inch. Another rider who has run about 50,000 miles has had three sorts Of chains fitted.</p>
        <p>"PREPARE FO It THE END."</p>
        <p>The Penalty That Will Have To Be</p>
        <p>Paid For The War.</p>
        <p>Speaking at aqarge open-air meeting at Brad- ford this week, Mr. Philip Snowden, M.P., said he saw a very real danger for the future in- dustrial condition of the people in the present industrial Prosperity. It was difficult for many working people to understand the illusory and fictitious character of this prosperity', and the idea might gain acceptance that the industrial classes were never going to suffer as a consequence of the unparalleled waste which was arising from the war.</p>
        <p>What would be the condition of things the army returned to industry and the Lisal.,ll,ll;e''; works were reduced? On the one how f ':fir would be competition between emploYer'„ia°t the recovery of lost markets and the conso4"--(1 demand for 'cheap labour, and on the other 116710 a terrific struggle on the part of lahellroas secure adequate wages. In addition, there the increased increased cost of living. Ile saw flOd pect of prices going down.. He foreshau", as the outcome of the 41 per cent. War Loa- increase iu rents and in municipal rates. iking In view of all this he appealed to iff.:r. the- men to do what was possible to prePare 'l";.t tie end of the war. Especially vigilance Mu arkl , exorcised to preserve 'the weapons of offenc!A ; defence from being imperilled and destro3'F"' the eniployers and the governing elioises-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>AN INNOVATION WANTED.</head>
        <p>AN INNOVATION WANTED.</p>
        <p>Rotor cycle makers are usually willing to act up points from motor car manufacturers, an here is a practice they would do well to, follow - Nowadays motor cars are turned out fulli equipped with all the necessary accessories. This, however, is not the case with motor cycles. Car buyers appreciate this practice for two reasons- In the first place, it saves them the trouble of purchasing, and having fixed Buell things as lamps, tools,. horns, &amp;c. In the second plaCie accessories are generally standardised, whereas cars are not. This necessitates in some cars an alteration being made in the brackets, &amp;c. A maker knows the exact type of lamp or' horn which goes best with his own car. If motor cycle manufacturers would follow suit /*- would be a great boon to the purchaser.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>RENOVATION OF BODY WORK.</head>
        <p>THE RENOVATION OF BODY WORK.</p>
        <p>Practically everyone is in the saMe position to-day, namely, that they find it necessary .to economise. No matter how much the bodY work of a car may require touching up, the aver- age man does not feel like paying for the work - Naturally; it stands to reason that the ordinary owner cannot Produce anything like the results which the professional can, but all the same, the appearance of a car can be considerably bet- tered by the amateur. Paint and varnish should be given the go-by, and enamel used in its stead. The surface of the body should be robbed down with fine glass paper, particular care being taken to get into the corners and less accesSibie but visible parts. Suitable enamel can be 5€ cured in most places.. A good brush is a neces- sity. The cheap, ones are only a source of an- noyance, for bristles come out and adhere to the enamel. The body will take several days to. dry, an4'.t should be kept in ja dust-proof shed as fur 04.•-possible. Three ortfoor coAts he given. Between each Coat ,the surface should be rubbed down with fine glass paper. Much can be done by the average owner if the abaci' directions are followed</p>
        <p>motoring contemporary has made the /A lowing suggestion as regards the disposal 01 those motor cars that will be returned to thio country at the conclusion of the war: "A queo'' tion of considerable interest, and importance which we have not seen ,diScussed up to the present is—What will be done with cars coining back from the Front? It may be that a large, proportion of these vehicles will be release from service in quite a saleable condition, ana we understand that several large dealers has'. already made overtures to the Government with._ a view to buying up the old cars. Should these, dealers. gain their end we shall, we suppose' have a number of auction sales at which thel cars will he put up for the highest offer. Wool" it /not be a better plan for each of the marl.'" facturers to be asked to take back all surpille cars of their respective makes and to offer then; to the public through their recognised f.gents• It is quite conceivable that there will ,be considerable number of cars which can thus le offered for sale through the ordinary selling channels, and the handling of such vehicle would help, to bridge over the interval of non- production." I hope the proposal will " realised</p>
