        <p>eiliincillor H. ROBEUTS, in moving that the 112.4tter be deferred for further considera- said / that it was extremely re- to him at the present time to "4 -€Ely into, any dismission which was. unneces- -44r7, and which might engender some little un- Jgeasa.at feeling. He felt that the time was Itttogether inappropriate for any serious differ-' of Opinion, and deeply regretted that the *textures Made by him were not met in another iY. lie sincerely desired to avoid any refer- "lWO. to unpleasantnesses of the past. He was 110.te prepared to have it wiped out altogether. ,4141 see if they could not arrive ,Ett some lolitually satisfactory arrangement, because the ebiect both committees aimed at was the same: t..1.2n health of the children of the town. The ttePltti ComMittee acted as they did on a zuajority of one. That committee had claimed telleateclly in the Council , that it .was :.the alltherity to whom was entrusted this particu- branch of the public service. It never was intended that the administration of the provi- sions of the Act of 1907 should be carried through by any other body than the Education Authority of the town in which the Act oper- ated. The Education Authority of Warring- ton. was that Council—not sitting as a Colima. bat as an Education Committee.</p>
