        <p>The Board of Education, who promoted the kalluded to, recommended that the *edical I.:lC,'bi4.!c of Health .should advise the Education thoittt.re• There was not a. suggestlin " that 611tra til Committee of any town shbuld be alinfEsit:l. With the responsibility for. the aft; were j`lon of the Act. Out Of 37 boroughs. 2.3 est.croistering the Act in the way he siig- , and there were only three acting in the vrb IvaT as they were at Warrington. The 111.84).„,,t° o,f the work was done by the Medical .rerr'„„44'on Committee, and it was not ever to the Health Committee. Either th;: xedical Inspection Committee was doing vork satisfactoiily, or the Health Com- Arill3t4"; which claimed to be ultimately respon- -4„. Por the work, was not doing its duty in that. the Medical Inspection Committee Ccs itS ,ork pro Perly. Apparently, the lk.ledie.al. f tjeetion Committee was' doing its work sati- actorily for the Board of Education were them the highest passible plant. He ;lieu to see how anyone could suggest that it 4,43. in the interests of the health of the town %at . the tools which the •Mectic'al eo • . ' .11 Oututtee used they were incapable of select- 4, for themselves.</p>
        <p>Things Better. Left Unsaid. • To his mind, it was the mast extraordinary raition that had occurred in that house since " had been a member of the Council. He had 4111-ne his best to effect a compromise, hand- kip Tr...l •-3' supported by a suggestion put forward by Worship the Mayor. If they did work, and 71d it properly, and did not report at all to the Ilealth Committee, then they ought to have ~,46_131e voice in the eleetion of the officers for the. 'lrrYing out of the work. He was absolutely ,Itonished when he found that the Health 1: Ir.- • cimunittee had refused to meet his committee 'll an way. It was extremely unfortunate if a fact that the members of the Health ..t°ll4mittee had been affected in their attitude 14""aYds the matter by a certain remark made IZta member of the Medical Inspection Com- vii'tee, who had given the very best of his sert to the work. They were not there to tl,:e Personal ends. The question they had to 15,4'.'sider was: how best to look after the inter- C9i of-the town. Even he had said in that eil Chamber things he wished he had never All that he suggested was that three "kiptrs of the Medical Inspection Committee s 'd join with the Health Committee in the Iction of the officers.</p>
