      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>_ COMPLIMENTARY CONTRADICTION.</head>
        <p>_ COMPLIMENTARY CONTRADICTION.</p>
        <p>▪ -a.ClllOr YEMBERTON, 111 s-upporting the Mill- ornplimented Councillor Roberts on his tfa speech which he thought no member • lat Council could have bettered with .so 1241: an amount of fact. What Councillor Its had put forward was an absolute mi- -"le Warrington Town Council was the mto• ation authority of 'Warrington, and they ciiieated a certain amount of their work to the thineation Committee. Conncillor Roberts said thejs had been going on splendidly: . -0•011, ' villY alter anything?</p>
        <p>WANTED—A COMPROMISE. The pi tor,„ Emil' MAYOR, said he thought in tho the public health, the Medicalln- Committee should gither choose the,</p>
        <p>officers or else help to do so. He felt that ecrae compromise should be made.</p>
        <p>FACTS AND FIGURES.</p>
        <p>Alderman JAMES EVANS (chairman of the Health Committee) said he dict not think any member of the Council understood the condi- tion of child life in Warrington better than Councillor Roberts, but he must know that con- siderable work, had to be done 'by the Health ,Authority, of the town. It was work which had been. enjoined upon' them to do. Considerably more than half the work had to be done among children in the schools of the town. Last year</p>
        <p>14,206 cases were attended to, apart altogether</p>
        <p>from the school clinic. The Health -Com- mittee claimed that they were doing as much in .the interests of children as the school _clinic. It was not the duty of the, Health Committee, to modify or mollify the "unfortunate expzcs- sion M to which Couneillor Roberts had alludcd. The responsibility rested with the perAosi made "it. Ile did not think the 'unfortunate expression" had blinded his judgment or that of any member of the committee, but to he rubbed the wrong way up ruffled some people. It would, in his opinion, be a mistake for the Town Council' to alter the position which the Health Committee had taken up, however de- sirable it might be that. there be swne association or •connection between the two com- mittees, which was more tangible and more agreeable than it was at the present moment: The course which had been taken that night, however, was not the way to bring about that state of things. It was up to the School In- spection Committee to consider: a reasonable line, to be adopted without saying hard things, and the Health Committee should consider the same aspect of the question. If there was a dual authority to select officers and work under one administrative head he was sure it would cause greater friction than that which existed to-day. The amendment was lost.,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>INSURANCE AND THE WAR.</head>
        <p>INSURANCE AND THE WAR.</p>
        <p>Warrington Burial Society's</p>
        <p>Good Year's Work.</p>
        <p>The members of the Warrington Burial Society assembled at the British Workman</p>
        <p>Public-house, Horsemarket-street, on Monday, August 9th, to celebrate the ,forty-fourth anni- versary of the establishment of the Society. The attendance was exceptionally good. The chair was occupied by the President, Mr. James T. Barton, who was supported by the Vice-president, Mr. John J. Baskerrille; the treasurer, Mr. James Parr; the trustees, Messrs. William Eckersley, Joseph Adams, James Gresty; the auditors, Mr. John Haley and Mr. D. Harris; and the general secretary, Mr. Lawrence Weir.</p>
        <p>The Chairman at the outset made a few remarks on the past. year's working of the Society and on insurance generally. His hearers would know that the past twelve months had been a very critical time for in- surance and collecting societies financially, and for new business.</p>
        <p>During the first months of the war people were panic-stricken, foodstuffs were purchased in large quantities, and every penny that could be diverted from other sources was used for this purpose. That had a- serious effect on in.sur- arice, and business was very difficult to main- tain. New business was at a standstill. Decreases followed,: but he was,pleased to say that the Warrington Burial Society ,had made increases all round. It spoke well for the whole of the staff, both management and collectors, that they had piloted a concern through such ,difficult times. -They, undoubtedly merited the SOciety's warmest- appreciation.. The committee had not been behind in recognising patriotically any members joining His Majesty's Services. The Society had paid their premium. The committee proposed to increase the benefits after a certain 'number of years membership had been served. All pro- gressive societies periodically, as their funds increased, allowed the members to participate in the profits. It was one of the best methods to retain present members, and certainly an inducement to offer to prospective ones. In conclusion, the President thanked the whole of the staff for their generous and loyal 'slipnort during his year of office. The General Secretary (Mr. Lawrence Weir), in presenting the annual balance sheet, showed that the Society's funds now amounted to £5,458 2s. 10-A., as compared with .55,241 &amp;s. nd. the previous year, a gain of £216 1.45. The numerical strength was 1,560, compared with 1,555 last year. The report further showed that the Society had paid away in death claims £ll4, against £lll ss. the previous year, and they had invested in the Warrington Corpora- tion and on mortgage .:5,215. The auditors re- ported everything satisfactory, and said it gave them pleasure to note the steady progress the Society had made through another year, a year unprecedented in difficulties and exceptional liabilities. The . permanent offidials were thanked for their courteous assisanee.. The President, Mr. James T. Barton. and the Viee-President, Mr. John T. Baskerville, were duly elected for another year. Mr: David. Harris was elected auditor for 'the next two ,years.</p>
        <p>The principal remaining item on the agenda was the alteration of the benefit table upon the last • stage. Members who have been entered upon the books of the society for over ten years and under 25 years nre to have their benefits in- creased to £ll, and those who have made 25 years' continuous payments to the society are to be increased to ..tll 10s. The following mem- bers took part in the discussion: Messrs-. John Haley. William Massey, William Eckersley, J. Dell, James T. Barton, William Hall, John J. Baskerville and others.</p>
        <p>A vote of thanks WIIR Recorded to the Presi- dent (Mr. James T. Barton) and the Vice-Presi- dent (Mr. Johzi J. Baskerrille) for their year's services.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>PRISONERS OF WAR.</head>
        <p>PRISONERS OF WAR.</p>
        <p>Uralitude For Parcels Sent From Warrington.</p>
        <p>Funds are urgently needed to enable parcels Of food to be forwarded to the prisoners of war in Germany. The following extracts from cards received from prisoners show the need for this fund to be kept in view : "I am a prisoner of war at gennelager, and as I am an orphan I have been advised by one of my comrades to write to you. I hope you will do your best for. nie. I suppose. you can guess what it is like here. I, should be very grateful if you will forward me some eatables, such as breed, cocoas, and tinned foods. I am sure your kindness shall never be forgotten. I will now elOse, 'hoping •to hear from you soon.—l remain;. yours, sincerely, CORPORAL W. TUCKER.m</p>
        <p>The two extracts below show how grateful the men are for any small service done 'for them:— " Dear sir,-4 received your most welcome parcel posted on the 24th June. Everything was in good condition, and it was, we/I packed and thought out, and they are just the things I require. I am very thankful to you for Your kindness to me while I am •a. prisoner. May God bless you and yours, and give You much happiness. Again thanking' you, I re- main, yours sincerely, CORPORAL J. W: KELLY." "Dear sir,-4 received your first parcel on •; the,lBo/15. It had on it 'parcel D,' and I must thank you very. much \ for, it, as 1 have no friends to send me anything. It was a sur- prise to 'receive the parcel, as it was the first - I ,have had from 'anybody, .and you may be sure I appreciate it very remain,</p>
        <p>• yours very sincerely, PRIVATB A. FORD 7 Three shillings per week will enable an un- fortunate captive to feel that he is not forgotten at home. It is the duty of, those who are unable to fight to do their "bit," and this is one of the many ways in which it can be done. All in- formation will gladly be given-at the Parr /Tall. The above are selected from about thirty communications received,from prisoners of war on Thursday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>TINS FOR MEAT AND FISII.</head>
        <p>TINS FOR MEAT AND FISII.</p>
        <p>The following announcement was ‘issued from the Press Thireau on Wedisday evening With reference to the communication issued on July 20th, the Army Council notify that from this date the restrictions on the use of tins for sending meat, fish and other CQIIIIIIO- - to prisoners of war in Germany are with- drawn.</p>
      </div>
