      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>4.• +4. CHAT and GOSSIP.</head>
        <p>4.• +4. CHAT and GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>By MARJORIE.</p>
        <p>Spirit of the Women of France. A short time ago, the elder men of the French Army were granted short leave, in order that theY might visit their families. Before their return a most touching appeal to the women of France was made through the medium of a leading feminist paper. The wives, mothers and other lady. relatives of the men were exhorted to prove themselves worthy of the soldiere, and of their country, by not giving way to weak tears and regrets, but to send the men back strengthened and heartened. It was pointed out that in this crisis the responsibility of the women was overwhelming, and that their strength or weakness must prove a decisive factor in the struggle. To shake the patriotism of the men would be criminal—for they were fighting not only for their country, but for all the world holds dear, for truth, and for justice. Love should inspire, not weaken, and the sacrifice entailed must be regarded as an honour and a joy. Such is the noble spirit of the women of France. A Problem of the Hour.</p>
        <p>1 Problems dealing with economy in every r department of the household, in dress, amuse- ments, travel and so on, are being seriously dis- enssed wherever women congregate, for with all classes retrenchment in some form .has become a necessity. The wealthy portion of the com- munity is conscientiously endeavouring to curtail expenses. Schools for housecraft are springing up everywhere. Mayfair housewives are endeavouring to solve the problems with as much patience and zeal as the wives of the middle classes. Duchesses give "housekeeping teas" where visitors are invited to offer sugges- tions arising from their personal experiences. These gatherings are in part educational, since a .lecturer attends to explain the quantities required for the dishes 'that form the daily repasts. Such a course of study is imperative as a preliminary to approaching the chef, housekeeper and cook, on the question of reform. Otherwise the aristocratic housewife might make deplorable mistakes and• sustain a serious defeat at the beginning of her cam- paign.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>HOME NOTES.</head>
        <p>HOME NOTES.</p>
        <p>Profitable Recipes and Useful Suggestions.</p>
        <p>Cheese Omelet.</p>
        <p>The yolks and whites of two eggs, loz. ,of grated Parmesan cheese, loz. of butter, season- ing. Separate the yolks and the whites of the eggs. Whisk up the yolks with a fork, and season. Beat up the whites very stiffly, and add lightly to the yolks. Put the loz. of butter into a very clean frying or omelet pan, and when hot pour -in the mixture and turn it about till the middle is cooked. Then sprinkle the top with grated cheese, and put the pan under the.griller to set the top. To take out omelet, fold it over towards the handle of the pan and then gently slip it out.—" Church Fatally Newspaper"</p>
        <p>A Summer Soup. One and a half pints of tender peas measured in the pods, lb. of young spinach leaves, one sprig- of mint, one quart of good white stock, 1-oz. of butter, I,loz. of flour, two tablespoonfuls of thick cream, seasoning, one lump of sugar. Shell the peas and wash the pods carefully, also the spinach and mint, and put all, including the pods, into a saucepan containing just enough water to boil them. Boil gently till quite tender. Remove about half-teacupful of the peas, and rub the re- mainder, including the pods, through a fine wire sieve. Save about half-pint of the water. Skim and strain the stock, and add the half- pint of vegetable boilings to it, also the puree. Melt the butter in a clean saucepan, stir in flour, and add the soup gradually. Bring to the boil, season, and put in one lump of sugar, and add the cream just before serving. Put the whole peas that have been saved into the tureen, and pour over the boiling soup. Serve with fried bread.</p>
        <p>Value of Cereals. Cereals, whose immense food value is often entirely overlooked, should at the moment form practically our staple food, for there are many ways and methods of introducing them into the daily menu in an appetising and attractive manner, besides that of the homely milk pud- -ding. Cereals contain 'from 5 to 14 per cent, of nitrogenous matters, chiefly in the form of gluten,,while large quantities of starch are found in all grains. Owing to the, peculiarities of gluten for bread-making, the only cereals suit- able for this purpose are wheat and rye; but all grains can be used for the making of cakes, porridge, puddings, and a variety of soups. The whole grain is the most nourishing. Often to get a fine white appearance, not only the gluten but much of the valuable salts found in all cereals is removed; so that in choosing dishes for the sake of nourishment the whole rather than the ground should be used, although the ground grain usually take S less time to cook. Oats rank with wheat in possessing the largest amount Of proteids. They are also rich in fats</p>
        <p>and salts. Rice, which is perhaps the most commonly used, is the poorest of all the cereals, as it contains practically nothing but starch, and requires albumen and fat in some form to be added to make it into a really good food. Barley, which is not nearly enough used, is almost as nutritive as wheat, and should be made much more of as a food than it is. Cereals and pulse can be varied and made into many nice, inexpensive dishes by the addition of such things *a eggs, fish, cheese, milk, &amp;c., and the cost will be very moderate.</p>
        <p>bressing and Serving. The housekeeper should make a study of the ways and means of dressing . and serving cereal food, and she will be surprised how greatly the introduction of them, if nicely flavoured and carefully cooked, will reduce the heavy expenditure of the housekeeping, and also make them much appreciated, especially for light or hot weather supper or luncheon dishes. The flavour of meat can still be re- tained if a free use of good stock made from bones is used in conjunction with such things as beans, lentils, &amp;c.—" Church Family Paper."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>• 4. • 4. 4. • 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. . • 4. 4. • 4.4.</head>
        <p>• 4. • 4. 4. • 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. . • 4. 4. • 4.4.</p>
        <p>4 , • Children s Corner.:?'. . ...</p>
