        <p>3 4. .:. .:. .:. .:. 4,.. .:. ..... ...... 44. 4. .:. 4. 4. .:. • .:. + 04. 3</p>
        <p>CHILDREN Or Au AGEE are cordially invited to join this Corner if they read it regularly. Application for membership, stating name, address and age, should be made to "UNCLE 'NED; Examiner' Office, Bewsey- street, Warrington." Every girl or boy, whose name is in the Big Book, is entitled to enter the competitions held every week, to the winners of which useful book-prize are sent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>HERE WE ARE AGAIN:</head>
        <p>HERE WE ARE AGAIN:</p>
        <p>MY DEAR CRILbREN,—</p>
        <p>How. ozio you do? Very well? That's all right; I'm, pleased to hear it! Me? , Oh, well, of course, I am very well, thank you; but at the same time I am very glad to be with you all again, now that my holidays are over. First of all, I want to correct that little slip of Uncle Ilubert's about the numbers--which, you will remember, he made,a week or two ago through my going away in a hurry, and nothaving time to show him how to find your numbers 'for you. So here goes: . • •</p>
        <p>LILIAN .HIMSWORTH, of 15, Pitzherbert-street, is 352. HORACE HIIISWORTII, of 15, Fitzherbert-street, is 351. MAURICE ARCHER, of 11, Heath-street, is 357. TinitoLD JONES, Of 2, Godfrey-street, is 437.</p>
        <p>All the other numbers given were correct. One of our new members, John Selan, of 48, Oldham-street, Latchford (who, you remember, carried off the prize- last week), wants to know his number. It is 464, John. Thank you for the very good essay you have sent this week also. Keep on! I have real pleasure in welcoming very heartily another new member to our midst— WILLIAM LEICESTER, aged 12, of 30, Priestley- street. William is a friend of this week's prize- winner, who has introduced him to me. Good luck to you, William! Your number is 465, and please don't forget always to put it by your name on any letter you Write me—Mid I hope there will be a lot of them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>TRAIN JOURNEY.</head>
        <p>A TRAIN JOURNEY.</p>
        <p>And now for that little essay you had to write about "A Train Journey "—what a like inter-</p>
        <p>esting subject! That was a good choice of Uncle Hubert's, and I award the prize to 446 Nephew Sam Backhouse, whose winning letter runs as follows : "6, Milner-street,-V.arrington, August Bth.</p>
        <p>1915. - - "Dear Uncle,—l have decided to write about a train journey to Llangollen. I went with our school trip on Ascension Day. After going to church we assembled at Bank Quay Station. On our way we went through Chester; here we changed our train for a motor train. We also passed the Penrhyn slate quarries. We were the only persons in the train, because we had engaged it. When we were nearing our destination we could see a range of hills. —I remain, your loving nephew, Sam Back- house." Capital, Sam! You won a prize only eight weeks ago, but you deserve another, and it is a pleasure for me to give it you this week. William Backhouse, Sam's brother, writes: "Dear Uncle,—l have decided to write _ about my journey to Southport with my school. We went to the Central Station and our headmaster got us our tickets. I was in the same carriage as my headmaster. On our way I looked out through the window. It was very bare outside the carriage window; there were a few trees here and there. I liked the journey very much. As you' do not like long letters I will now draw to a close.—From your loving nephew, William BackhOuse." A good effort, William, and, as you say, not to long to make me tired. Please in future give your age, number, and school, whenever you send me a letter, and I shall be even more glad. I want all my nephews and nieces to do this: Never write to me without giving your age, number and school. It is much better for you all that I should not forget these particulars when judging your letter's, because then I make a fairer choice, of course. A novel idea is contained in Niece Marian Itarton's letter, and I am happy to think that there is some originality (what a big word! ask your fathers or mothers what it means) amongst my family : "42, Wakefield-street, Warrington, Aug. 9th, • 1915. "Dear Uncle Ned,—l think a train journey is very pleasant if accidents do not happen. It is very nice to see the cattle grazing in the beautiful green, fields. There is always plenty of nice scenery, which is very inter- esting. A great many children like to go a train journey.—l remain, your loving niece, 444 MARIAN BARTON." " P.S.--Very sorry I have neglected writing to you.".</p>
        <p>A JOURNEY BY NIGHT. Nephew John Solan also sends a neat note : " 48, Oldham-street, Latchford, Warrington, August Bth, 1915. " Dear Uncle Ned,—The train journey whieh I am going to, describe is one at night in a third-class carriage, which is not always en- joyable. The guard blows his whistle, and the train started. Passengers of all sexes huddle together and try to make themselves as comfortable as possible. The stillness of the night is broken only by the loud breath of a sleeping man, or by the cry of a child. Then suddenly there comes a jar and a jolt, and the train journey is ended.—l am, yours sincerely, John Solan."</p>
        <p>Well done, John! By the way, you had a somewhat novel experience. I have done quite a lot of railway travelling , by night,' but usually the trains are !not bt all- crowdect. Which line did you go by? I promise not to tell, and get the railway into disfavour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>T. 21-. 3 s 4r, -gtg : 4 -1/4 tirkr– ; . .</head>
        <p>T. 21-. 3 s 4r, -gtg : 4 -1/4 tirkr- - ; . .</p>
        <p>Thc.. New Economy.</p>
        <p>At these teas, the old-fashioned storeroom and</p>
        <p>linen cupboard have been the subject of much discussion, with the result that both have been condemned as obsolete and wasteful. In the country the former may still be tolerated for a liniited Supply of goods,.not easily obtainable there; but in towns, where stores and shops deliver fresh provisions and groceries daily, the storeroom is an anachronism. . It also gives much unnecessary work and its place may very well be taken by a well ventilated, good-sized larder. Piles of unused napery are as unneces- sary as stacks of stale and superfluous stores, and make demand upon the time of mistresses and maids, that eould be put to a better use. .rhe new economy applies the same verdict to the personal linen and the overstocked ward- robe, and in fact hoarded stores of any kind. The basis of true economy is a sufficiency with- out superfluity. .</p>
        <p>The Triumph of Velvet. In the coming autumn velvet will occupy a a position of great importance, and black velvet will reign supreme. It has-been largely used in millinery' throughout the year, and now gives the finishing touch to summer dresses of all kinds. Some of the most exclusive dress- makers are exploiting the black velvet sash with the greatest success, and are besides using this always beautiful material for garnitures of every description. The newest boleros and coatees for wearing with afternoon or other sraart frocks, even when of the filmiest kind, are of black velvet. The lining of these hand- some little garments is of thin silk, which if coloured, repeats the leading tone or shade of the .dress they accompany. Even the tea gown has fallen under the spell of the black or coloured velvet coatee, which is sometimes of the bolero type, and sometimes has long basques reaching below the hips. Many these coatees display considerable originality in ign.</p>
        <p>The Taffetas Wrap. • Another equally popular material of the moment—and of the future—is taffetas. The soft, supple chiffon variety is a-most fascinating, fabric, and in common with velvet deserves its popularity. It is used this summer, more especially in black, for coats of many kinds, and is worn with dresses of all descriptions. Gener- ally speaking, taffetas coats resolve themselves</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>_ HERE AND THERE.</head>
        <p>_ HERE AND THERE.</p>
        <p>Most men are not as ugly as they look. Tell a woman she is dull and she is apt to disprove it by cutting you.</p>
        <p>The Reason For It "Mama," said little Elsie, "do men ever go to heaven?" "Why, of course, My dear. What makes you ask?" "Because I never see any pictures of angels with whiskers." " Well," said the mother, thoughtfully, "some men do go to heaven, but they get there by a close shave."</p>
        <p>The Bliss of Uncertainty. Margie was industriously sewing for, her doll, when she suddenly stopped work and turned to her mother.</p>
        <p>"Oh, Mamma, what do you thiiikr she ex- claimed. •</p>
        <p>"What is the trouble, dearie?" asked the mother.</p>
        <p>"I started to make my dolly a bonnet," ex- plained Margie, " and,l do believe it's going to come out an apron t"</p>
        <p>Not Far Wrong. "Suppose," said the lady teacher, " your mother sent you to the store to buy 31b. of lamb, 21b. of potatoes, lb. each of carrots and turnips, and 11b. of tomatoes—what would you have then?" Tim shook his head, but Mary, only a year older, raised an eager hand. " Well, Mary?" said the teacher, with a sorrowful glance at little Tim. "Stew!" said Mary, sweetly.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>... .0 4. 4. ++ 4. 4. 4** • 0 •</head>
        <p>... .0 4. 4. ++ 4. 4. 4** • 0 •</p>
        <p>into two types, the long and the short, and cer- tainly no more useful coat than the long wrap</p>
        <p>of black taffetas has ever- been designed. it serves many purposes, and is as suitable for evening wear as for giving the necessary warmth, on chilly days, to a summer afternoon toilette. The favourite shape has the half Magyar yoke, an attractive high collar, and is very full in the skirt. It reaches just below the knees. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>Frivolous Frocks.</head>
        <p>Frivolous Frocks.</p>
        <p>There will be much outdoor 'entertaining of convalescent soldiers, and others home for short leave,.. should the weather prove fine this month. There will, of Ceti/me- be no other festivities, worthy of the name, but in'VIPW of all these alfresco gatherings, many dress- makers have provided very charming frocks of the fully and frivolousib description, which are quite a contrast to the simple, not to say severe styles that have prevailed since the war began. Embroidered lawn and voile, or net, are chiefly used for the confection of these pretty dresses. A charming model was of ivory white spotted net, very much frilled and lavishly trimmed with Valenciennes lace. It was lined through- out with white chiffon. A novel bolero of pink and grey shot taffetas completed this pretty toilette. Rich ivory white poplin skirts go well with the lovely \embroidered blouses, trimmed with beautiful Valenciennes lace, and make handsome afternoon frocks.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>From Club to Provision Store.</head>
        <p>From Club to Provision Store.</p>
        <p>The National Food Fund as well as feeding nearly six thousand Belgians daily, is assisting the anti-luxury campaign. It has turned the fashionable Grosvenor Club into a provision store, for gifts in kind—meat, flour and groceries are received in large quantities from Canada, Australia, the Argentine, &amp;c. On the ground floor is a butcher's shop, and there are various other departments. The provisions are divided up into rations and weekly parcels, and delivered by women in motor cars in Lon- don, the suburbs, and outlying country dis- tricts. In addition quantities of literature on household thrift and management are being distributed. The Educational Committee ,in- eludes Lady Chance, Lady WolseleY, Lady St. Gyres, besides several food experts, ard its comprehensive scheme aims at the enlighten- ment of County Councils, schools 1::d educa- tional .bodies all over the country, as well as</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>HINTS ON HEALTH.</head>
        <p>HINTS ON HEALTH.</p>
        <p>Useful Information In Popular</p>
        <p>Language.</p>
        <p>riarty Hours. Just a word must be said in conClusion on the importance of early hours. Six o'clock is a good bedtime for little children, certainly until they are six years old, and from then until they are ten the hours should gradually lengthen out until eight o'clock. There would be far fewer cases of nerve strain in school days, of irritability and fretfulness, even of indiges- tion, if such wise counsels prevailed in practice as generally as they do.in theory I—" Queen." Fish Bones in the Throat.</p>
        <p>Prevention is the first aim, and should be fairly, easy. It is well not to feed children, at .any rate, with fish of the particularly bony order. And generally the accident would seem the consequence of undue haste or negli- gence. It scarcely ever occurs in adults who eat slowly and take pains properly to masti- cate their food. Should a bone really lodge in the throat, you may try to dislodge it by gulp- ing down large mouthfuls of bread and milk or rice pudding. Failing this, see the doctor. But it is important to remember that the sensation often persists—especially in nervous people—long after the cause has been re- moved. Repeated gargling with warm vinegar and water may be resorted,to by an adult. Bedtime.</p>
        <p>It should be unnecessary to remark that no baby should be rocked or " patted " to sleep; at the same time, old prejudices die hard, and many young mothers fall victims to a pretty idea, and start hushing off their little ones when bedtime comes. It is, however, a wrong begin- ning to make, for once the habit is formed it is exceedingly difficult to break, and many hours of fretful wakefulness for the baby and nerve- racking irritation for nurse or mother are being laid in store. The baby should be laid down warm and dry, with every care to each detail of its comfort, well fed, and shaded from any light, even from the first day, and so accustomed to go to sleep unheeded and unroeked. Sometimes it will be awakened suddenly—possibly by some noise or exterior influence, possibly for a cause which cannot be determined; do not immedi- ately take the child up, even if it is crying, but simply turn it over in its cot, soothing it with soft words as you do so,, and giving it a tea- spoonful' or so of cold water, if it appears hungry. This simple process, carried on in the dark, or in the same subdued light which ob- tained before, will in ninety-nine eases out of a hundred induce the little one to go straight off to sleep again.</p>
        <p>Of e-Our;e, a baby should be laid to sleep on its side, in alternate positions each night, and care should be taken that the little ear is laid flat and not crumpled. Oddly enough, people</p>
        <p>are far more careless than one would -tbin-lc sible about this matter, and undoubtedly many children owe protruding ears in later life to such carelessness in infancy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>NEXT WEEK: "A HAPPY HOLIDAY."</head>
        <p>NEXT WEEK: "A HAPPY HOLIDAY."</p>
        <p>I know that a good many of you are away just at present enjoying your holidays by coast and in country. Well, I hope you will get your papers just the same while you are away, for I know you are all very interested in our Corner. Indeed, I think you must all be getting your papers, for if you were to be with me up at this nice place where I write your letters, you would be quite surprised to see what great piles. of papers are sent away every week to all parts of the country—and even to, other countries, hundreds of miles away. So as you are reading the Corner just _as regularly don't forget to spare a moment in your pleasure-making to pen a line or so to poor old Uncle Ned, who, alas, has had his holi- days and can now only, follow yours with the same keen, interest as you followed his. Please write to me about "A Happy Holiday," and don't forget that your letter must be short, and that it must also say your age, number and the school you attend. -</p>
        <p>With' love from UNCLE NED.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>THOUGHT FOR THE WEEK.</head>
        <p>THOUGHT FOR THE WEEK.</p>
        <p>Never forget that an act of goodness is of itself an act of happiness. No reward coming after the event can compare with that which went with it.—Maurice Materlincks.</p>
        <p>"I shall pass this way but once: any good thing therefore that I can do, or any kindness that I can show,.. . let me do it now. Let me not defer it or neglect it, for I shall not pass this way again."—Fos.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>Only 1-, WOMAN Knows</head>
        <p>Only 1-, WOMAN Knows</p>
        <p>the price she pays to keep up the struggle. WARRINGTON</p>
        <p>Woman's Statement.</p>
        <p>On January Bth, /Ms Miss A. Bate of 35, Chorley-street, Warrington, said :—" Through _overwork I became subject to kidney complaint.' I had sharp, stabbing pains in the region of the kidneys; and they wore me out completely. The complaint affected my head, and I came ever so dizzy that I was afraid of falling. Thf kidney ' excretions were scalding and cloudy and full of dark heavy sediment. "I got no better until I took Dean's Backachl/4 Indney Pills. This was just the medicine required: Two, boxes completely cured me, strengthened my kidneys and cleared the water. That is nearly two years ago, and I. have never been bothered since. Doan's Pills, are a grand medicine, and it is a pleasure to, recommend them. (Signed) "A BATE."</p>
        <p>On November 21st, 1913—isminvr TWELVE MONTHS LATEn-3fiss Bate said :—" I have en- joyed very good health since I gave my testi- monial for Doan's Pills."</p>
        <p>If you don't feel well or look so well as you used to, just answer the following questions: Do your limbs, back and loins ache from morn till night? Have you dropsical swellings in ankles, hands, or under the eyes? Is there too frequent need of urinary relief? Do you always feel tired .and unfit for exertion?</p>
        <p>If you must answer "Yes" to any of these questions, you have ample reason to suspect your kidneys. Doan's Backache Kidney Pills heal and soothe and regulate weak kidneys and inflamed bladder, and so correct the cause of these and more advanced symptoms, such as Rheumatism, Dropsy, Stone, Gravel and Sciatica. _ Send for Free Book on "Moderation, Cheerfulness, and other Long Life Laws.'</p>
