      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>1N THE COURTS.</head>
        <p>1N THE COURTS.</p>
        <p>A PAWNBROKER'S SUSPICIONS.</p>
        <p>Charge Of Stealing From Dwelling-</p>
        <p>House In 80-stock-street.</p>
        <p>Margaret Sherwood, of 23, Bosto-ck-street, Warrington, was charged at the Borough PoLice Court on Monday with stealing fiom a dwelling-house, No. 25, Bostock-street, on August 7th, a blouse piece and a lady's dress skirt, value, in all, 305., the property of Sarah Verdon. The Mayor (Aldllrincat Peter Peacock) was in the chair, and .his colleagues were: Aldermen • Smethurst, Boltt.ri .and Picirpcint, and Messrs. W. Furness, T. Locker, G. Pilling and S. Wright. The. Chief Constable said that he proposed to ask for an adjournment for seven days, in order that inquiries might be made as. to twrnty pawn tickets found in prisoner's posseAion. Prisoner said the tickets related to her own pro- perty. There was a second charge to be preferred against prisoner, as it appeared she had been in the habit of going into people's. houses and " lifting " things. Inquiries were made by the pawnbroker of a place where prisoner had pawned an article or some articles suspected of .having been obtained in this way, and as a result Inspector Foster. was. called in.. and prisoner was apprehended. The remand was granted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>ROY'S ALLEGED THEFT FROM BROTHER'S POCKET.</head>
        <p>ROY'S ALLEGED THEFT FROM BROTHER'S POCKET.</p>
        <p>A boy was charged at the Warrington Police Court on Monday with stealing from a jacket pocket at his home, the sum of 14 in cash, the monies of his brother. The Mayor pre- sided over the Bench.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable said that on Friday, July 30th, prisoner's brother, the prosecutor, went to bed, leaving his jacket hanging in the house with bank notes in the pockets and • a script of the war loan. He got qff with the money, and nothing was heard of him till the Sunday morning, when he was seen by Inspector Foster sheltering in a doorway in Sankey-street. lie said he had missed the last train to Liver- pool. Inspector Foster was not satisfied, and, on inquiries being made, it was discovered that prisoner was the boy who had stolen the money. All the money had gone, with the ex- ception of 3s. 1.d.. Prisoner was remanded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>SOLDIERS' THEFTS FROM BARRACKS.</head>
        <p>SOLDIERS' THEFTS FROM BARRACKS.</p>
        <p>Practice Which Authorities Are Determined To Put Down.</p>
        <p>At Wednesday's Court Messrs. J. W. Hay- ward (presiding) and C. Jackson Holmes were on the Bench.</p>
        <p>Thomas Lofthouse, labourer, of no settled abode, wils charged with having, on Tuesday, August 3rd, received from a private at Orford Barracks a Pair of Army boots (value Is. 6d.) contrary to the Army Act, 1881. Lofthouse pleaded guilty to receiving the boots. Detective Green said that at 11.45 p.m. on that clay he saw prisoner going up Roorae-street with something under his coat. He followed, and stopped him in o.rford-lane, when he saw that he was carrying boots. When questioned, Lofthouse said he had had them given to him at Orford Barracks; later he admitted he got them over the wall. The day after he told that a certain private had given them to him, but "didn't know anything about it being wrong." Witness added that the private himself I had stolen them, and that he had been keeping watch in that district as a result of cofhplaints of these thefts from the military authorities.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>'Must Have a Market."</head>
        <p>'Must Have a Market."</p>
        <p>Sergeant-Major Quick, warrant officer, stated that frequently- boots and articles of clothing had been missed from the Barracks, and the men must have a market somewhere for them, as they had been got rid of, in one. way or another. The -military authorities felt that this man ought to be severely dealt with. Lofthouse: Well, I didn't want to steal them. He came to me and gave them to me. Superintendent Williamson: He was given them over the wall and should have known that wa's not correct, '</p>
        <p>Lofthouse: He wouldn't give them to me at the gate. Superintendent Williamson continued :that this man had been all over the country and the police had hardly been able to trace any- thing about him at all. During the last few days he had worked at Messrs. Crosfield's. Smartly Fined.</p>
        <p>The Chairman (to Lofthouse): You received these stolen articles of apparel; and we are determined to assist the authorities in putting down this form of stealing. You will be fined forty shillings or one month's imprisonment</p>
        <p>INGT (Regd. Trade Mark.) • oFFEE ESSENCE The Coffee Maid, of Symington's, says: "No Coffee like mine, I only use Symington 's." Be sure the water is boiling. 1:4.0,r' • 71105. SYMINGTON if CO., EJsnbligh and Limb*, -,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>LETTER LADIES.</head>
        <p>LETTER LADIES.</p>
        <p>Postwomen Make A Good Start in Warrington.</p>
        <p>an il4 wind that blows nobody goof!" The war has given women their chance. From taxi-driving, bank clerking and munition making they have proceeded to the letter de- livery; and, so far as Warrington is con- cerned they are covering themselves with glory. Owing to the numerous enlistments amongst the postmen—nearly seventy per cent. of the men have joined -the Forces—the Warrington Postmaster (Mr. H. Chambers) has tried the ex- periment, of employing women for the delivery of letters. An " Examiner " man was courteously received by the Postmaster yester- day and informed that the experiment is, go far as can at present be ascertained, working most -satisfactorily. In the words of Mr. Chambers, the 'postwomen are, "turning out trumps." "The way they are dropping into the home sorting "—[the street to street classi- fication carried out en route)---" is splendid," the Postmaster said.</p>
        <p>Mr. Wrench, the Inspector in charge of the postal staff, informed our representative that there were five "lady postmen" at present en the staff and another one was about to com- mence work. They take their place side by side with the men as ordinary postmen," he said, "and do a full day's work." Their ser- vices were obtained through the medium of the local Labour Exchange. The day after the. intimation was given to the Labour Exchange there were thirty applications. As a beginning the ladies are to work the easier beats, but a.* they advance in kknowledv of the game they will be introclucea to the more difficult routes.. At present no uniform is being worn.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>REVELATION.</head>
        <p>A REVELATION.</p>
        <p>The delicious crispness, freedom from al/ trace of greasiness, and the perfect digestibility, only obtainable when ATORA Beef Suet is used for frying fish and pancakes, is a revelation. 10d. per 1111. carton, whether block or shredded, and 50. per Vb. carton. Ask your grocer for Atora in blocks and refuse substitutes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>LIGHTNING DAMAGE.</head>
        <p>LIGHTNING DAMAGE.</p>
        <p>Pen Wrecked In Young Clerk's Fingers.</p>
        <p>The thunderstorm that visited the town en Thursday, shortly after noon, left its trade mark in several places. A young clerk in the goods 'office of —the Midland Railway, by the Central Station, was writing when there was a vivid lightning flash, and the pen in his fingers was wrecked, the ironwork being torn from the wooden holder. The lad experienced a slight shock, but was none the worse."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>Cemetery Belfry Damaged.</head>
        <p>Cemetery Belfry Damaged.</p>
        <p>Another instance of damage by lightning was that of the belfry of the Roman Cathane Church in the Cemetery, earlier in the week, the destructive fluid entering the cavity and cracking a part of the stonework inside. The belfry is of Yorkshire stone. A few pieces were thrown down and broken, but happily the lightning did not strike down into the church. The belfry, however, will require considerable attention, as a large gap is now clearly to be seen in the stone and one side is. cracked right down and might be dislodged by a strong wind. Roof Torn In The Causeway.</p>
        <p>The lightning was impartial in its wander- ings. A third- district it visited with dsquiet- ing results•was the neighbourhood of Wilders- pool-causeway. The .roof of a private house be- tween the Causeway Hotel and the Presbyteriau Church was slightly torn.</p>
        <p>There is a good deal of mirage about mar- riage.</p>
        <p>Many a man repents at leisure who didn't marry in haste.</p>
