      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>i 4 THE EXAMINER. .1 , t 1 SATtin-DAT, AUGUST 14, 191-5.</head>
        <p>i 4 THE EXAMINER. .1 , t 1 SATtin-DAT, AUGUST 14, 191-5.</p>
        <p>GERMANY'S HIGH TIDE.</p>
        <p>Several of the leading American newspapers suggest that the. Gorman Government recog- nises that Germany has now reached the zenith of its possible successes, and is cense- quently Q11X1011.4 to effect a peace settlement at the most favourable moment -for itself. This reading of the German mind is principally based on 'a message sent by the Kaiser, through the German Chancellor, to the "-United American Press," in which he claims that Warsaw has brought peace in sight, and urges the plea that Germany is fighting for the freedom of the commerce of the world. It is also stated' in several Continental journals that the Kaiser proposed peace to Russia through the King of Denmark—an offer which was repulsed with indignatidn, and a reminder that Russia, who has pledged herself only to make peace in consultation and conjunction with her Allies, does not regard her engage- ment as "a serap of paper." When Ger- many talks of the freedom of the seas and of world-commerce, she means the destruction of British sea-power. Great Britain is quite con- tent that neutrals should contrast her uses of sea-power during a hundred years with Ger- many's exercise of land-power in Belgium. But the crass obtuseness of the German official mind is surprisingly di/splayed. in the belief that any sane person, not a German, could swallow the proposition that Germany, with her extreme protectionist system, desires to crush the sea-power of Great Britain, who stands for the open door to all nations, in the interests of freedom of commerce.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>ARE WE DOING OUR SHARE?</head>
        <p>ARE WE DOING OUR SHARE?</p>
        <p>Our sympathy for Russia, who has for some time been bearing the chief brunt of the Ger- man attack, and for France, who has seen' the invader entrenched 43n her soil for more than twelve months, is being exploited by a cer- tain section of the Press in order to insinuate, for purposes of its own, that we. as a nation ' are not bearing anything like our fair share of the war. It is, of course, right and proper that we should, as a nation,i be eageri to in- crease our contribution to tile efforts of ;the Allies, and still more so that we should, indi- vidually, ask ourselves whethpr we are doing all that we can and ought to do. It is by cumulative effort that the, final result will be won, and there is a sense in which it is true to say that we ought not to be satisfied until the utmost limit of effort has been reached. But that is not to say that we should minimise and disparage the efforts and achievements that the British Empire can already set to its credit, or impair the usefulness of the assistance which she can give by hastening to proclaim our zeal in ways which are more showy, but less effective, than those which the special conditions both of our Empire and of this conflict point out for us.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>PUBLIC MISFORTUNE.</head>
        <p>A PUBLIC MISFORTUNE.</p>
        <p>Mr. HOLMAN KINGDON, under whose chair- manship the Medical Inspection Committee of the Education Committee have in, a short time brought about remarkable improvements in the health of , the school children of War- rington, has resigned. He gives his reasons in a letter he addresses to us to-day, reasons we have reported and supported on many occasions. The Health Comnlittee insist upon making all the appointments of officials em- ployed by the Inspection Committee, and the Town Council on Tuesday night supported the Health Committee. The result is the children of the town lose one of their best friends, and the town itafeif may suffer sadly in the long run. We hear that . other members of the Inspection Committee may follow their chair-</p>
        <p>man. May we hope, as we said last week, that these gentlemen, whose services we can ill afford to lose, may withhold their resigna- tions until the Health Committee have be- come wiser than they seem to be when they, with their eyes open, alienate some of their best auxiliary workers in their desire to treat those workers as interfering interlopers?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>COUNCILLOR COUGH AND WATER CHARGES.</head>
        <p>COUNCILLOR COUGH AND WATER CHARGES.</p>
        <p>Councillor GOUGH succeeded' by his persist- ent' efforts not only in getting a hearing on the water question, but in getting his motion carried at the last meeting of the Council, held on Tuesday evening. His complaint had reference to the more favourable terms that the Corporation have offered to firms using large quantities of soft water for manufac- turing purposes. It is usually held, as it was argued on Tuesday, that large buyers of any- thing get better terms than smaller buyers, but those in favour of bringing prices to a state of uniformity argued that 'the bigger consumers were,being favoured. It is a pity that a question involving so important an economic point should have been decided by sixteen members of the Council, which is 36 members strong. The motion to rescind the new rates and reinstate the old ones was carried by ten votes to six—figures which have a holiday fragrance about them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>SERVE BY SAVING.</head>
        <p>SERVE BY SAVING.</p>
        <p>THRIFT.</p>
        <p>The Imperative Need For National And Local Campaign To Explain Individual Economy. Its Meaning.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the local Thrift Committee, held at the Town Halkon Thursday night, Mr. W. Peter Rylands presiding over a good attend- ance, it was decided to hold a series of open-air meetings in different parts of the borough, at which speakers will explain the need there is for national and individual, thrift at the pre- sent time. The first meeting will take place to-night at Bridge Foot at 7.30, when the speakers will include Mr. Ryiand.s and Alderman Tinnion. Other meetings arranged for next week are : Monday; Bewsey-street, by Farmers' Stores.</p>
        <p>Thursday, Latchford, near Cheshire Cheese. Saturday, Bridge 'Foot. How People Can Help.</p>
        <p>1. By steady work and good workmanship, in producing necessary things. 2. By wise expenditure on the necssaries Of life and by getting full value out of everything bought—especially-food: - 3. By avoiding .wa4e of all kinds. By wing work—not by making work which is wasteful. Big demands make high prices. 4. By ,giving up luxuries, the making of which diverts men from pie more useful kinds of labour.</p>
        <p>.5. By banking or otherwise investing money not required for food and other necessaries. 6. By paying promptly far goods supplied and services rendered, because long. credit is Wateful and tends to increase prices.,,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>NCREASED LIABILITIES.</head>
        <p>NCREASED LIABILITIES.</p>
        <p>To the Editor,of '"•The Examiner."</p>
        <p>is Ow 4uty- of everyone to study the reasons why our statesmen, financiers, and others are urging the imperative necessity of national and individual economy. I propose to set forth the reasons for individual economy from two standpoints—first, the direct effect of the war on the nation's purse; 'second, the indirect effect on the industrial life of the nation.</p>
        <p>—.— It is almost certain that before the war is finished, and we hove cleared up the whole liabilities, our national debt will be increased ona thousand 'five hundred.- millibn—the in- crease is already about one thousand million. Interest at 44 per cent. on '" this stun involves an annual charge of sixty-seven million. ! Nothing i here -iillevred for 'redemption or ' -paying AA of debt. Then there is the further burden of pro- viding (which the nation must in honour do) for the dependents of those who have been killed or maimed. Suppose we say that of the possible three hundred thousand killed and maimed half of these have dependents, and the cost of these on the average will be, per family fifteen shilling's a week, equal to over six millions per annum. These two items together arqount to seventy-three mil- lion.</p>
        <p>This sum works out to nearly 83s. per annum</p>
        <p>for every unit of the population—man, woman and child. If we consider that each pro- ducer on the average supports one woman and two children there will be a burden of .26 12s. per annum laid upon each worker. In other words, that ~£6 12s. per annum, or half-a- crown a week of a man's earnings must go to pay his share. Of course, I am speaking only of averages. It would, for example, be almost impossible for a man getting 225. a week to find• an. extra half-a-crown a week. Even if the poorer classes have to pay nothing directly in taxes they will have to pay in increased cost in what they buy. This in- creased cost of living has already arrived, and we cannot expect it to come down for many years. A. sovereign is now only worth 17s. in its purchasing power; in food alone it is only worth 165., i.e., compared with pre-war time. Every manufacturer or trader who makes or distributes the necessaries of life will have the extra taxes to Pay, and this imuSt inevit- ably increase the prices of the.article produced and 'distributed. However the new taxes may be arranged, a considerable part of the burden will fall on the consumer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>Indirect Effects.</head>
        <p>The Indirect Effects.</p>
        <p>So far I have only dealt with the charges which affect the National Exchequer. These are probably the minor c,ausee which will affect adversely the well-I4ing of the people. The indirect effects of war, although more difficult to calculate, are probably the more serious.</p>
        <p>First: We sustain loss through the men killed and maimed in the war. The number of these will probably not be less than three hundred thousand. The economic value of in able-bodied worker can be taken on the average as £6OO. This means that his yearly -worth is ,X3O; in other words, he earns £3O more than will keep himself. This means a loss of nine million pounds per annum. Second: Whatever our feelings at the pre- sent time towards Germany may be, the im- portant economic fact to be remembered is that Germany was our second best customer, and a large and profitable trade existed be- tween the two countries. With a ruined Germany and an impoverished England, we cannot expect that this trade can be resumed for many years to come in anything like the proportion which existed before the War.</p>
        <p>Third: We haveto consider the effect of dear money caused by the enormous consumption of capital in the war. If the manufacturer, has to pay .£6 per annum for every £lOO he uses, and for which formerly he only paid' £4, manufacture becomes more expensive, and, in consequence, the goods produced .dearer. Fourth: We have also to suffer the material loss of warships, merchant ships, and the individual losses and war expenses incurred by individuals and .manufacturers apart from the nation. The aggregate amount of this must be very large. Fifth: When the war ends, many trades, new prosperous, will be reduced to stagna- tion. Workmen now earning high wages may possibly have to seek work and may find none, and our Poor Rates will go up if we have to sustain those who cannot find work. This means higher rates and higher rents. It is contended that Germany is wiser in looking ahead and preparing for the evil day than we are. Perhaps ;there is something in this, and we do not give the thought we ought to the future. We must mend our, ways, and it .is our -clear (Int,' to preiiire ourselves for the, time of depression which may, and almost Certainly will, follow this exhausting war. Waste Involves Want. •</p>
        <p>It is possible that there may be some minor advantages which might be named as Et set-off against these losses and disadvantages, but they will not go far to balance the account, and are too uncertain to depend on. my readers do not .care to calculate the effect of increased national debt, dear money, and the like, put these aside and grasp this one truth—that war involves enormous waste, that its consumption of labour products is wholly unproductive, that the holes in the ground made by the explosion of shells which have taken much labour to produce bring no economic return, but only waste, and that this involves want. The above are some of the reasons why the leaders of the nation have thought it neces- sary to inaugurate this National Thrift Cam- paign, and why we in Warrington are doing what we can to help. We want everyone, therefore, to consider this matter very seriously. Do not be de- ceived and made indifferent by the present artificial activity due to war requirements. What of the time when the war is over, and the war, demand ceases, and three million men now under arms require -work at home? We must not treat this thing with indifference or hide our heeds in fear, but bravely face the lowering future. And that we may be pre- pared for the coming time, of stress and strain we must husband our resources, we must save our money, we must lesson our expenditure, and we must cut out our luxuries. Will all enlist to Serve by Saving? CHARLES E. PARKER. Penketh, August 10th, 1915.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>w n thIGION STEAMER SUNK.</head>
        <p>w n thIGION STEAMER SUNK.</p>
        <p>Fate Of The Surnmerfield 0</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>Zillah Company.</head>
        <p>Zillah Company.</p>
        <p>-Yesterday afternoon th,e Press Assodation issued the following message:7-</p>
        <p>The ,steamer Summerfield has been sunk</p>
        <p>The mate, his wife, and the chief enginee were drowned. ' Seven of the crew were landed; and two sailor 3, badly injured, were taken to hOspital for treatment. Captain Saidley was rescued. The names of some of the chief crew are:— , Captain GP Hardley, Preston, In hospital, injured, but not seriously. Thomas Summerfield, Parston, mate, drowned. Owen drowned.</p>
        <p>Griffiths, single, chief engineer,</p>
        <p>The Summerfield was a steel screw three- masted steamer of 687 tons, built in 1913. She was owned by the Zillah Shipping and Carrying Company. Limited, and registered at Liverpool. This company was originally n Warrington com- pany.</p>
        <p>The Broken Promise. Arthur sat on the front doorsteps crying softly. "What is the matter, little boy r' asked a kind-hearted woman who was passing. "Ma's gone an' drowned all the kittens," he sobbed. "What a pity! I'm awfully sorry." "And s'she promised—boohoo !—'at I c'u'd cli• it."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>WORKERS AND SAVINGS.</head>
        <p>WORKERS AND SAVINGS.</p>
        <p>Trades And Labour Council Suggest' More Wages: I At za Meeting of the Warrington Trades and Labour Council, held at the Bridge Foot Rooms on Tuesday evening, The Secretary (Mr. C. Dukes) informed the delegates that he had, been invited by tie Mayor to attend a meeting in connection with the National Thrift Campaign. lie did not attend. (Laughter.) Not because he was out of town at the time—for if he had been at home he would not have attended. Mr.. W. Rowland (the secretary) now invited the Council to nominate three- representatives on the town. Thrift Com- mittee. He (the speaker) had prepared the following reply, which now only • awaited the Council's cazifirtnation :- August 11th, 1.915. Deer Sir and Bro.,—Replying to your invita- tion to appoint three representatives of Labour, we feet it our duty, to first see that the worker gets sufficient for his material requirements. Apart from a small minority, the workers are not receiving sufficient to meet their domestic needs. Prices continue to rise, whilst wageS. despite war bonuses, have lost their pur- chasing value, leaving the worker considerably worse off than he was prior to the war. Men are working excessive overtime, which make their wages, when viewed in the aggregate, appear more favourable than hitherto, but anyone who has experienced the strain of long hours knows too well that extra money 63 earned is required to make good the losl of vitality sustained. - We do not objeet.to the lesson of national thrift being taught to those ,who enjoy excessive wealth, but past experience has taught us that sermons are of little use to the favoured classes. The workers are to-day giving of their best to the country's need. We think the true economy is that of judicious rest and healthy recreation. If those who are now working at high pressure are to be soured by thrift lectures and chastised for their reckless expenditure by people who enjoy many times their income then we may expect his value as a productive unit to be seriously impaired. We are of the opinion that double the amount now earned could with advantage be expended in hundreds of working class homes.. We should like to witness a development of the desire for better food, clothing and home life amongst the workers. Our grumble is that they are prepared to do without so much. It may with advantage be pointed out to your com- mittee that dazing the. wax- prices have in- creased approxim.ately 38 per cent. or Is. 6d. in the pound, whilst the worker has received a miserable 3s. war bonus on the colossal wage of 225. per week, this is roughly 2s. 91d. in the pound, which leaves the worker worse off by Is. 81d. than be was before the war broke out. To preach thrift to men who are overworked and underpaid is an occupation for which we as Trade Unionists are unfitted. We must therefore respectfully decline your offer. Yours sincerely, • For the Council. ' CIIAS. DUKES.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. J. Rowland. Secretary Local Thrift Committee. (Laughter, and hear. hear.) The Chairman (Mr. F. Berry) said ,that he also had been invited to that, meeting and attended just to see what would be done—with a perfectly open mind. He afterwards found he had been appointed on the committee, quite without his sanction.</p>
        <p>Mr. E. Plinston said he believed the move- ment should be to increase the wage and not to tell the people how to spend it! It was accordingly resolved nem. con. that the letter be forwarded to the Thrift Committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>THRIFT COMMITTEE'S REPLY.</head>
        <p>THE THRIFT COMMITTEE'S REPLY.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the local Thrift Committee on Thursday night it was decided to send the following reply Dear Mr. Dukes,—Your letter of the 11th inst. was laid before the meeting of the com- mittee, and / am directed to express to you their great regret that the Warrington Trades and Labour Coudeil canirt see their way to unite in the National Thrift Campaign. , The committee fully and regretfully Appreci- ate that in many cases wages earned are not sufficient to provide any margin for Saving, and they certainly have no desire to lecture working men or even to criticise in any shape or form the manner in which they spend their money.</p>
        <p>Much money, however, is undoubtedly spent wastefully by all classes—rich had poor alike, and the object .of the National Thrift Move- ment, under the presidency of the Prime Minister,. the Rt.'Hon.• A. Boner Law and. the Rt. Hon. Arthur Henderson, is only intended to lay stress upon the importance of the matter to the country, and explain hew immensely the .effect of thrift will help the nation in the pre- sent crisis of her history'.</p>
        <p>Yours, faithfully, WILI'RID J. ROWLAND</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>SIR ARTHUR'S THANKS.</head>
        <p>SIR ARTHUR'S THANKS.</p>
        <p>Council's Congratulations Wilt Be Cherished All His Life.</p>
        <p>Sir Arthur Crosfie/d's reply to a letter from the General PurposeS Committee, of the Town ,Council, congratulating him on his ',reception of 4 baronetcy, was read at Tuesday's meeting of the C,ouncil.,„The letber was addressed to the Town Clerk, and read as follows: " West-hill, Highgate, " July 30, 1915-:</p>
        <p>"Dear -Sir,--May. I ask you to be so good as to convey to our Town Council my very sincere and heartfelt thanks for the resolution they have passed ; and for the most welcome message of congratulation which in this resolution they have sent Jim? I am most deeply grateful and p,pprecititive, and warmly thank every member of the Council for the resolution, which I so highly Salim Coming as it does from members of the Council, irrespective of party, this resolu- tion and message will be cherished all my life as an honour I can only compare to the dignity his Majesty has graciously conferred upon me.' "I send you also very cordial thanks and acknowledgments for the kind congratulations I have received from your brother officials and from yourself."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>Colborne-street Baptist Church.</head>
        <p>Colborne-street Baptist Church.</p>
        <p>The preacher at the Golborne-street Baptist Church to-morrow will be the Rev. Professor Ellis, of the Baptist College, Manchester. Ccl- lections will be taken for the College, whioh is the only one of the denomination in Lanca- shire. Professor Ellis is very popular amongst Baptists.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>ENUMERATING.</head>
        <p>ENUMERATING.</p>
        <p>Prevalent Ideas Upon The National Register.</p>
        <p>HUMOUR AND PATHOS.</p>
        <p>'The very hairs of your head are all num- bered."—Matthew x. 38</p>
        <p>" 9th to .14th August; enumerators to de- liver all their forms during this week." So ran the direction to the workers, and on Mon- day morning Mr. X. could be seen setting out in good trim for his duties. Naturally, as he • approached his 'district"=--which was in one of the poorer localities 14 ot very far from his home—his thoughta were occupied in conjec- turing what sort of reception he would be accorded. It was not without some temerity that he knocked upon the first door. , For some minutes no answer was forthcom- ing. He rapped -again in manner half- apologetic. hi the twinkling of an eye the door stood wide open, and a shilling was liter- ally thrust into his face. Mr. X. Stammered and gasped. _</p>
        <p>"Oh, I beg pardon," e-xcfaimed the some- what untidy housewife who had appeared; "I thought you was the club collector."</p>
        <p>" No -madam; I have called in connection with the National Register, I should like to know how many forms "Oh, well, there's none here you can tek for a soldier," interrupted the good dame; "they've had my mesther already. He's out at V Front now; been there three months and wounded once. His casuality was in the Paper —" "Yes, yes," broke in Mr. I. somewhat breathlessly. "You need not, of course, fill up a form for him. But You will have to take one for every person between fifteen and sixty- five who sleeps in this house next Sunday; and I want to know how many you will require." "Oh, I see. Well, there's me and our Martha and our Jim—but he's on what you call the munitions; you can't conscription him."</p>
        <p>The enumerator explained that the idea was not conscription, but that he would still</p>
        <p>need a form.</p>
        <p>" Oh," reiterated the lady. " Well, that's all," she concluded, eyeing him with some dis- trust. Mr. X. extracted two grey forms and a white one from his box, and handed them to her. She gazed at them with a critical air. _</p>
        <p>"You understand how to fill them ?" he ventured.</p>
        <p>"Oh, yes," she said, " but I don't see the use of 'em here. I couldn't do nothing for the war, and Martha's at home, and Jim couldn't make shift without me. One from t' family's enough, and he keeps all t' rest of us."</p>
        <p>" Yes," remarked the enumerator, with a non-committal air. "By the way, in filling the form, you must not say you are dependent upon your husband, you know."</p>
        <p>"Eh, but I am." "Perhaps so, but the Government says not. You are 'independent' unless you are crippled, or anything like that." "Oh, that's it, is it? Would they say that if we wanted the vote?" she added, in an after-thought. The enumerator coughed. " What's this about occupation 'P" she inquired casually, after a pause. "Well, you may put'household duties'</p>
        <p>in that column," answered Mr. X. "I see. Doing for the soldiers, like. If you ask me, them soldiers don't wish for to be waited on," the lady opined. "They'd sooner make shift theirselves—e.,specially them as have been in the trenches. Maybe, though, I could cook 'em a good bit o' bacon, if that's what it means!"</p>
        <p>" I'm sure I can't say," sighed the gentle- man. Do you understand the rest?" She scanned the forms. " Yes, I think so." "Well, good morning'! "Good morning. Oh, by the way, our Nellie's just turn'd fifteen, now I think of it. I'll want another form!"</p>
        <p>* *</p>
        <p>At the end of three hours Mr. X. had de- livered ten lots of forms. But he had also made many discoveries. For one thing he had learned that even in this day there are people who cannot read and wish they could. He had learned that there are others who have never been taught to offer a visitor a chair, but who, when he asks if he may take _one, hasten to dust it with their apron, and trust that he will" pardon their iggernerance.".:He found that though people do not believe in laws they will obey them willingly. And he found that they can take an interest in the things which concern them.</p>
        <p>* *</p>
        <p>Other discoveries there were, too, which he</p>
        <p>made. He found, for instance, at least one home N'here there lived an old couple, who had pensions and a dozy black cat. The man was paralysed, and the lady rheumatic. They had a fine young son—an only child. But no form could be left there. Their son had given his life at the Front Ever the faith endures, England, my England : Take and break us;-we are yours, England, my own! Life is good and joy runs.high Between English earth and sky: Death is death; but we shall Vdie To the song on your bugles blown. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>MR. HOLMAN KINGDON</head>
        <p>MR. HOLMAN KINGDON</p>
        <p>Resigns Chairmanship Of Medical Inspection Committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>AN UNFORTUNATE DISPUTE.</head>
        <p>AN UNFORTUNATE DISPUTE.</p>
        <p>To the Editor of " The Examiner."</p>
        <p>Hill Crest, Frodsham, Cheshire, August 13th, 1915.</p>
        <p>, Sir,—ln viei of..what took plakte.at the Coun- cil meeting on Tuesday last I have to-day written to the Chairman of the Education Com- mittee as per copy enclosed.—Yours faithfully, • HOLMAN KINGDON.</p>
        <p>Dear Mr. Roberts,— In ,view of the decision of the Town Council on Tuesday to support the action of the Health Committee, I beg to resign my position as Chairman of the Medical Inspection Com- mittee, such resignation to .date from to-day. After starting the dental clinic and appoint- ing our ovrti °filters, the Health Committee now wish to make • these appointments them- selves, and also to select the school medical officer, without reXerence to us in any shape or form, although we pay five-sixths of his salary and are responsible for the( results of the work, which is a statutory imposition and Which has actually been deputed by the town to the Medical Inspection Committee. In these circumstances I am unable to accept any further responsibility for the work of this committee, and can only express my regret that Warrington should insist on taking a course of action which, so far as I know, has not com- mended itself to any other town or borough in the United Kingdom, and which is neither calculated to produce efficiency nor be in the best interests of the chl—ren themselves. , The five years of my association with this committee haVe witnessed the establishment in Warrington, amongst the earliest towns of Eng- land, of the treatment and dental clinics, which are achieving far-reaching results, and that our organisation has been on sound lines is shown by the fact that these clinics have always been awarded the maximum grant allowed by the Government scheme.</p>
        <p>The thought that has always been uppermost in our minds has been the welfare of the chil- dren, and whoever my successor may be I hope that he will allow this to continue to be the guiding spirit of his policy in the future. • Yours. sincerely. HOLMAN KINGDON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>LOCAL INTE.,LLIGENCZ.</head>
        <p>LOCAL INTE.,LLIGENCZ.</p>
        <p>Penketh and Sankey Show.</p>
        <p>The Penketh,,Sankey_and District Garden and Cage Bird Society's annual show will be held this afternoon, starting at three o'clock. There will be sports, competitions and a band.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>Electrical Engineer and Munitions.</head>
        <p>Electrical Engineer and Munitions.</p>
        <p>was reported by the Electricity and Tram- Ways Committee, at Tuesday's meeting of the Town Council, that the electrical engineer had been authorised to assist in the out Put of munitions of war.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>Technical W•oodworkers.</head>
        <p>Technical W•oodworkers.</p>
        <p>The following students of the Warrington Technical Institute have passed the First Year's examination in Manual Training (woodwork) of the City and Guilds of London Ircatitutes Davenport, Raymond. Jones, Fred. • Roughaedge, Robert L.</p>
        <p>Sixpence Per Meal For Prisoners. The Watch Committee minutes, submitted at th.e meeting of the Town Council on Tuesday. included this resolution: "That sixpence be allowed for each meal supplied to prisoners sentenced by the justices to detention in police cells, under Section 13 of the Criminal Justices Administration Act, 1914, as also for naval and military prisoners."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>Obstruction in the Market.</head>
        <p>Obstruction in the Market.</p>
        <p>The minutes of the Markets Committee read at Tuesday's meeting of tbeovin Council, con- tained the following :—Resolved, that notices be given to all tenants in the Market, who obstruct the passages or footways, to remove such obstructions, and that, failing such removal, proceedings be taken against offenders for a breach of the bylaws.--The minute was approved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>Assistant Nuisances Inspector.</head>
        <p>Assistant Nuisances Inspector.</p>
        <p>The Health Committee reported at Tuesday's meeting of the Town Council that the com- mittee had interviewed the four candidates for the appointment of assistant inspector of nuisances, and that it was resolved that •Mr. F. J. Sanders be appointed assistant inspector of nuisances, at a salary of £7B, per annum, rising by annual increments of £6 10s. to a maximum of .£125 per annum.—The minute was .apiaroved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>Temporary Health Appointment.</head>
        <p>Temporary Health Appointment.</p>
        <p>The Health Committee reported at Tuesday's meeting of the Town Council that they had interviewed Dr. D. R. Qilder, of Rotherham, after considering a number of applications for the temporary post of assistant medical officer of health, and had resolved: "That he be ap- pointed temporary assistant medical officer of health, at a salary of ..2300 per annum, the appointment to be determinable on three months' notice." The recommendation' was approved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>Blue Coat School Retirement.</head>
        <p>Blue Coat School Retirement.</p>
        <p>The Holy Trinity Parish Magazine, comment- ing upon the retirement of Mr. and Mrs. E. Rose, headmaster and matroa of the Blue Coat School, says: "Their connection with the school is a splendid record of work faithfully done, to the entire satisfaction of the scholars and their guardians, as well as to that of the trustees. We are sure they will carry with them into their retirement the affection of many and the respect and good wishes of all their felldw townsmen."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>"Not Painters' Work."</head>
        <p>"Not Painters' Work."</p>
        <p>Mr. Bebbington, painters' delegate to the Trades and Labour Council, referred on Tues- day night to a dispute which had arisen between the painters and Mr. W. S. Haddock (the Cor- poration gas manager). Previously the lamp- lighters had been painting their standards, and at the painters' request Mr. Haddock had it stopped immediately. Now the gas-meter men were painting the meters with whitewash, and Mr.-Haddock contended that it was not painters' work, as it was only done to protect the iron.— The matter i was left to be dealt with by the Council Secretary and Mr. Bebbington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>Unauthorised Street Collections.</head>
        <p>Unauthorised Street Collections.</p>
        <p>The minutes of the Watch Committee, sub- mitted at Tuesday's meeting of the Town Council, contained the following :,--Complaints having been received of collections in the streets for war• funds by unauthorised persons, and of promiscuous selling of articles and col- lections in the streets by school children: Resolved, that the Town Clerk issue a notice requesting the public not to contribute to any war fund except to persons authorised to col- lect by this committee, and forbidding school children to sell articles or to collect nion.ey in the streets.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>Effect of Dearer Fuel.</head>
        <p>Effect of Dearer Fuel.</p>
        <p>The minute; of the Electricity and Tramways Committee, placed before Tuesday's meeting of the Town Council, contained the following: A report by the Electrical Engineer on the effect of the. increased price of fuel was read. Re- solved: That the price of electric current be `increased ai3 follows as and from theist August, 1915: For lighting purposes and for tramways, 10 per cent., in addition to the existing scale of charges; for heating and power purposes (ex- cept in cases provided for in which the charges for current are regulated by the price of coal), 15 per cent., in addition to the existing scale of charges.—The item was approved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>"Not Eldorado For Railwaymen."</head>
        <p>"Not Eldorado For Railwaymen."</p>
        <p>At Tuesday night's Trades and Labour Coun- cil meeting the Secretary (Mr. Dukes) read a letter. from the Railway National Society, in- viting help, the object being the protection of :the railwaymen in their industrial condition-s after the war. Mr. Dukes went on to say that the executive sympathised with the Society's object, but felt that perhaps the various Unions catering for the me ,n would be a better driving force. He felt that with the Council's' limited funds they could not :flake any grant— and after-all, it was not going to prove the workers' Eldorado which they were seeking.— There was no discussion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>Local Call To Government.</head>
        <p>Local Call To Government.</p>
        <p>At Tuesday's Trades and Labour Council meeting it was unanimously decide&amp; on the motion of Mr. E. Plinston that copies of the following resolution be forwarded to the various Parliamentary leaders: "That this meeting views with increasing alarm the increasing high price of coal and other necessaries, and calls upon the Government to use every means in ,its powers to bring to a speedy end the attempts of certain unpatriotic people to make profits from Ur war." Mr. Plinston said he thought Sometinies that the importance of this question Of profit-making in war time was not brought before the public with the emphasis it should be,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>First Burial In Soldiers' Ground.</head>
        <p>First Burial In Soldiers' Ground.</p>
        <p>The first use.of the' ground in the Cemetery set apart and dedicated for the burial of soldiers who die from wounds in this district had to be made 'on Tuesday afternoon, when the, Rev. Mr. .Chadwick. army chaplain at the. LOrd Derby 'War hospital, Winwick,i con ducted the funeral of Private Robert Baynes, of the 4th Yorkshire Regiment—who had passed away at that hospital on 'Satiirclay. A strong military detachment, with band, marched from the hostpital to the cemetery and back, along .)vith, the *hearse. They were to have come through town (via Market Gate), but were pre- vented from, doing so by the storm which came on. At the graveside a considerable crowd gathered, and there were many wet eyes as the "Last Post" was sounded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>Upper Mersey Navigation.</head>
        <p>Upper Mersey Navigation.</p>
        <p>We cull the appended paragraph from the minutes of the Rivers Committee submitted on Tuesday at the Town Council meeting :—Mr. R. T. Fairclough, the representative of' the Corporation on the Tipper Mersey gavigation Commission, reported that the charts showed that the channel'was fairly good. It runs on the Lancashire side of Richmond Island, while farther down it rune along by. Cuerdley Marsh past the entrance to Widnes Dock, from there it crosses over to Runcorn, then back again through the North Runcorn Bridge past the entrance to West Bank Dock, and over the Seldom Seen Rocks at Hale. The channel con- tinues good from Hale to Garston. The ap- proaches to Widnes Dock, West Bank Dock, and Old Quay Lock are good. There is no channel to Bridgewater or Weston Locks. The lightships could not be better placed nor im- proved upon_</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>Councillor Hawthorn's Resignation.</head>
        <p>Councillor Hawthorn's Resignation.</p>
        <p>Ex-Councillor.llawthorn, replying in a letter that was read at Tuesday's TOWn, Gonncil meeting, to a resolution acknowledging his services, sale" it was a source of gratification to him to know that his services were appreciated, and he thanked the members for their kind resolution.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>To-morrow's Park Band.</head>
        <p>To-morrow's Park Band.</p>
        <p>The Warrington. Borough Prize Brasa Band (conductor, Mr. T. Griffin) will play in the Park to-morrow, the following being the programme: Hymn, "Golden City"; march, "For Freedom and Honour," W. Rimmer; oyerture, "Caliph of Bagdad," Boieldieu; selection, " Maaanello," Auber ; solo, "Ora Pro Nobia," M. Piccolomini; selection, " Mercadamte"; "God gave the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>King."</head>
        <p>King."</p>
        <p>Footballer Married.</p>
        <p>,Bert Bradshaw (to use the appellation by which he is most widely known), the popular skipper of •'the Warrington. Rugby Football Club, was. married on Thursday week to Miss Leigh. daughter of Mr. Leigh, of Hall-lane, GrappenhalL The ceremony took place at the Grappenhall Parish Church, and the nappy' couple ouipsequently departed to Blackpool for the honeymoon. Mr. Bradshaw will have the warmest 'es of his numerous frieade and admirers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>Smallpox Hospital.</head>
        <p>The Smallpox Hospital.</p>
        <p>In moving the confirmation of the Health Committee's minutes at the' meeting of the Town Council en Tuesday evening, Alderman James Evans .pointed out that the plans for the srualipox hospital, which had been adopted by the Council, had received the approval of the Local Government Board, although certain amendments had been made which would have the effect of increasing the loan from £1,50 to</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>Pigeon versus Motor Driver.</head>
        <p>Pigeon versus Motor Driver.</p>
        <p>A light motor van owned in Northwich waa, on Thursday, being driven from Budworth to Stretton. When near Mr. Derbyshire's farm a pigeon dew into the driver's face and eaused him to release his hold of the wheel_ The result was that the van skidded, ran into a ditch, turned over, and threw out all the occu- pants. None of them were much hurt, but the pigeon was so badly damaged that it had to be destroyeel.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>New Deputy-Lieutenant.</head>
        <p>New Deputy-Lieutenant.</p>
        <p>One of the new deputy-lieutenants for the county of Lancaster, recently appointed by Lord Shuttleworth, the Lord Lieutenant, is tolonel John Arthur Edelsten, C. 8., T.D. He is n member of an old and highly respected Warrington family, and for several years was in command of the 4th Batt. Prince of Wale? Volunteers (South Lancashire Regiment). He has held with conspicuous ability from time to time a large number of important military appointments. He was born in 1863 and is the son of Mr. J. Malin Ecielsten. He was appointed a justice of the peace for the borough of War- rington in 199.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>Trams and Relief of Rates.</head>
        <p>Trams and Relief of Rates.</p>
        <p>During the year ending March 31st last, the profits made on the Warrington Corporation tramways totalled £2,499, a decrease of £741 on the previous year's figure,,. Passenger receipts amounted to .222,632, compared with £23,233 for the year preceding. There is nothing excessive about the decline, taking into consideration the circumstances of the times. The temporary- de- crease in the resident population has affected each of the rontes with the exception of that to Latchford, the receipts for 'this route showing an advance of nearly a halfpenny per car mile. The net profit has been carried to the appre- priation account, increasing the balance in hand to £6,053, of which £2,000 is to be transferred to the relief of rates.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>Scotland-road Fire.</head>
        <p>Scotland-road Fire.</p>
        <p>In the minutes of the Fire Brigade Cora- miittee, presented at the Town Council meeting on Tuesday, was the following: The Superin- tendent reported the meritorious conduct of the following: Messrs. Percy Allansoa, J. W. Arm- strong, Thos. Bramhall, W. Bridge, Sergt. F. Clegg (fire brigade), and P.S. Kenwright, at a fire which occurred at premises in Scotland- road, which resulted in the rescue of two chil- dren and the saving of their lives. It was re- solved, that the best thanks of this committee be given to the above-named, and that steps be taken to bring their conduct to the notice of the Royal Humane Society. The committees rec.ommendatioa was approved. It will be remembered that, as reported in " The Examiner" at the time, two young chil- dren were rescued at this fire through the sky- light and with much difficulty.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>White Heather Day.</head>
        <p>White Heather Day.</p>
        <p>We would remind our readers of the grand fete and bowling carnival which takes plaeo next Saturday on the Cricket Ground at Arpley. The proceeds are in aid of the Infirmary, the Nursing Association, and the Children's Sum- mer Camp. The great number of 'calls in con- nection with purely war losses and casualties has caused the claims of these deserving chari- ties tp be somewhat overlooked. This is par- tic ulai.ly so in the case of the Infirmary, where a great number of wounded soldiers have had the benefit of treatment. In connection with the distribution and sale of white heather on Saturday Mr. J.. H. Smethurst, chairman ,of the Infirmary Board of Management, makes an appeal for the assist- ance of ladies in its distribution, and would be glad to meet as many as possible at a special meeting to be held on Tuesday evening next at the Infirmary at 7.30 p.m., when it is hoped to get a sufficient number to carry out the dis. tributing arrangements.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>Pleasant Sunday Afternoon.</head>
        <p>Pleasant Sunday Afternoon.</p>
        <p>A good meeting of the Wycliffe P.S.A. held on Sunday was presided over by Mr. B. Farrar (the secretary); who read letters from Private J. T. Yeates (of Orford), who is still wounded and in Woburn Hospital, Bedfordshire, and from Mrs. Ashworth, in grateful acknowledg- ment of a letter of sympathy sent to her from the last meeting.—The Chairman also moved a vote of sincem sympathy with the Rev. John Yonge, with a strong hope of speedy recovery after his accident.—Mr. Holt gave an address upon "The Bible," and said he believed in God's Word, which stood, for all time and was applicable to us in all our experience.. It behoved us to be careful and earnest about our condition with God; as the tree fell so would it lie. So he that was filthy would remain filthy still; and he that was righteous, righteous still.—Messrs. McEachern and Delooze, also participated in the meeting, 'whilst Mr. Bromley presided at the organ.</p>
        <p>—The Chairnian said the catalogues would in ready for Sunday next,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>Transfer Sessions.</head>
        <p>Transfer Sessions.</p>
        <p>Transfer sessions were held at the Warring- ton Police Court on Monday, the Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock) presiding. There were eight transfers of hotel and beerhouse licence's. The list was as follows :—Beer, wine and spirits (off licence), 112 and 114, Battersby- lane, from Joseph Whitfield to Albert Thomas Clay; beer (off), 2, School-street, from. Charles Harrison to John Starkey; beer, wine and spirits (off), 211, Winwick-road, from John Lockett to John Wood; Midland Hotel, Win- wick-street, from Samuel Wakefield to Robert Walker; Mersey Inn (beerhouse), •Mersey- street, from Michael Burke (deceased) to Albert Prescott; Barley Mow, Market-place, from James Gleave (deceased) to Mary Gleave; Green Man (beerhouse), Powys-street, from Ann Kenyon to William Campbell; Bowling Green House, Liverpool-road, from Willi iw Campbell to James Holland. M. Arthur Browne, applying for the trar-- fer of the Midland Hotel and the Green Man. observed that it was a strange thing that tbat moriAng two old licensees—Mr. Wakefield and Mrs. ICenyon—should be giving up the'r licences. , Mr. Campbell was Mrs. Kenyan's brother.</p>
        <p>Alderman Bolton applied for permissior to make structural alterations to the premise; of the off•lieence, 363, Winwick-road. The altera- tions, he said, would make the house more at- tractive for modern business purpose.-,-The proposals were sanctioned. Mr. Ito Mind was formerly steward of the Conservative Club, Sankey-street, and Mr. Clay occupied a; like position at the Liberal' Club.</p>
        <p>The New Councillor. Councillor George Dale, on Tuesday evening, 00 book his seat for the first time as a memlkir, of the Council. 'Wesleyan Farewell Service. To-morrow, at the Bold-street Wesleyan Church, the morning preacher will be Mr. E- ll. Sutton, whilst in the evening tne Rey. Hugh Williamson will conduct his farewell service taking for his subject "The Triumphant Choir."</p>
        <p>Thelwall Military Hospital. Preliminary notice is given of a garden sale to be held on Saturday, September 18th, at Barnacre, Latchford Without (the residence of Mrs. C. Jackseit Holmes), in aid of the Thelvan Military Hospital.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>Chance for Connoisseurs.</head>
        <p>Chance for Connoisseurs.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday next, afternoon and evening, Messrs. A. H. Clive and Co. will sell by auction a valuable collection of oil paintings and water- colour drawings, to be on new the day prier to and morning of the sale. Little Girl Drowned.</p>
        <p>A little girl, named Ada Hodson, aged six years, of Liverpool-road, has been drowned ia the Sankey Canal. The canal was dragged, the child being missing, and suspected to have fallen intb the water. The body was found last night.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>War Fund Auction Sale.</head>
        <p>War Fund Auction Sale.</p>
        <p>The Mayor makes a direct and earnest appeal to all Warringtonians—and, of course, others to contribute at the Parr Hall between 10 8.133- and 4 p.m. anything which could be sold IoY auction and so converted into cash. Donors are requested to attach their name and address. together with a description. The object of this forthcoming sale is the assistance of many local war funds urgently needing help.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>Slump in Births and Deaths.</head>
        <p>Slump in Births and Deaths.</p>
        <p>In the week ended August 7th there were ifl Warrington 29 births against 49 in the corre- sponding week last yea and 13 deaths againSt 21 last year. Of the. 29 'births, 18 were males. Two of the deaths were from pneumonia, on• each from measles, scarlet fever, diarrhcea and cancer. There were 27 fresh cases of scarlet fever, making the total in hospital 115.</p>
        <p>"Come atfd Sign the King's Pledge!" Under the auspices of the Warrington Citi- zens' Temperance League a pledge signing campaign will take place on Friday next. Au open-air roosting is to be held in Bewsey-street at 7.30 p.m., under the chairmanship of Bre. W. Burrows, G.W.P.. of St. Helens; whilst an address will be delivered' by Bro. R. Mclntyre. (N.D. Organiser), of Manchester. The War- rington division of the Order of the Sons of Temperance hays charge of the meeting.</p>
        <p>Warrington Market. Wednesday.—Wheat firmer, with a slight advance, viz., 7a. id. to 79. 4d. per bushel of 701 b.; oats, 48. 4d. to 4s. 7d. per 451 b. There are fair prospects of a good corn harvest. Although the hog harvest has been light, the recent rains have increased the yield, and, given fine weather while the corn is harvested. there will be good results. Clover hay, ..t4 10e. to .24 15a per too; straw, 355. to 369. per ton; XX sour, tla. 61. per 2801 b.; best bran, is. 4d. ' per 201 b.</p>
        <p>Latchford Garden Society. The third annual flower show of the Latchford Garden Society will be held this afternoon - The Mayor and Mayoress will open the show at three c'elook. There will be selections aria entertainments by the Latchford Orchestral and Entertaining Society, and novel competitions in aid of the wounded soldiers at Raddon Oeurt Military Hospital. Visitors to the show will be able to obtain refreshments at reasonahio prices. The joint hon. secritaries are Messrs. George Hooper and 3'. S. Burgess.</p>
        <p>Ra-ddon Court Pound Day. The usual Raddon Court Pound Day was 'held on Thursday. The gifts, which totalled £6, in- cluded groceries, jam, fruit, vegetables, vas, stationery, cigarettes and matches. Cigaretfes and money were also received from the fon:eV/jug Cigarettes to the value of El as the result of a sale of scent bags by Misses Eva and Edith Phillips; El is. 3d. and El lls, lld, as a result of sale of lavender bags by Miss Lizzie Manual and Miss Eveline Leigh respectively; lea- obtained by the following ladies singing' Misses Jessie Morbury, Eveline Fraser, Ethel Holt. littp, Richardson, Vora .-Hough, 1 Bss- and Dora- Gerrard.</p>
        <p>Infirmary Musical Festival v, • The Working Men's Infirmary Committee ie on Sunday afternoon next holding , a musical Festival in Bank Park, in aid of the Warring- ton Infirmary and the fund for providing tom forts for the soldiers and sailors engaged is service at the Dardanelles. There will be a grand performance of Handel's oratorio "The Messiah."' to be given by the combined church choirs of all denominations in the town. Sortie talented soloists have been engaged, and the oratorio will be conducted, by Mr. W. if. Payton, Mus. Bac. In the evening a grand concert will be given in the Hippodrome by the band of the and South Lancs. Regt. With such a galaxy of talent and so worthy a cause to be helped, the festival should not need a second recommendation.</p>
        <p>Local Leather Trade. Writing in the current issue of the "Lathe' Trades Review," the Warrington correspondent says: "Trade last week was quieter in conse- quence of the absence of the usual callers, "ille apparently were making the most of the short holiday in places remote from leatherland• Apart from this the week was much as usual, as holidays here were strictly confined to Mon- day and the remainder of the week Provided quite the average amount of briskness. Although- many boot factories were closed, it was impera- tive that stock should be ready for the re- opening, so tanners were well employed getting out deliveries. Although the output was natur.: ally somewhat below, that in recent weeks, k.v made a fair showing. New business was, el• course, very limited, but with values now on 2 more permanent basis, the market will proh- ably be active as soon as trade gets into swing again. It is somewhat curious to note the opposite vraws people take of even so straight- forward an issue as the regulation of hide prices, and I find one journal expressing t,i. opinion that forcing these down to 101 d. WO .have a downward movement in leather value". It does seem as though it should have been obvious to all that the only reason behind the regulation of hide prices was the desire t. prevent leather values going to unworkable figures, and it is only because it seems possib/e to establish 101 d. as the maximum for natice hides that consumers of leather can have atY guarantee of being able to supply their recur,: ring requirements at somewhere about preset') , rates. As regards any downward tendency in !leather values, it should be'sufficient answer i• I point to the strength of the demand, and til I relative value of rair stocks 12. mentils to-day. • ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>DEAriSig4S AND BLINDNESS.</head>
        <p>DEAriSig4S AND BLINDNESS.</p>
        <p>Special Visit To 'Warrington, iltursdaY</p>
        <p>Next, August 19th. 4.41 Mr. Thomas Toon, the erainent oculist aurist, of Ison's Eye and Ear Dispensary, tht,7 Leeds, has arranged to visit the Lion Warrington, on Thursday ne-rt, August V"' from 11 to 2; Union Hotel, Liverpool, on Wt. day next, August lah, from 12 to 8; and 03: be consulted on all diseases of the eye and Or; Sufferers from failing eyesight and heario='; should avail themselves of the opportunity `7l,' consultation. Speeial home treatment. No e5O drums, ear cornets, no electric batteries, lid painful appliances. Spectacles for weak on defective sight. vr' , Those unable to avail thertle" selves of a per!sonal consultation should Id. for printed form of questions to be answ,c,,reg, sent free. Letters to 71, Great Georg-. Leeds.—{Advt.]</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>FANCIERS FOR . THE FRONT.</head>
        <p>FANCIERS FOR . THE FRONT.</p>
        <p>A- circular that has been issued by the N- 'West Centre of the National Homing states that the War Office has asked the 101° 'era te' to assist in the enlistment of sixty *mei • conduct the pigeon service for the ExPeditit:ed 4ev ary force, the arrangements to be ce''"' —, this week. The men, who will not h3veirec4 undergo any military training or he refilivoy- to fig ht, will act as loft managers and con.iod ett. Panciers will be enlisted for the Pe.' of Alie mixtr- in -di. Royal Engineers.</p>
        <p>orll</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
