      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>MORE CASUALTIES 1.-11 THE FOURTH BATTALION.</head>
        <p>MORE CASUALTIES 1.-11 THE FOURTH BATTALION.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Territoriale, after, their reat,, again appear to have entered the fighting toe, and this week the following additions hawia been made to the Roll of Honour of the Battalion 3-</p>
        <p>KILLED. Private Arthur Astall.</p>
        <p>WOUNDED. Lance-Corporal Harry Vic.,ls^ly. Private Thomas Weston_ Private John A. Riley. Private John Wright.</p>
        <p>. LAST NlGurs 'The following casualties were reported "tidally last night:—</p>
        <p>KILLED.</p>
        <p>nte- Corp° ra I Leah,--10, ifilner-street, War- rington. WOUNDED. Coiraoral G. CHM' 11, Batteraby-la.n3. iA Prte S. (or W.) Aspinall, 39. Clipreli-lane. Newton.</p>
        <p>Killed.</p>
        <p>Asti,ll served, and whose home is in Legh-street. lie said :---" It is my painful duty to have to ittforna you of the death of your son. Arthur, who was killed whilst the Company was out digging on the night of August 2nd. Ile met, his death instantly, and did not sutler one moment's pain. May God help and strengthen Yon to bear up in your sad trouble. Ile was a good lad and •a brave soldier, and w'll be sadly by all .his comrades ,in A. Company. _I c; all, 'tender to you our .‘deepest sympathy?' tgarle-rnaster-Ser 15 Yeant Albert 'Jeffs', sub- "c •- „ ftteuti Y wrote :- I am very sorry to inform You of • your son's death, which occurred on t 2, about 11 p.m. Ile was hit by- a stray whilst digging trenches some , distance t`°tri the .firing line. . . . It grieves me to .have El. write such. a letter to you Your on was a f°otl soldier, loved by his comrades; and he :Aght bravely for six months. Your son "e-red no pain, le was unconscious until he 4144.</p>
        <p>4 Was present at his burial, ai 10.30 on the Q-"ling of August 4. Expressing synipathy oti rh,alf of his many comrades, I remain. Sr.e."</p>
        <p>Participated In Great Charge.</p>
        <p>Private Astall, who Was twenty year., of age, ....71'49 formerly employed as a moulder at 31-essre. Whyman's Foundry Co.. Ltd. He was Ildlleated at, St. Benedict's School."and attended gt... Benedict's Church. He had been in the,* 'Territorials about eighteen months, and took part in the great charge on June 16th. when he i.ad several narrow escapes. He only returned la the Front about a month ago. after enjoying short leave tit home.</p>
        <p>WOUNDED.</p>
        <p>PRIVATE 1110. MAS WESTON.</p>
        <p>Mersey White Lead Worker And Old Sacred Heart Scholar.</p>
        <p>• - News has reached Mrs. Weston, of 58, Oldham. street, Latchford, that her son, Private Thomas 'Weston, of D Company of . the Warrington Territorials, was shot in the right shoulder on</p>
        <p>PRIVNTE T. WESTON. Wounojed.</p>
        <p>. • 413-gust 2nd. Writing to 'the, Private's mother °II behalf Of her 'eon, a comrade says: "Please '4O not worry; it is not. ft bad wound and 'I am Tting on all right. I got hit by a bullet in I right shoulder about 11 pm. yesterday, and 'lad the bullet taken out this morning, and 4"rYthing is 'a ring of roses.' '• 4,Artother letter was received by a friend of ' rivate Weston from Sergeant E. Widdocks, 14 hich read as follows:—" I am sorry he has ;,wounded, as he was one of my best ,orhi ti::.ne-gunners. Please aitcept my sympathy; ia • his comrades'. It won't be long before he In England?' .</p>
        <p>A Prize Bandsman. :rrivate Weston, who is 24 years of age. ceived his education at the sacred eart Wan :fellool, Bank Quay. Before war broke out, he iemployed by the Mersey White Lead Co., tk(l. ne formerly belonged to the League of V Cross Band, and later ta the Peketh aAllnel'Y Prize 134.1141.</p>
        <p>Private J. Charleston, 10, Princess-road, Aghlan-</p>
        <p>in-Makerfield. Private H. Hipkiss, 25, ,Foreliaw-street. Private T. B. Jackson, 09, Ashton-road, Oldham:</p>
        <p>PREVIOUSLY REPORTED WOUNDED, NOW WOUNDED AND MISSING.</p>
        <p>Private W. Bate, 73, Old.hara-etzeet. Private J. Brimelow, 22, Piekmere-street. Private W. Gibson, 5, Mill-street. Private G. Harding, 14, Manchester-row, Val-</p>
        <p>can, Earlestown. Private W. Holmes, 55, Hume-street. Private R. Taylor, 27, Leicester-street.</p>
        <p>TOTAL CASUALTIES. The total casualties in the Battalion now are:— Killed Missing Wounded 370</p>
        <p>Total</p>
        <p>461</p>
        <p>PRIVATE J. WRIGHT. Wounded.</p>
