      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>LANCE-CORPL. HARRY WELSBY.</head>
        <p>LANCE-CORPL. HARRY WELSBY.</p>
        <p>Struck With A Piece Of Exploding Shell.</p>
        <p>Lance-Corporal Harry Welsby, who Made his home with his brother-in-law and ,sister, Mr. and Mrs. R. Mather, at 33, Sharp-street, was wounded. on July 9th. He was one of a num- ber of men who .were struck with a piece of exploding shell. Lance-Corporal Welsby, who is ?A years of age, enlisted at the, beginning ,of September, having previously been an employe at Messrs. Rylande Brothers, Ltd. He is a member of</p>
        <p>LANCE-CORPORAL 11. WELSBY.</p>
        <p>Wounded</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <p>Wounded</p>
        <p>the head on July 10th. A letter was received on Wednesday morning to say that he has now almost recovered, and hopes shortly to 'rejoin his regiment in the trenches: Private Riley, who is 21 years of agfe, en- listed in the Territorials shortly after the out- break of war, having previously been employed by Messrs. W. Mortimer and ,Co., tanners, Orford. As a boy he atteadea the Silver- street School.</p>
        <p>PRIVATE JOHN WIIIGHT.</p>
        <p>tn Old Soldier Who Rejoined In The</p>
        <p>Terrilorials.</p>
        <p>News has reached• Mrs. Wright at 103, GiOrsey-laia‘, that her son, Private John</p>
        <p>44: •!F,'.. : .4.; :'4.,:w: ~i•,,,fa,:v,- . -e0,0t.1,' -,:.-- -'• - - ' ni-,,,-E.,,Met..1%-wf:Aic :•:.Z;;;..1,,.:•:-:,-,-. k.:'.:-10,4iR n. tiqa:' .:'. '--, t‘..:: ..-•;,;*i..;:.'.•,:. -. ...,1*.00,,F1414,0pg.A1,20, •;:';AI. 4.0 4i-0cl; .AP.tZ:.'..iiV;;;,:tiMALltra IRV&amp; , ......-,:.... t.•-• , -... 1.,,,:bart,-e..:,....,., :... s :f.....:4.13.:....,;: ... , , • • ~. • • .p 7 . - ..„, `:, .: .. k*: -..... 7,„.i.a....*, ..,:- 'O.-.: mri. ,' . . 4 •.:." -,...,-.:::-.. ••,-. .q . ; :---'.- iti,,,,,44,0* -• . ,:,,..,,,,, ... . ~:.,,.. - ~.....: -.„: it.:-igk0V.A..,:...:,,, - :..-,:.' --,-,,,,t...;•-. .: . :.-. ---.4 -:,.,....,. ~,••••••,;,•,:f•,,:,....A.1.1f;;-,.-4,%N:44, v.:44'.- --..•.t._,-.V..•,';..;- M.•::.44.•.,;:::„.;.; r, m'4.1.-.••At -W.I:::-''. -'— -, . --',A,:-.:.:•- -,..mV 1.,,y.'.....Q....;.k. &lt; A &gt;:',:i:f.. i..: 4%,,,,,,,:•••• ..' 4.- ,is •• ' 1,10 F.-:#.•-.4 p 40.-: ~:g. .1.40.; - • :..• -k, ~' --,. ••••• :•,,*•,4 -• 4:.:,tt-nitk.A.„...)i• . .: - '..'..- - 04:;4004;m2% 4'41:-.f,Az',:!•'et!':),.‘ AC-•., • '`'' - . .."; . 0,444501,4,M5, ;, :•..Tile.:":-..:.4 V. :•.'....‘..:.. .. . ....4,.-"•-. ri.f...; i,,A).4 .:' ;..."'•::..7 .-.‘,F:]..,,,`;'-,:- '''A:' • ' ..., oi'Ag: .: 5i.,)4.,4:1;(.., hi:- ~...e.' ~..4.4:,;:i4,1i,;-;:i-!ii i',.,.ii%1.4...... -..„t•-.)!Vl'.:' ..-g4k,':"''':':::::ii ..•:1:311,'.4.';;4...i:.47,..t.V,T'AV11PA,,,....,..*:414;44' t '''::*l.g•*''',l:g:,...:flAttl..*,•:adAllTti:::';',%! t iNc.s.zei..'-;:k.:,:,...:.-4::::t -.-, ..,..•••,,,,,,ii."-:.** : • .4t454,.;V Al:14-2:-.4x,4704. ,:;..„.:::•;: .g.•-,A-AAP.,:?,:t;°,.&lt;4.2!'...:,'.::. ,:-.i.d;;',4.2,f'. t'l •,i.-4 r ~,,ii.,:-Ttii::4.45,;:,:., ••. - s',-,,,A1Aii41:. .40* If.-' 4. •:', V-2.44...,..,ittw0kt,,tte, v,.. 4,::,010,,,.0.4:,,:]:5aief,,..,NTw,ip:. 0 ~,...$l-itg: qv.5,... vt.47ilkii. z „4 ~.... ~:-.-.1'J.i.,,..0-,- **,,,A.,.15...,..:04,.:' .1:..er,:!!..:',, .: •,:•;,:.!NtAilsi •-i,* 14;2,4,4, _.,:;.•-;„ --,..,,...z,71 .„,..„:„...</p>
        <p>PRIVATE j. A. RILEY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <p>Wright, has been wounded in the shoulder by a bullet. Later,. n letter W 23 received basizg</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>that he is in Edmonton Hospital, London:</head>
        <p>that he is in Edmonton Hospital, London:</p>
        <p>Private Wright, who is 26 years of age, had previously served in the ranks, and upon the outbreak of war he rejoined in the Terri- torials. He was formerly an employe of Messrs. Rylands Bros., Ltd., and as a boy, he attended the Idamilton-street School.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>LATE PRIVATE LAKE. Letters From The Chaplain And Medical Officer.</head>
        <p>THE LATE PRIVATE LAKE. Letters From The Chaplain And Medical Officer.</p>
        <p>Mr. and Mrs. Richard W. Lake, of 67, Cum- berland-street, Latchford, whose son, Private George William Lake, of the 2/4th Battalion South Lancashire Regiment, died of wounds in France on July 14th—as we fully reported in a</p>
        <p>_ previous issue—have received two letters of sympathy from the Front. One is from Arch- deacon H. Southwell, chaplain to the Forces, and runs as follows:—"I am afraid you have not received my letter in which I informed you of the sad news of the, death of your son, George. His wound was in the leg, and so serious that he died soon after I wrote my first letter to you. I laid him to rest in .our, cemetery here, and have placed a cross on his grave. Please accept my most sincere sym- pathy with you all."</p>
        <p>Died a Soldier's Death.</p>
        <p>The second letter was from Lieutenant Chas. D. Law, medical officer to the 4th South Lanca- shire Regiment, which ran :—" Will you please accept my very sincere symptthy in the loss of your son? lam very sorry that the wound which your son received has proved fatal. He was brought to me immediately after he had been hit, and we made him as comfortable as it was possible to do before sending him away to the Field Ambulance Hospital. I knew your eon very well, and when he was in the.dressing station he referred to the work he had done on the stage in connection with the amateur per- formances I had been associated with at the Royal Court Theatre. It is very sad that- you should have lost your eon so soon after he had arrived• at the Front, but you have the know- ledge that he died in a great cause, and had proved how genuine were his wishes to fight for his King and country. I hope that the fact that your son met his death as a soldier will lessen a little your sorrow and will help you to bear your loss."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>MEN INVALIDED FROM</head>
        <p>MEN INVALIDED FROM</p>
        <p>OVERSEAS.</p>
        <p>Correct Procedure In Case Of</p>
        <p>Continued Unfitness.</p>
        <p>To the Editor of "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>Sir,—May I ask your assistance in regard to informing men of the 1/4th South Lancashire Regiment, invalided from overseas of the action they must take in the event of their not being fit to rejoin their depot unit at the Drill Hall, Warrington, on the expiry of their fur- lough? The instructions are so clear if they are fit that there is no need to explain them.</p>
        <p>May I point out at the beginning, that under no circumstances may an. extension of fur- lough be granted by anyone. In the event -of any man forwarding to the Officer Command- ing, Administrative Centre, Drill Hall, War- rington, a civil medical practitioner's 'cer- tificate recommending that he is ,not fit to re- join, arrangements will be made by the officer mentioned for the man's readmission to a military hospital. This certificate must reach the Drill Hall on the morning the man is due to report, and if the man is capable of travel- ling, must be brought by him in person. It must be clearly understood, however, that if a soldier does not report on the day on which he is due, or a medical certificate as men- tioned is not received, then the soldier renders himself liable to the full military penalty for absence without leave. • - .</p>
        <p>I trust that the foregoing remarks are plain, and that the same mistakes which have occurred in the past will not take place in the future. May I also add that the Military Authorities wish to that unfit soldiers with every consideration, but their efforts are nullified by the soldier being ignorant of, or wilfully disregarding, the. corrept procedure. • I have the- honour to. be,- Sir, Your obedient servant. F. R. MONKS, Major, Commanding 3/4th South Lancs. Regt. Weethn Camp, 11/8115.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>DISABLED IN THE WAR.</head>
        <p>DISABLED IN THE WAR.</p>
        <p>Return Of Allowances Paid To Dependents Of Employes. The General Purposes Committee reported at TuesdaY's 'meeting of' the Town Council that the Town Clerk had submitted a return obtained from various towns of allowances paid to the dependents of employes killed and to employes disabled in the war, and that it had been resolved. (1) that payments under the :resolutions of 'the Council of the Ist Septem- ber and the 6th October, 1914, to the person Or persons appointed by married employes whilst serying with the colours be continued in the case of death or presumed death of the latter for a period •of six months, or until such time (if previous to the expiration of six months) as any Government pension to, such person or .persons commences; (2) that pay- ments made by the Council to the aepen4ents or nominees of unmarried emplo:css who have died, or whose death has been presumed, be discontinued from the date of the rifficial noti- fication, except in cases where the dependency is of a responsible nature, when the allowance paid by the Corporation shall, at the discre- tion of the committee concerned, be continued for a period not exceeding six month, or until such time (if previous to the expiration of six months) as any Government pension to such person or persons commences; (3) that in all cases where the committees concerned are satisfied that it would not be in the interests of the public service that employes who have been incapacitated and discharged as unfit for military service should resume their former duties under, the Corporation, the in 6 shall receive, for a period not exceeding six months from the date of their discharge, the allow- ance formerly paid by the Corporation in addi- tion to any disablement allowance which may he paid to them by the Government, provid- ing that the total amount received does not in any case exceed the amount of the wages</p>
        <p>earned prior to the outbreak of war and the war bonus granted by the Council; (4) that all questions arising under the' above resolutions be referred to the Advisory Committee sitting at the Parr Hall. The Council approved of the recommenda- tions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>FIVE BROTHERS SERVING.</head>
        <p>FIVE BROTHERS SERVING.</p>
        <p>Proud ilecord Of An Old Wycliffe Family.</p>
        <p>Five sons in the family and all serving in the, Warrington Territorials ! Such is the proud record of the Smith family, of 18, Ellen-street. All of these patriotic sons are married. John, who is the eldest, is 35 years of age, and lives in peace time with his wife and family. at 18, Ellis- street. Before the war he was employed as a furnaceman at Bewsey Forge, and, like all his other brothers, was educated at Wycliffe School.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>One Who Was Wounded.</head>
        <p>One Who Was Wounded.</p>
        <p>Fred, who is 28 years of age, was also formerly employed at the liewsey Forge. He rejoined the Territorials at the war outbreak, having served in them. already for several years. Now he lies inlEindell.streetllilitary Hospital, London—for he has been wounded.</p>
        <p>Joe, who lives at Howard:street, has also been wounded, and is in hospital near BlackSOol. He formerly worked for Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Sons.</p>
        <p>An Ocidfellow.</p>
        <p>William, who took part in the big bayonet charge on June 16th, is a member of the Ancient Order of Ocldfellows. He was formerly employed in the mills at Bewsey Forge. He also had served the necessary time, but rejoined at the outbreak. In peace time he makes his home with his wife and only child at 24, Forster-street. Albert, who is the only one not yet at the Front, lives at 79, Hoyle-street. He was em- ployed before the war by the London and North- Western Railway Company, and is now in train- ;lug in Haat&amp;</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>"HE WAS A MAN."</head>
        <p>"HE WAS A MAN."</p>
        <p>How A Soldier Fell On The Plains Of Flanders.</p>
        <p>"YOUNG ARTHUR."</p>
        <p>Then let me like a soldier fall</p>
        <p>Upon some open plain."</p>
        <p>There - are moments when these words from the famous opera thrill one as nothing else can thrill. Such a feeling is engendered by the following stirring letter from a Warrington warrior at present at Hooge. We are in possession of the partiCulars• as to his name and regiment, &amp;e., but, ut his request, 'we are not publishing them. Here is the letter:—</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>Grunt, a Curse, or a Quiet Laugh.</head>
        <p>A Grunt, a Curse, or a Quiet Laugh.</p>
        <p>We were improving a communication trench somewhere in Flanders) time 11 o'clock on the night of August 2nd, and a dirty night at that. Sufficient rain had fallen to make the ground we marched over, with shell holes here and shell holes there and almost everywhere torzt up by traffic—well—not quite pleasant. -Moving along in the dark, twisting and turning like a huge snake, we at last filed into the trench and each took up his task in almost complete silence. How difficigt this is can scarcely, perhaps, be appreciated by our friends at home, but how much depends upon it! To march to a job under cover of darkness, carry it through, and withdraw- without being seen or heard. But</p>
        <p>if discovered! Shells! And• much sadness and grief at home. To resume. We had got going nicely, each of 'us doing, his hit, only stopping to hide his face when a light was sent up, and. greettl.g astray rifle bullet whistling uncomfortably near "to him, with a grunt or a curse, or maybe a quiet laugh. Through the darkness the message was sent: "Man hit, stretcher bearers." As the word passed along there was a slight pause and numerous queries: "Who is it?" "Is it much?" "He's not dead?" Feeling and opinions are expressed very briefly "What Your Astall P" "Hard lines" "Has he any young Astall?" "Hard lin‘s!" "Has he any mother?" " good job he's• not married, anyhow" and so on. . . . •He "got it" in the head and didn't last long—perhaps half an hour and, thank God, unconscious all the while. - The Boy Who Was a Man.</p>
        <p>Just a stray bullet, stopped while he was helping to fill a bag with sand. Nothing glorious or blood thrilling about such work, you may think. But wait. Along that crooked line of trenches, •which runs across France and' Belgium, there are millions o? men watching carefully, day and night, the trenches of the enemy and the ground between. By means of the communication trenehes, running to them, these men are enable 4 to get in and out in more or less safety. Otherwise they would have to cross the open country—need I say more? And Astall's dead. We grieve at all losses and dis- Cover all our late sham's good qualities, for- getting the others... . . But Arthur Astall, or "Young Arthur" as we called him, was different, you see; foi one thing, most of us are big raw-boned fellows, an all of us, almost with- out exception, could be spoken of as "men," so far as physical appearances go. But *Arthur—in build and appearance gener- ally, was—well, boyish. He told me on hir honour, but a few ,weeks ago, and I believe him, that he was 21, But I am afraid most of his pals rather thought he, had made a mistake. In height he would perhaps be sft. 6in. and very slightly built, weighing perhaps 8 stone. His face made him lbok young, especially when he smiled, which was frequently. Then he looked quite a child. _</p>
        <p>When his company; old B as4t is called, were on detachment at Appledore (England) he is said to have gone to a door ,(I think, for a drink, but am not sure). An elderly matron answered it and on seeing Arthur in khaki, exclaimed: "Why, my little boy, whatever are you doing here with all these men?" But he was a man. One of our poets (Swin- burne, I think) has said: " A man is to be judged, not so much by what he achieves as by what he attempt,s apd the spirit in which he makes the attempt.' Physically, Arthur was, well, not strong, and time after time broke down, but at all times, whether things were rough or smooth, a smile was never far from his face, a smile that did us good. _ _ .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>"He Gave His Life For Britain."</head>
        <p>"He Gave His Life For Britain."</p>
        <p>But now he's gone; at present his body is lying in a "dug-out" under my note-book. We are all glad that we shall at least know where he lies, for our Quartermaster will fix it up all right and he will rest along with several of our men in --. ' I repeat—he was a man; a great spirit in a frail body. He has given:. his life, not for Belgium, nor for France, but for Britain, for Warrington. Filling a sand-bag to protect a fellow-soldier on his way to or from his work. We are all proud of him, and when we think of him we feel ashamed—ashamed that we don't do better.</p>
        <p>- A week or two ago I was in England, on leave, for five days. During that time I con- versed with and questioned a number of young men, splendid fellows physically, and I was staggered at some of the reasons given for them not having joined; and I thought .of "Young Arthur," and sighed. Astall, whom any of these men would think it below their dignity to fight, he was BO small, laying down his life (to take it up again, please God), and big strapping ohaps still hanging back. "Are You Afraid of Death?"</p>
        <p>Men of Warrington, those of you who really can be spared, come along and help! There's heaps of room. Are you afraid of death? So are we; but fear is only one of the elements in the make-up of a man, and we only "go out" once, and I daresay this dear old earth of ours will not falter in its, path even if Ive do "go out." But, above all, we have a Cause. We are Right. Come out here and see. Remember "Young Arthur."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>WANTED, TERRITORIALS</head>
        <p>WANTED, TERRITORIALS</p>
        <p>Furlifer tall To The Nation's Manhood.</p>
        <p>To the Editor of " The Examiner."</p>
        <p>Sir,—Few 'men who, are thoughtful of , the welfare of their country can fail to understand' that the manhood of the nation is under a ,greater demand than at any time since war burst upon us a year ago to exert all its energy and strength, not merely to carry our arms to victory, but to stave 'off disaster ,and ruin from our homes. The need for men is as great as ever, and to those who would answer' that Warrington has done well, the reply iat"Warirngton cannot do too much." The 4th South Lancashire Regiment has one .battalion at the Front, doing its duty nobly, another in Kent waiting for the word t 3 go.. It is for ,the men of Warrington to say that 'word by enlisting now in the 3/4th South Lancashire Regiment, ,statiOned at Weefon, and creating the reserves that will release the 2/4th Battalion for active service. To release a man for service abroad is as grand a thing in its own way as to go abroad oneself, and while a man may not pass the piedical test for foreign 'ser- vice, he may still be accepted for service at home iota such time as he may become fit for foreign service. No man, therefore, has answered his country's call who is not actively engaged on war work if he has not presented himself for enlistment for such service as he may be fit to undertake. We fight for the soul and soil of England', whether the soil upon which we take our stand be the soil of France or of our homeland. The honour .of fighting for King and country is thia same wherever our duty lies, and I would adsoks?noosew.who have not yet joined tle Army to, The Drill H itiall is open for recruits °in 9 a.m. to 9 p.m. every day, and to those who do not know the opportunities and conditions of ser- vice, full information will gladly be given.— Yours, &amp;c.,</p>
        <p>R. M. MATHAMS, Captain, Commanding Administrative Centre, • 4th South Lancashire R,egimen Drill Hall, Warrington,. August 9, 1915.</p>
        <p>Killed.</p>
        <p>I did not know him quite so well as some of the others in my company, but from what I. Faw of him- he had all the makings of a good soldi•?r, and we are all extremely grieved to have lost him:" .</p>
        <p>Private Williams was an only son. He was 18 years of age, and at the time of his joining the Army in August last year was employed by Messrs. Fletcher, Russell and Co. He attended Holy Trinity Church, and, as a lad, Arpley- street School.</p>
        <p>The last letter which Private Williams wrote home bore the date August 2nd, and ran as follows :--" I received your welcome letter yes- terday, but I am sorry to say the parcels have not turned up yet. . . . . We had a poor August Monday here, compared with last year. I have received that parcel of Woodbines,' matches and writing paper, but that is all the pareela I have received."-</p>
        <p>~.., .... ..,,,,,i.elfkitatittlifillilfo . '4,..iitilikfilit*llikiltitifillifital iiintlfralAtlaligii*lfilliki4,7:.:::4. :;-.,:.*.1.:-,„;.::K4,.,..,--,c&amp;tf0,:•.,..,,,:,..:',..,••:' , ,Ivi;4".gs'le,z,:t.':.k •....:,.,,.;:,.:•:::-,,,m;..:,.:A:4;:t:',:::„:::.,,:,•:•-•,•-•,'• . :,;.::,.444,,,,,,,,,,,,,0.0::- 4.4 z,.: ...:, A„i.:::::::,.,A,o•:siit,,qmoiii*. .::,• ' ' . .-‘47.,':gr0z,1'1A.:::.:,-..., tileO'.l4iioWok.'44 '' ,:,, - • , - ..4::l4,':!:•:,,t&gt;l:i..K,•'_kAttc,•o Rkat..:k ...'''' ,1 . , ?'•:i•ir•::rlVl4,..';',l;#';Nl!-*41.4,,,4'k,' „,„„..,,-.:.,:P0,41.''''.r I L. '.4 I*.'4Af:;:".ik.4• 'At' •,-,-..:. .....i: ....:-, :-...,:::•0#*;41,5'. ts,%,- • ... ri‘44oliToWeaL'..: IR.g"''' ..;. ...:4'..1,:.'•':',...7 rflitv..g .. ~-.. 044:iiekrni.7tRctiitzi...-.7.:. ~. ~,,A4,64.... ~.. ~.7, ~;*!.'...7:(itiii*.i.,:::::i.-..:,:; :,...„:-.. s i. 'i1.'.0..4.,;..44 . :,...I,Simikiii:::..,,.., ii)ilk::::..44 . tzati1imp.A.44.4,..:11;:1...,*„,...'::...,A...*** It4l/4 Loottili„ip...,4kt.,„::,.:.‘";:ii:LApt-„, ..7,..-0,it.,„,_::::?,;izigzk.37,0.;„,,,..-,:mt:::; •:E?,44i,itm•tgiti,...::•:,.Ntv,,;,.1.::-,-:A:s:tg:im::..s,.,,, .. ~. '..,,,,..-:,..:",.....:•.:,.,..1;,„:•,,t0:,,,;::4;rakriii.,,.,.4,:i,,,:,....-.,,,„,L...-4-;...4:..,0;,;-,.,.,,,.•,.•,. :-,,-•.:i.:70-4.,,„,,.-7,..:-,.,..,.;.:,-q!,,,,,7:liiii.g*,....,ik,viiiii?.- . ...„...... .., .. . .... "...,,•:„..i,....,.1.;,::,,,," •.......,:::,,::::.,:m4.44..,,,..:,,,...6.•••‘ ~.. ...„,,,,,,„.,:•:-....,.:?:4'.a5:5:'..,0'44A*,,.'... , ~. ....,.*,,, ~:„4:,„.,.,„, .:&gt;. • 0 ::-..,,q411:ii.:t'''•'..',..&lt; - :::::il: 7::01k!',,' - •</p>
        <p>PRIVATE J. D. WILLIAMS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>ON HONOUR'S SCROLL.</head>
        <p>ON HONOUR'S SCROLL.</p>
        <p>KILLED.</p>
        <p>PTE. J. J. MILLINGTON.</p>
        <p>Comrade Writes That He Saw Him k Lying Dead.</p>
        <p>" To my pal's dearest parents,—Just a few lines to express my deepest .sympathy with you in the loss you have sustained by the death of my chum lohn, your son. I know it will be a great shock to you and to his friends. I can hardly find words to express ray own sorrow. I was one of his workmates and best pals. His death occurred during the bombardment of our trenches on Sunday, August Ist. I had the opportunity of obtain- ing his Bible and shoulder badges, which I will forward on at my first opportunity. His other things were sent to headquarters. He has been laid to rest in a neat grave in the</p>
        <p>PRIVATE J. J. MILLINGTON. • - Killed.</p>
        <p>churchyard at St. Eloi, and a cross will be erected in remembrance of him. , No truer words were ever spoken than those contained in the little text 'ln the midst of life we are in death.' I ask you to accept this note of sympathy and to believe that 1</p>
        <p>share your sorrow." ' The foregoing letter, written by Private L. Roberts, 11525, A Company, 2nd Battalion South Lancs., and dated August 6th, conveyed to Mrs. Clara Millington the news that her son, Private John James Millington, of the same regiment, had given his life in the service of his country. Mrs. Millington is a widow, and carries on a small business at 46, Grafton- street, off Lovely-lane. This is the second heavy bereavement Mrs. Millington has sus- tained in the short space of a few months, her husband having died a few hours after her son left for the Front.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>Popular Crosfield Employe.</head>
        <p>A Popular Crosfield Employe.</p>
        <p>Private Millington was only 1P years of age. He was highly spoken of by all who knew him, and enjoyed much popularity among the young people of the district in which he lived. He was educated at St. Barnabas' Day School, and was a regular—attendant at the Sunday School and Bible Class held in connection with the church of the parish in which he spent his boyhood. After leaving school he went to work at Messrs. Crosfield and Son's soap works, being engaged in the box room up to the time he enlieed in the 2nd Smith Lancs. on August 29th last year.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Millington also received tidings of the death of her son through the medium of a letter written by a comrade to a Mrs. Armstrong, of Liverpool. - The deceased Private became acquainted with this lady whilst undergoing his training in Liverpool, and the letter - received by her stated that the writer missed young Millington from the trench and went to look for him. He found him lying ' dead on a stretcher. Neither of the letters received by Mrs. Millington states the nature of the fatal hurts. . ,„</p>
        <p>i memorial service to the late Private Millington will be held at St. Barnabas' Church to-morrow evening at 6.30. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>PRIVATE J. D. WILLIAMS.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE J. D. WILLIAMS.</p>
        <p>A Promising Lad Of Eighteen Years.</p>
        <p>Private John Dutton Williams, of the 3rd Battalion King's Own Scottish Borderers, whose home was with his parents,. Mr. and Mrs. Chas. Robert Williams, at 27, Fletcher-street, Wilders- pool-causeway, Warringt4n, has been killed in action. The intimation came through in the following letter written to the parents by Major R. H. Maxwell : " It is with extreme regret that I write to you to inform you of the, death in action of your son, Private John Dutton Williams. lam glad to say, however, that his death wa's almost in- stantaneous, and that he suffered no pain at all. He was killed at 7.30 this morning (August 4) by a bullet in his head. As he only came out here last month with a draft prom the 3rd Battalion,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>PRIVATE JACK BECKETT.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE JACK BECKETT.</p>
        <p>"Greater Love llath No Man Than This."</p>
        <p>Private Jack Beckett, of the 87th Battery Royal Field Artillery, has been killed in action. News to this ,effect reached Mrs.- Lloyd, 36, Wakefield-street (who was an inti- mate friend of Private Beckett) through the Medium of a letter written under date August 6th by S.Q.M.S. Parsons, of the 87th Battery. "I have no doubt," ran the letter, "that you will be surprised to hear from me. It is not really a pleasant task which I have to per- form, and I am afraid it will cause you a lot of sorrow and sadness. But you must - try and bear up, and be brave. Greater love hath no man than that he lay down his life for his friends.' That's what your friend- has done. I feel for you all and know what you must feel. He was killed in action at at least he died in hospital shortly -after .re- ceiring the fatal wound at about 7.30 this morning. He was buried this afternoon at 4 o'clock just behind the hospital in conse- crated ground, and the place is called between and -, not far from the main road. It may interest you to know that Beckett was very popular amongst his asso- ciates, and was a very good soldier. He had been with the battery since leaving 'Kilkenny and had come through without a scratch. Now he, poor fellow, has gone. I am sue we all sympathise with you, his friends, in your sad bereavement. This is a terrible affair, but, brave lad that he was, he used to make light of it, and was always ready with a word of cheer to his comrades. He will be greatly missed by all who have come'in con- tact with him. I hope in your deep affliction it may be some consolation to you to know that he died like a soldier, fighting for all- we held dear, and was buried by, a clergyman of his own religion. And thus ends the career of poor Beckett out here in Flanders. We shall see that there is a suitable emblem to mark his last resting-place. , But, dear friends, we believe that his soul shall live on for ever and ever."</p>
        <p>Enjoyed Mr. Mountield's Articles. The last letter which Private Beckett wrote was one in which he heartily thanked Mrs. Lloyd for sending him a parcel containing food and also the Editor c)f "The Examiner" for sending him a quantity of writing, material, The last letter written by Private Beckett and found in his kit has been forwarded to us by a comrade—Driver F. Willimott, of the same battery. It was addressed to the Editor of "The Examiner," and wished our interesting paper every prosperity. . "the Bobbers," he added, referring to a recent article by Mr. Arthur Mounfield, "'Was a very interesting piece and afforded a hearty laugh as I passed The Examiner' round my battery."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>PRIVATE ERNEST BENNETT.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE ERNEST BENNETT.</p>
        <p>Denies In A Letter The Report That Ile Had Been Killed. An official report received on Sunday from the War Office by Mr. 'Bennett, of 75, Pier- point-street, Bewsey, conveyed the news that his son, Private Ernest Bennett, of the oth Battalion South Lancashire Regiment, had been killed in action on the 16th inst. at the Dardanelles. Later, however, a letter was received from Private Bennett ,himself, writ- ten under' date July 18 —two days after he was said to have been killed. The letter ran as follows :—" Just a few lines to let you know I am in the best of health. I was very glad -to get your letter. . . . . Please semi me some 'Woodbines,' for they would be more in my line. We have just come out of the firingTline, after being in for a week. We have had a few casual- ties, but I have had the luck to keep out So far. Don't forget to send me 'The 'Examiner,' and remember me to all my friends." enne</p>
        <p>Privw is 31</p>
        <p>~ who years of age,</p>
        <p>PTE. E. BENNETT.</p>
        <p>worked at the Bewsey Wire Ropery for seven- men years. As a bog, he attended the Wycliffe School. At one time he was a member of the St. Paul's Young Men's Bible Class. He enlisted shortly after the outbreak of war, and is a member 01" D" Company. He has been at the Dardanelles about two months. An- other brother, who is in the R.A.M.C., is also serving there.</p>
        <p>„,. 1,44. W • `4`4' *ink otAW -111"1' 44* r 440451. I 'v;isso't roft</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <p>Wounded.</p>
