        <p>STOKER A. W. •CROOKS.</p>
        <p>40.44.401 t,</p>
        <p>„</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <p>few days' time. I have just had a couple of "'Warrington Examiners" given to me by a Warrington 'chap, who is in hospital, whose name is Tom Clare. There are four of us from Crosfield's in here. We have had a trip around the ward looking for Warrington lads.</p>
        <p>Injured Eye. "I have had an ulcer in the eye, caused by a shell bursting in front of our Maxim gun. We were doing a little too much damage for We have had our gun "nit out of action twice. I was firing the gun once, when the Turks came out and were attacking us, so we put the gun out of action that time, but we picked up rifles and let them have it. They retreated all right, with only about half the men with which they came out. "It was like shooting rabbits. They always ' seem to have a lot of bad luck. If they show their heads in the trenches they pet souvenirs</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <p>in them, while if we attuelt them witia'the bayonet they run and drop t,rerything. "Those who cannot get away put up fteir hands and shout Allah! Allah!, which is the name of their god."</p>
        <p>First-Class Stoker Crooks, who ig twenty-rix years of age, Was employed in peace times .by Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Son's, Lid. As a. boy he attended the Heathside and Peoples, College Schools. He is very fond of hozing. One of his cousins is setting with the 4Z-jesn'tt Greys Regiment:</p>
        <p>"A LUCKY CHAP."</p>
        <p>Three Inches Of_Shrapnei Taken fl-env-</p>
        <p>His Side. A.B. Thomas *Clare, a Naval Itoservist, wI was employed at Crosfield's, and who unite • wounded aboard one of His Maiesty's shipi in the Dardanelles in his 32nd aotion age-instAAbe- Turks„hae written to his sistett, Mrs. •Stitilitin::' 14, Sharp-street, off Orford-lane, from hesPitul: at Malta, as follows: " A line to thank you very =inch for the • Examiners,' which I received quite safe.ly hospital. lam glad to ipferin Yoq- iPiWation proved suec'essful, and I am alsVldY to say that lam improving grandly. The.</p>
        <p>wound is healing splendidly, but I have suffered great pains indeed. I am glad, however, that I am' alive, for the doctor said I was a luicky chap. I had to go under the operation in order that they could extract the contents of the shell. They took a piece of shrapnel shell from My side nearly three inches big. . " I was wounded on the 9th of July, and taken to hospital on the 10th, and undevarent the operation on the 12th. Only three „clays elapsed from the time I was hit until I .was operated upon, so you oan guess what Ernart work it was. We -can thank our elever and kind doctors and nurses for it all, for if it wasn't for them God only knows what we would do. They are the means of saving over half the lives of our sick and wounded. They look ' after us just like little babies. I don't l.low whether they will send me home or not ar I am discharges] from hospital, for I am so for from home—over 3,000 miles, and we are sa busy fighting this great battle which is mil* up the Dardanelles. I don't mind so repels, for I shall be glad to get back to my old 414. gun again, because I want my revenge. Praise From The French.</p>
        <p>"4.130 you remember me telling you about that French battleship, the Bouvet, going down ix four minutes after striking a mine? Well, 1 have been mentioned in despatches for my bravery. I went to their rescue in our life- boat, and we rescued 49 of her crew under heavy fire. The French Admiral has forwarded our names and full description to the Freneh Government for recognition of our bravery, so I am now awaiting the result. . . . I am iont lof bed to-day, which is the first time for btenty- two days, but 1 am a little weak yet."</p>
