        <p>A.B. THOMAS CLARE. Mentioned in French dispatches.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>VICAR OF BURTONWOOD'S NEPHEW KILLED.</head>
        <p>VICAR OF BURTONWOOD'S NEPHEW KILLED.</p>
        <p>Thomas Hammond, of the Australian ens- tingent, nephew of the Vicar of Burton.wood (the Rev. A. M. Mitchell), died of wounds in hospital at Valetta, July 24th. Ho was badly wounded at Gallipoli. The left leg was ampu- tated, but too late to save his young life—ho would have been 21 on Thursday this week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>FELL INTO A SHELL-HOLE.</head>
        <p>FELL INTO A SHELL-HOLE.</p>
        <p>Padgate Football Club Secretary's Hard</p>
        <p>Luck.</p>
        <p>Private William Gerrard Hodkinsea, of Sunnyside, Orford Green, has written te mother, informing her he sustained a fra-otnred foot and a sprained back through fulling Into a "Jack Johnson" shell hole, and has now been transferred to the Castle Hospital, Dublin. Private Hodkinson, who was employed as a clerk at Messrs. Pearson and Knowles', enlisted in the R.F.A. soon after the declara- tion of war, and left for the Front in 'Tuve last. He is well-known and highly respected in Orford. lie was hon. secretary of the Padgate Football Club, and a prominent member if am Warrington Junior ConserTatiy• Associatlaia.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>TOMBS, V.C.</head>
        <p>TOMBS, V.C.</p>
        <p>Decorated By His Majesty The King.</p>
        <p>On Thursday three men who hare won the coveted V.C. were decorated by Ilia Ack)esty the Bing at Buckingham Palace. These men were:—</p>
        <p>Lance-Corporal Tombs,' Hewley-lans, War- rington, of the Ist Liverpools, who at Rue ea Bois on May 16th crawled out under heavy Title fire to rescue four men, lassooing one with his rifle sling and dragging him along into sa4., v. Private May, Scottish Riles, who, on Ot&amp;ber 22nd, at La Bouteillere, attempted to roue a wounded man and got him out of the firing zone, but unfortunately the man was killed by enemy fire. On the same day, however, ling ,rescued an officer, whom he earned for a Jita.. tance of 300 yards.</p>
        <p>Private Mariner, King's Royal Rifles, Who, according to the official reeord of the deed for which he was honoured, remained in a "ynogt dangerous position for an hour and a half dur- ing his single-handed attack of the Gollnan trench, where he threw bombs at the enemy.</p>
        <p>The King, who was in khaki, gave cash-man a hearty handshake, pinned on the decoration in each case, and in his own gracious way-Daid tribute to the gallant eonduct which had led to the'award of the soreted cross. lb ex- pressed a wish for their good health and long life.</p>
        <p>Upon leaving the Palace the three men, who were accompanied by friends, were received with great enthusiasm by a large crowd of people. They seem to have been a little Wien aback at the demonstration awaiting them, and, being modest heroes, took refuge from admirers in the first taxicab that came along and hastened away. They had already removed the medals from their breasts and placed them in the neat eases provided for their safe keeping. One of the officers decorated by the King 'had a particularly fine record, having in the course of twelve months obtained a commission from the ranks, being mentioned in despatches, and awarded two military decorations.</p>
        <p>Among those who received the Distinguished Service Order was Major E. M. Beall, King's (Liverpool) Regiment. Lieutenant-General Sir James Willcoclre was invested with the insignia of a Knight Grand Cross of St. Michael and St. George.</p>
        <p>Major-General Aylmer Hnnter-Weston kao conferred upon him the honour of knighthood. and was invested with the insignia of the Knight Commander of the Bath, military aka.</p>
      </div>
