      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <p>. • ; -</p>
        <p>A WATER QUESTION.</p>
        <p>Council's Reversion To OW Rate</p>
        <p>Of Charges.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>ALLEGATIONS OF FAVOURITISM.</head>
        <p>ALLEGATIONS OF FAVOURITISM.</p>
        <p>Considerable discussion _took place at the monthly meeting of the Town Council on Tuosdair evening, arising out of a proposition by Councillor Gough. _</p>
        <p>-Councillor Gaon- moved: "That the follow- ing resolution ',passed:l)Y the Council in Feb- ruary last-,be rescinded, and that the old scale of charges for water by meter for manufac- turers that were working before the 26th March last became operative on the ,26th June next; 'That the scales of charges for water for manu- facturing. ,purposes-04:-Meter, be as follo:Yrs 'For sup Plies taken per quarter, from domestic sup Pry mains within the borough, and domestic. -tritiins outside' the -borough—is. per' 1;000 gallons forthelirst 200,000 gallons, but not less than 10s. per quarter. For any quantity exceeding 200,000 gallons and not /exceeding 5,000,000 -gallons at 9d. per 1,000 gallons. For supplies taken per quarter from trade supply mains within the borough-Is. per 1,000 gallons for the first 200,000 gallons, but not less than 106. pe:i quarter. For any quantity exceeding 200,000 gallons and not exceeding 3,000,000 gallons at 9d. per 1,000 gallons. For any quantity exceeding 3,000,040 gallons and not exceeding 5,000,000 gallons at 7d. per 1,000 gallons. For any quantity exceeding 5,000,000 gallons and not exceeding 15,000,000 gallons at 4d. per 1,000 gallons. For any quantity exceed- ing 15,000,000 gallons at Id. per 1,000 gallons. That a discount of 7i per cent. be given for all quarterly consumptions exceeding- 200,900 gallons paid for within a Month after presenta- tion of the account. That these scales Of charges shall come into operation as from the 26th. March.'"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>Under No Obligation.</head>
        <p>Under No Obligation.</p>
        <p>Councillor Gough said the members of the Council were well aware that the Corporation were under no obligation to supply firms with water for manufacturing purposes. He had obtained some statistics, which showed how the community wore affected by the new scale of charges. There were 25 firms in the borough (none outside), who had benefited under the new scale. Twenty-four of the firms had only benefited by the extra discount of 71 per cent., none of these having reached the number of gallons that would entitle them to participate :n the further reduction under the new scale. The total amount of the extra discount which those 24 firms had received was £Bl Is. Id. One firm only of the 25 had received all the benefits from the new scale. That firm bought from the Corporation, during the first three months of this year—under the old scale of charges, in- cluding both domestic and trade supplies— mecum gallons of 'water, for which they paid a total of £604 ds. Bd. During the second three months of the year, under the new charges for trade purpo§es, the same firm bought 15,494,000 gallons; for which they paid a total of £342 is. 6d. Of this amount the firm got 71- per cent. discount, which left the not -cost £316 Bs.. sd. The firm would have had to pay under the old scale for the same number of gallons•,-45,494,000- 2519 18e. Bd. A reduction of the cost under the new scale from that under the old scale showed a saving to the firm in one quarter of £203 I,os. Sd.</p>
        <p>The loss to the Corporation was a very serious one, especially when it was remembered that the water undertaking cost the ratepayers, to capitalise, half a million pounds. The under- taking had not been a successful one, not having contributed anything, for a congecutive num- ber of years, towards the relief of the rates. He ventured to say if this state of things apper- tained to any private firm, the responsible heads would be 'discharged.</p>
        <p>Councillor Gogh concluded by asking the members to pass the resolution "in the public interest."</p>
        <p>Councillor ARCHER. seconded the resolution in order that the matter might be discussed.</p>
        <p>DID NOT UNDERSTAND._</p>
        <p>Councillor JENEiNs said he did not understand one iota of what Councillor Gough meant by his opening statement. Would it not have been a wiser thing if Councillor Gough had sup- plied the members with copies of his statistics for their consideration?</p>
        <p>Councillor LINDLEi said the new charges were made with ,a. view to securing an increased use of water, and they could not have a better argu- ment in favour of them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>JEOPARDISING THEIR POSITION.</head>
        <p>JEOPARDISING THEIR POSITION.</p>
        <p>Councillor WILKINSON averred that the firures quoted meant nothing.; the firm who were stated to have received the largest amount of discount had naturally paid the largest amount for water. It would be a scandal and shame, in-view of the progress the town had made in the last 20 years, if they jeopardised their future position by using, or having to use, the good domestic supply of water from Winwick for manufacturing purposes. The new charges were intended to be an inducement to manu- facturers of the town.</p>
        <p>Alderman JOHN EVANS said that since the new scale had been adopted, the increased demand had been 33 per cent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>"AN ABOMINABLE THING."</head>
        <p>"AN ABOMINABLE THING."</p>
        <p>Councillor PEEBEIMON supported the motion, and said he had opposed the new scale of charges in committee and in the Council Cham- ber. "It is one of the most abominable things the Council his ever done," he continued. " Threequarters of the Council never under- stood it, and I say they don't understand it to-day."</p>
        <p>CoUncillor WILKINSON: Which quarter? Councillor PEMBERTON : I am certain.. tho Chairman of the Financo Committee does not understand it.</p>
        <p>Councillor Pemberton went on to say it was well known—who were going to benefit by them. He contended that the advantage, of the scale could only be reaped by the firms who could take a large supply; the small manu- facturer must pay three times the 'price. Did they think there was any equity in that? This was due to the fault of rushing things. There was only 040 profit last year on the water undertaking, and yet they were proposing this year to grant .t 1,200 in discount. Where would they get it from? A grave injustice had been done, not only to the rest of the manufacturers, hut to the ratepayers in 'Warrington generally. If this thing were not put right they would hear more about it. It was known that there was only one firm that would benefit. -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>TO AVOID ENCROACHMENT.</head>
        <p>TO AVOID ENCROACHMENT.</p>
        <p>Tile DEPUTY MAYOR (Councillor Joseph) said the discount was held out to encourage a greater consumption of water, for manufactur- ing purposes. That was in view of the exten- sion of the water engineering plant, and, to avoid encroaching on the domestic supply. As the extension could not be carried on at present on account of the war, he thought it wise to suspend the new charges and revert to the old ones.</p>
        <p>Alderman JurES EVANS acquiesced with the Deputy Mayor that the war had changed the whole conditions. Although, perham, they had not studied the matter quite as deeply as Coun- cillor Pemberton thought they ought to have done, they were not:g-uilty Of so much -neglect as Councillor Pemberton contended., they were. He thought somethingv:shonld..,be done with regard to the modification of the ypt:esent scale. He did not feel free to vote for Councillor Cough's motion as it stood, hut 'thought time should be given for reconsidetation .of the matter. The motion was c3rried by 10-6.</p>
        <p>French Church Music.—Mr. William Cooke, Organist at Lymm Parish Church is, during tnis month giving voluntaries by French composers, as follows :--rPastorale, Ed. Lemaigre; Offertoire in C, L. Wely; Offertoire in D Minor, Batiste; Crucifixus, Faure; Grand Choeur in 4G, Th. Salome; Larghetto, Claude Dupre; Gavotte D Major, J. M. Leclair; Gavotte C Minor, J. P. Ramean;.La Res-e, G. Bizet; Finale (Fantasia E flat), C. Saint-Saens; Absence, Hector Berlioz; Prayer, Alex. Guilmant; and March Romaine, Gounod.</p>
        <p>If you don't r, et t beF.t ci it, make the best. of it. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>II DISTRICT tiEwsl.</head>
        <p>II DISTRICT tiEwsl.</p>
        <p>lIYMIVI</p>
        <p>A Proficient Cricketer.—The Grammar School, bat, kindly presented by Mr.- H. Sa,ville, •Ika,B this.yeer been won by S. Allchin for proficiency in cricket.</p>
        <p>Vokinteers' Long March.—ta command of Captain Williamson and Sergeants Nlooton. and Lister Farrar, the Lymm Volunteers-on Tues- day evening did a long route march. Setting out early from the Drill Hall, they proceeded to Thelwall, thence round by Grappeuhall, and so hope—arriving in again at about tErn. o'clogk. _</p>
        <p>'Burials.—ln addition to two 'children Nth° died young, the July burials at the Parish .Chnreh include that of Kate Child .(Battersea, London), aged 80 years—which took placa on Saturday, the 19th.</p>
        <p>Marriages.—During July three weddings have been solemnised at St. Mary's Parish Church: On the 17th, William Haslam (Christ Church, Harwood) to Janet Sharpies (Lymm); on the 24th, Arthur James to Ethel High&amp;ld (both of Lymm); and on the 31st, Thomas Rathbone to Ethel Darbyshire (both of Lymm).</p>
        <p>Parish Church Liabilities.—A fully attended meeting of the Parish Church Council was held on Wednesday, July 28th, in the ,Pepper-street Schools. At this the chief business was the consideration of the parochial budget for the year ending next Easter. Mr. F. E. Evans had drawn up .a careful statement in regard to the ten funds for which it had been the custom to collect subscriptions, and on striking a balance between estimnted expenses in regard to these funds and estimated credits through collections and other sources, the Council decided that £2OO was required to meet all liabilities during the year.</p>
        <p>Rush-Bearing Week.—This week has been Rush-bearing, Week—otherwise the Ly.mm Wakes Week. Nowadays, however, it is only kept up in name. Talking yesterdaY to an old resident, we learned that at one time visitors used to flock from all parts to a big fair irhich extended from the Spread Eagle to Lymm Bridge—right through the village—and from the Cross to Church Green. In those times Morris dances always played a prominent part in the festivities, which lasted throughout the week; and these dances, if nothing else, have always been kept up until this year, having now been abandoned owing to the war (as all the dancers are at the Front. even " "the old •` woman ' with th'e spoon and bell "0 The only celebration this year was the shopkeepers closing, as usual, all Monday.</p>
        <p>Miss J. C. Hubbpck.—",We feel we cannot allow Miss Hubback's departure from Lymm to take place without an expression of apprecia- tion in the magazine," writes the Rector. "Miss Hubback, who is an accomplished and experienced scholar, came to Lymm as senior mistress of the Grammar School six years ago. The school', at that time was in a transitional state on account chiefly of a somewhat recent change in the scheme which permitted the admission of girls as scholars. Under tjae Patient and wise direction of Miss Hubback. this ,side of the school has now been 'fully developed, and established in the fine new build- ings erected for the purpose three or four years ago. The governors, and --we may add 'the parents, part with Miss Hubback with great regret, a special resolution having been passed to that effect at the last governors' meeting. This kindly feeling was echoed by the scholars in a beautiful presentation made on their be- half by Miss Ruth Willan, on the 'breaking- up' day, at which the 'headmaster and the chairman of the governors were present, and expressed to the best of their ability the great services Miss Hubback had rendered to the school. Miss Hubback goes to London after the holidays to take up the post of tutor at St. Mary's College, Lancaster Gate, under her old friend and headmistress Miss Powell." The presentation referred to by the Rector was the gift of the school staff and scholars, and con- sisted of a handsome solid silver writing set— inkstand, pentray and blotter, each appropri- ately inscribed. Miss frubback received it at the hands of Ruth Willan, the senior girl. It is interesting to note that quite recently Miss Hubback's father published a volume en- titled "Russian Realities "—as a result of, his travels—which is being most favourably received by readers and reviewers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>WITH THE COLOURS.</head>
        <p>WITH THE COLOURS.</p>
        <p>Additions To The Rector's List.</p>
        <p>Continiiing-the,Lymm Roll of HC4101.11%, which he compiled and inverted in the June I'arish Magazine, the Rector (Rev. G. P: Thurston) gives the following names in this month's issue:—</p>
        <p>OFFICERS. Lieutenant Oscar de Jong, R.A.M.C. Lieutenant W. Wild, Manchester Regiment.</p>
        <p>NON-COMMISSIONEO OFFICERS AND</p>
        <p>MEN. Antrobus, Harry, King's Royal Rifles. Barfoot, Edward, A.S.C. Bolt, William, King's Own Edinburgh Rifles. Bra-zendale, Frank, King's Royal Rifles. Burdett, John, South Lancashires. *Daniels, Fred, Cheshires. Dewhurst, Edgar, Royal Engineers.: Garvie, Oswald, R.A.M.C. Holmes, John E., A.S.C. Holt, Peter, King's Royal Rifles. Howcroft.. Cyril, 3rd Manchesters. Jackson, Harry, King's Royal Rifles. Kenwright, John. South Lancashires. Owen, George, Kings Royal Rifles. *Sh.epheard. Thomas W., Cheshires.' Summerfleld, G. J.. R,F.A. Taylor, James If_ South Laneashires. Twithy, H., A.S.C..' Warburton, George A., Cheahires. * Killed in action.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>SUNDAY SCHOOL TREATS.</head>
        <p>SUNDAY SCHOOL TREATS.</p>
        <p>Some Held At Home Owing To The</p>
        <p>War.</p>
        <p>Sunday School Excursion.—On Thursday was held the Parish Church. Sunday-School annual excursion, when a, happy day was spent in a visit to Belle Vue, Manchester. Early in the afternoon the pleasurers trained.to Broadheeth,. where special cars, provided by the-'Manchester 'CorporatiOn, -conveyed them. to the, gates Belle Vue. The afternoon wa&amp; enjoyably spent in- the usual way, whilst in the'evening -there wzta given one of those celebrated firework ex- hibitions. Yesterday the infants had their treat.</p>
        <p>The Congregationalists held their annual treat this year on Wednesday afternoon, which took the form of a field day. In the absence of the Superintendent (Mr. J. H. HMO,- G. Willan and James Hewitt had charge of ,the arrangements. The day, -which was favoured with very good weatLor, was happily.i Ipttssed'at the Booth's-- Farm. The Children's sports and..races were, managed I:l....Messrs., H. Hesketh the prizes taking.the'forrn of " coin •of -the realm --Y=lan--innovation, which' the youngsters voted capital. • Mrs-Egerton ,catered for the tea, giving the greatest -mail.; faction. The celebrations closed ,at about nine o'clont by the- rendering of .a few anniversary hymns and the National Anthem, and the chil- dren dispersed, cheering for their, school and- officers.</p>
        <p>The annual school treat in connection with the Lymm Wc,sleyans was held on Wednesday of last week: Instead of the usual trip to the seaside it was decided. as a result of the war. that it should be held in the township itself, and accordingly a field was lent by Mrs. B. Egerton at her spacious (C.T.C.) Booth's Farm. About seventy scholars and teachers turned out in the early afternoon, and spent a happy day in sports and games. each child receiving a capital prize. Afterwards an excellent tea was served—having very kindly been given by Messrs. A. H. Hudson, W. H. .Finch and W. Hinton—to whom all were Aiuch indebted. After four more hours' fun the scholars con- cluded their enjoyable day by the singing of a selection of favourite hymns. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>SOLD!ER;IIUSBAD MYSTERY.</head>
        <p>SOLD!ER;IIUSBAD MYSTERY.</p>
        <p>, Wife Not Certain Of fleturned Man's Identity.</p>
        <p>RELATIVES AND COMRADES CONSULTED.</p>
        <p>Husband or not? This is a question that is still to be answered for. Mrs. Herbert Dandy, a soldier's wife, of 227, Clowes-street, West Crarten„..lllanchester. It concerns a;than ltho for •a• week 'shared her home with her, saying he was her soldier-husband returned from the Front.</p>
        <p>Prisoner Persistent.</p>
        <p>The remarkable story was told at the Man- chester City Police Court on Wednesday, when a man .giving the name of Sergeant Herbert Dandy was charged with falsely representing himself to be a sergeant of that name, No. 1752 of the 1/Bth Battalion, Manchester Regiment. The accused persistently asserted that he was the real Sergeant Dandy. The real Sergeant Dandy, be this man he or not; is a Lymm man, being the son of Mr. and M.rs. Frederick Dandy, of Newfields. He was born in Lymm, but as a young man left with the family for America, where they spent a number of years, afterwards returning to the township. Inspector Thomas, who arrested he prisoner, stated that at the outbreak of war Sergeant Dandy (No. 1752) went out with his regiment to Egypt, but was subsequently transferred, and participated in the Dardanelles fighting. After an engagement on July 15th he was re- ported missing; and on July. 28th Mrs. Dandy, his wife, who lived at Clowes-street, West Gor- 'ton, received an intimation from the War Office to that effect. When The List Was Published.</p>
        <p>The day after the list wak published in the Manchester newspapers the .now-accused man called at Mrs. Dandy's house. He was wearing military uniform, and on seeing him the wife exclaimed: "Who are you?" and the man replied, " It's Herbert." They then embraced each other, and the man stayed at the house for a week.</p>
        <p>Suspicions were, however, aroused.- In the course of a few days the people living in the neighbourhood began to cast doubts as to the identity of the man, and the matter becoming common gossip, Mrs. Dandy took him to a sister's house in Marsden, Yorkshire.</p>
        <p>The man, however, appeared to know a great deal of the family history, and the wife even now could not say whether he was her husband or not. Her own relatives and also the rela- tives of her husband had been to see him, and they and also old schoolfellows seemed to have taken him for the real Sergeant Dandy. Unrecognised By Other Soldiers.</p>
        <p>Since his apprehension .(the inspector con- tinned). five other Men who fought with Sergeant Dandy at the Dardanelles, and who were now in Manchester wounded, had been placed before the accused. He was asked to name them, but failed toAame a single one correctly. These men were satisfied thaf the accused was not the real Sergeant Dandy. A remand was granted until Thursday next. The Army authorities pave the case in hand, and further inquiries are being made. The Bench refused to allow bail.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>RECTOR RE% lEWS 11 AR-OUTLOOK</head>
        <p>RECTOR RE% lEWS 11 AR-OUTLOOK</p>
        <p>Shallow Optimism And Unwarranted Pessimism Have Had Their Day. The Rev. G. B. Thurston, rector of Lymm, has something to say in the August Parish Magazine anent the recently-celebrated anni- versary of the war. In reviewing, the war, he says:— Of the present position of the armies in the field, their apparent successes and failures, and of the navies on the high seas, this is hardly the place to write, even had one the ability to grasp the enormous operations that are now in progress. I suppose, however, that the general impression on most people's minds to-day, after all these months of loss and struggle and dis- appointment, is one of less exultant certainty than it VTIL•9 a year ago. If this is-so it is all ko the good, because it is the only way in which the nation can be aroused to a realisation of the task before it. The time for shallow optimism is past. What we have to guard • against is a- reaction and an indulgence in pessimism, for which there is no warranty in the situation as it stands. Learning Humility. " What has happened to cause depression the fact that we have lost our reserve power and that buoyancy which can bear anxiety only when it is fresh; but when month after month goes on and we seem to be no nearer to the end, then is the time that our spirits are tested, and then is the time to find out what we really . are. The first difficult lesson we are learning is humility--a most precious Christian virtue and one so generally misunderstood. Men of excellent courage and intelligence are beginning to admit to themselves that they are losing faith in human power, and specially in their own judgment; they would like to cry aloud for help if only they were sure of being heard. This, again, is a move in the right direction, because it is a sign that we are being brought back again to the simple faith and dependence which characterised our country in her past struggles. 'They trusted in Thee and Thou didst deliver them,' implies no disregard for military efficiency, rather is it a real and vital accompaniment to armed force. At the impres- sive service held on the steps of St. Paul's Cathedral in London on St. James' Day, this spirit was very manifest. There were solditers without military glitter, crowds without noise, and prayers that we might do our duty and be worthy.of those Allies who have borne so well the burden and heat of the day; the whole' scene being summed up by one who was present as 'a will of power working for good things with a humility that gave it beauty: Prayer v. Pessimism. "What we need more, than anything else at the present time .18 more of- this simple faith and humble attitude towards Almighty God in all the parishes in England. Lack of faith and spiritual insight is the cause of the wave of pessimism which has been passing over us and threatens to overwhelm us unless it is thrown back. And there is no reason for it or place for it in the hearts of faithful people. Some of us .have realised what individual prayer has already done for our men at the Front, but what we want to feel is that the whole nation is testing the value of spiritual power. The thing we really, need is not that those who have some faith should have more of it so much as that those who have any at all should show it. It is as unpatriotic to hoard faith as to hoard gold, especially when we are so short of the former. . . . It is corporate witness to that fact—the fact of God—that we need even more than attual prayer to-day. The faults and shortcomings of our nation; have become more and more manifest under this ;mat visitation of God during the past year; we should be thankful of this, because amid much that is 'good and noble we should be better and nobler if these things were rooted out and swept away, and ,what we all desire to be, a greater blessing to the world, at large. The wisdom of religion tells us we can only cure ourselves of our faults if we hate them in themselves and not merely because of the perils with which they threaten us. It is conviction of sin, not con- viction of danger, that must change us if we are to be changed. Conviction of danger will only make us upbraid each other; conviction of sin will set us to the more profitable task of asking plain questions about ourselves."</p>
        <p>Private Henry Cadman.—ln a letter received by his sister, Mrs. S. Owen, of Brookfield Cot- tages, Private Henry Cadman says he has made still another move, having now left the Tunnelling Section of the 1/sth CheslAre Territorials and joined the Royal Engineers. He, however, will continue to work with his Cheshire comrades,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>LYMM TERRITORIALS.</head>
        <p>LYMM TERRITORIALS.</p>
        <p>STILL OUT OF TRENCHES AFTER MUCH TRAVELLING.</p>
        <p>The Lymm "Terriers " serving at the Front are still—according to the latest news—out of the trenches. They have been doing rather a lot of travelling, after which they are resting in a French village. Yesterday Mrs. J. Taylor, of 1, West Hyde, received, word from her son, Private Henry Brookes: "Well, we have moved, as I told,you we were going to do, but we have not been in the trenches yet since I came back, and I can- not say exactly when we shall go'in. And really I am not wishing to go in, as I have seen quite sufficient of them since we came out here. I may be sending you some good news before very long: It is a beautiful country where we are, and has not been destroyed by the Germans as .much as the last place we were :in. I should like to be home for Christmas, us I enjoyed the short stay I had with you. Jock Gibbon has been through' one place where we were, but we didn't get the chance to see him." • Private Ernest Hadden, of Pepper-street, says '"lfire 'are, at - present in quite a new dis- trict, which we reached last Sunday night, after a tiring train journey—or rather in cattle trucks. I hear Walter is discharged from hos- pital; I expect he won't be long before he is back with us. Fancy J. Thomason getting back to England! I, don't think I shall get much chance of seeing Arthur now; I-don't think their regiment is down this district. I have had J. Hinde to see me twice while being here; he does look well."</p>
        <p>Private Percy Clare, writing in interesting style to his mother, Mrs. G. Davies, of Booth's Hill, says: "It is Saturday morning again— just two weeks to-night since we left the trenches, and we are not in them again yet. We are in a small French village about ten miles from the trenches. We don't know how long we shall stay here; we have been here three days now. It is itist twelve months to-morrow since we first left home; I hope we shall not be away much longer. . . . The boys are in the pink up to now; we will all have a good time when this lot is over, if we have the luck."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>"On the Move."</head>
        <p>"On the Move."</p>
        <p>Writing a day earlier the Private said: "I have not had much time to write these last few days, as we have been on the move. We had a day's train ride lait Sunday, and we stayed inn French village for about four days. Then we Viere on the march again all night last night [August 4th]. We are in another village at a farmhouse living in a -Urn. I think we are making our way for the trenches. It has been fine to be away from the shot and shell for a while; we are out of the way yet, and don't mind how long we stay away. But we must not grumble, as we have had a nice stay away from the trenches—although we have had some long marches. .. . When we moved out of Bel- gium into France we got on the train at about seven o'clock on the Sundty morning, and we were in till about eight o'clock at night. Well, I said train. but I meant trucks—cattle trucks. In one truck we had between thirty and forty men, so you can tell what it was like. When we landed we had about five miles to walk, but here we are in a nice little village !" Private William Jackson's wound continues to progress well, and he has just been removed from the Banstead Military Hospital, Surrey, to the Lord Derby War Hospital, Winwick. This, of Rourse. enables him to be visited by his friends More frequently. Last ,week we inad- vertently referred to the Private by the name of "Percival."</p>
        <p>Vrivate Arthur Hadden—who. it will be re- membered, only recently arrived at the Front writes to his mother at Pepper-street: ".I was very glad to -hear of Walter not getting so badly wounded. I got a letter from Ernest, and he told me it was a splinter if* the palm of his hand, and that he is going on all right. I see that Ernest is expecting -to-get over for-a few days. We are out of the trenches again. We have been out no for about fourteen days. I think we are out or a good rest, as I can tell you we want it after we have been in there for a bit."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>"IN A HOT PLACE."</head>
        <p>"IN A HOT PLACE."</p>
        <p>Lance-Corporal Booth Having Change-</p>
        <p>able Weather Abroad.</p>
        <p>Lance-Corporal John Henry Booth, of the 2nd Welsh Regiment—whose home is at Cross Brow, Statham, and who belongs to the family whose fine military record we .gave a few weeks ago—has lately written several interesting letters to Councillor F. Lockyer, of the Star Hotel, Statham. These show that the Welsh have been having some extremes of weather lately:— was very, pleased to receive the cigarettes you sent me, as we get hardly any now—well, only once a week, and then we only get a few; I can smoke them in one day what they give us, then go,all the week without any at all. I had a letter from Billy Cooke, a Sergeant in the 2/6th Manchesters, instructing recruits in musketry in the South, and/he is going- on all right. Jim [Booth, also at the Front) told me he is going home for a few days, so I hope he will get it; also Joe. They are quite well so far, and send their best wishes to you all. "We are having some very changeable weather here at present, and we are also in a warm place .too, for they shell us from morn- ing till night, so you can picture us hiding in cellars."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>" Still In Land of Living."</head>
        <p>" Still In Land of Living."</p>
        <p>Writing on the 4th, the Lance-Corporal says: "Still in the land of the living, and up to our waist in mud and water, for we are having nothing else but rain at present and we are in the trenches now, so you can tell how -we enjoyed ourselves on August Bank Holiday. But I expect that Joe Hewitt enjoyed himself all right at home. .. I expect that Jim win be the next home according .to the last letter I , had from him, -but I think that I shall be home by the end of the year. I hope that is correct, for I can do with a good feed, as it is very poor rations we are getting now, and just the same day after day—bully and biscuits, cheese and jam. You soon get fed up with it, but, of course, have to put up with it. • Nearly Buried With Bomb.</p>
        <p>"I must say that I have had some very narrow escapes this tithe. I. nearly got buried with a bomb; it. dropped•.on,.my due-out and smashed it all in on top of me. We were only twenty yards away from the German trenches; they gave us a proper hot time of it while we were there.</p>
        <p>"This is one of our songs. On the line o march we sing : I want to go home. I want •to go home, _</p>
        <p>The shrapnel and coal-boxes whistle and roar; I don't want to go to the trenches no more. I want to go over the sea Where the Germans can't throw bombs at me, And oh my! I don't want.to die, I want to go home!"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>PRIVATE BERT BURROWS.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE BERT BURROWS.</p>
        <p>Gassed Mier Many</p>
        <p>Months.</p>
        <p>Two more additions have just been made fo Councillor Lockyer's collection of war relies. These two are the regulators of 'a French .and a German shell, and have been brought by Private Bert Burrows, of the R.A.M.C., who lives in Norfield-road. Private Burrows hail been at the Front practically since the war out- break, but has now come horiu after recover- ing, to some extent, from being gassed. The Private, 'who is married, and has one daughter, came to Statham from Cambridge- shire after the Boer War—through which he also served with the R.A.M.C. He has all his first-aid certificates. In peace times he is a Lymm postman. His wife is also patriotically serving at Brookfield, the Red Cross Hospital.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>PRIVATE A. H. THOMASON.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE A. H. THOMASON.</p>
        <p>Sherwooti Forester Discusses His 'Shrapnel-Wound.</p>
        <p>The news that Private Arthur Reyes Thomason, of the Sherwood Foresters (Robin Hood Rifles), had been wounded, was received late last week by his father. Mr. John Arthur Thomason, Brook House, Booth's Hill—who himself formerly held the rank of. sergeant in the Lymm Volunteers. The wound is a shrap- nel wound received in Flanders. , This week. in a letter revived by his father, ,</p>
        <p>on Thursday evening Private Thomason said he was going on as well as could be expected, but would have to undergo an operation. The letter was written from the 23rd General Hospital, France, and runs: "I am still going along well. I got up to-day for the first time. My legs are ,better, but very weak; I can just manage to walk a few yards with assistance. I hrfore• not felt a great deal of the wound itself; the only trouble now is that my shoulders and chest muscles are so sore and stiff. The doctors have found the piece of shrapnel by means of X-ray photographs, and it has passed over to my right shoulder. The doctor, wants another of these photos to see how deep ,the piece is (that is. a side view), but he says that should it involve any nerves or be awkward to get at, and provid- ing it causes no trouble and Shows no infection. it would not harm me to leave it in. The front view shows it as about the size of the thumb nail. . . . We get plenty of good food here." Nottingham Lace Designer.</p>
        <p>Born in Lymm 21 years ago, Private Thomason received his education at the Lymm School and later at the Grammar School. About five years ago he went to woik as a lace designer in Man- chester for the Calico Printers' Association. and after two years proceeded on similar work to Nottingham. For four years before the outbreak of war the Private had belonged to the "Sherwood Forest" Territorials, but, of course, did not then con- clude his service as he would have done in peace times.</p>
        <p>The Private belongs to Lymm Church.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>"GIVING THEM HOT -STUFF."</head>
        <p>"GIVING THEM HOT -STUFF."</p>
        <p>Sergeant_ George Appleton And The</p>
        <p>-7 - Kaiser's Discovery. Yesterday Mr. and 'Mrs. Thomas Appleton, of Cross Brow, Statham; reeeived from their son in the 14th Light Division 42nd Infantry Brigade, word as follows "We are not half giving them hot stuff here, and my opinion -is that the Kaiser has now fOund out he has got a better lot of fighting men against hip than his own, and we may now look to a speedy end of the war.</p>
        <p>"I am pleased to tell you of my promotion to Sergeant. . . . I haven't seen any of my Lymm friends since I left England. I ani going on all right."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <head>GRAMMAR SCHOOL STUDENTS.</head>
        <p>GRAMMAR SCHOOL STUDENTS.</p>
        <p>Gratifying Successes In University</p>
        <p>Examinations.</p>
        <p>The names of many students at the Lymm Grammar School appear in examination result lists recently published : Ruth Wade Willan has passed the Northern Universities Joint Matriculation. The Northern Universities SchOol Certificate has been carried off by C. K. Armitage. W. N. Benson. R. W. Rathbone and Annie J. Winnall. In the various divisions of the Royal Draw- ing Society Examinations appear with honours the names of 47 Lymm Grammar School stu- dents, while 14 others have passed. The examinations were " sat" at the conclu- sion of the year's school tuition.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0173" n="art0173">
        <head>"COME BACK TO ERIN."</head>
        <p>"COME BACK TO ERIN."</p>
        <p>Irish Harvesters Hastily Return—From</p>
        <p>National Register.</p>
        <p>MOST SERIOUS PROBLEM.</p>
        <p>A serious dislocation of labour has bees caused, amongst other agricultural districts, in Lymm. Singularly enough, it has been caused by the taking of the National Register. The problem is:— Many Irish labourer t have left their employ- ment to return to their native land in the hope of escaping registration, the exodus has been from agricultural areas. From the Boston dis- trict of Lincolnshire, the Fylde district of Lancashire and the Lymm district of Cheshire the numbers leaving have been so great that it is reported as to be viewed with consternation.</p>
        <p>One farmer in Cheshire, who is also in a large way of business in Smithfield, Manches- ter, declares that- from his own district every Irishman had left. The registration and fear of compulsory service was the reason he adduced. On Sunday last, he added, the men were about "as busy as ferrets" making arrangements for their departure. Not a word was said to the farmers, "but, like Arabs, they had by Monday night packed up their belong- ings and had silently stolen away." From in- quiries he found that they had gone in the direction of Liverpool. An impression pre- vailed that there was no registration in Ire- land, and that in the event of returning home they would escape registration and perhaps military service. Why Position Is Serious.</p>
        <p>The position is undoubtedly a 'serious one. "At our harvest time," say the Cheshire farmers, "we engage men at good wages. Many of the labourers would earn from £2 to £2 10s. per' week over a period of about three months, during the picking of the second and late crops •of, potatoes. Yet now .they have gone; and un- less labour can be procured the second crop will rot. Wheat, of which there is a grand crop, has been beaten down by the rains, and ready for cutting. If we cannot get men the ' grain will sprout and be ruined. The same applies to oats. Fruit, which is plentiful, re- mains to be gathered. There is, in fact, an ' abundant harvest, and the desertion of the Irish farm labourers now is a cruel blow, for the labour shortage caUsed a rise in the price of potatoe3 and other root crops when the quality did not justify the increase .in price." "The Government should take action in the matter, and intercept all Irish farm labourers as they reach Liverpool and send them back to the farms.' Recruiting has taken many farm hands, and now numerous farmers are left with old men and Army rejects. One farmer has usually found .employment for eighteen or twenty men and the only able-bodied man he has left is his own "son—who, in addition to superintending the farms, -attending to the engines, motors, dm., is called upon to assist in loading up produce for Manchester. On a pre- vious day not &amp; single potato arrived at his ware- house in Manchester, owing to there being nci men to pick and load them."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <head>Female Labour Impracticable.</head>
        <p>Female Labour Impracticable.</p>
        <p>It has been stated that in Cheshire, at least, women are of little use in the fields. They could not stand the strain of digging potatoes and standing knee deep in soil, it wasi stated this week. Again, in Cheshire it was considered degrading on the part of women to labour in the fields, though it was now ,certain that farmers and the whole of their families would have to do the labour usually' performed by the Irishmen if the crops were to be saved*. On. Thursday a Northwich man put the num- ber of Irish farm workers who have fled from Cheshire to Ireland or emigrated to America to escape registration at over 200. The sugges- tion is that they fear compulsory detention on Government work during the ensuing winter, and that as they hale left their wives to manage their farms in Ireland their wives would be subjected to great hardship through their absence.. /linty men,' it is seid. lave been working at Liverpool dock in readinesa to leave before the paper had to be filled West Lancashire, including Ormakirk end Balla m (near Lytham) are also suffering heavily from the same thing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0175" n="art0175">
        <head>GRAPPENHAL L.</head>
        <p>GRAPPENHAL L.</p>
        <p>Missing Since May.—Muh sympathy is felt for Mrs. Shaw, of Cliffe-lane, Grappenhall, whose son, Sergeant Samuel Shaw, was reported missing on May Bth, and has not yet been heard of. Mrs. Shaw has written to the War Office, but their reply is that they have heard nothing of her son.</p>
        <p>Left For The Front.—William Walter Jar- man, Mayfield-road, • Grappenhall, has received' word this week that his son, William Walter, who is in the Royal Field Artillery, has sailed for France. He is only about 18 years of 'age, And well respected in the neigh- bourhood.</p>
        <p>In The Trenches.—Mr, Booth, Weaste-lane, Grappenhall, has just received word from his son Frank that he entered the trenches in France for the first time on Monday, August 3rd, as a repairer. He is only 21 years of age. In a letter to his, mother he says he will never forget his first baptism of fire, but it is astonishing how quickly one gets used to it. He enlisted early in November, and was anxious to get out to do his duty for his . . eduntry.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0176" n="art0176">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>"Cat and Lion," Stretton.—At Tuesday's Ses- sions application was made by Mr. John Win. Starkey, licensee of the Cat and Lion, Stretton., for the transfer of the licence of that house from Mr. Robert Walker.—This was granted.</p>
        <p>Attendant Justices.—At the Daresbury Petty Sessions, held nu Tuesday morning at Stockton Heath, there were present on the Bench: Messrs. F. A. Frost (in the chair), F. W. Monks, C. Jackson Holmes and John W. Fletcher.</p>
        <p>"Because He Had No Lamp."—Roger Wil- liams, labourer, of Stretton-road, Appleton? was summoned at the Petty Sessions on Tuesday for haying ridden his bicycle in Tarporley-road, Appleton, on July 25th, without a light.—Acting- Sergt.- Worth said that when stopped Williams excused himself by -saying that "he had no lamp."—The fine totalled 3s. 6d.</p>
        <p>Scouts' Wet Camp Week.—The St. Thomas' Boy Scouts came back out of the wilds last week-end, after a wet camp holiday, at Cud- dington, the residence of Mr. Clarke, brother of the Rev. Mr. Clarke. The boys had a very en- joyable week, despite the weather. They were in the charge of Scoutmaster Thompson and Assist- ant-Scoutmaster Pearson.</p>
        <p>Enumerating.--The work of enumerating in connection with the National Register is prac- tically complete. The following are the enumer- ators for Stockton Heath: The Rev. Mr. Brook, and Messrs. Fred Rose, Sheward, J. F. Ferguson, J. H. Williams, J. R. Westbrook, Jonathan Welsby, John McDermid, J. H. Lloyd and J. A. Simpson.</p>
        <p>Offender's Promise.—At Tuesday's Sessions, Robert Alleockt jun., moulder, of Canal Side, Grappenhall, appeared for having, at .10.30 on July 19th,- ridden his bieyele in Grappenhall village without a light.—He pleaded -guilty, and P.C. Booth gave evidence.—After fining de- fendant 3s. Gd., the Chairman said: " ep a light in future, because it is dangerous to be without one."—Defendant said he would.</p>
        <p>Scouts and Sandbags.—The St. Thomas' Boy Scouts, feeling that they would like to do some active work to help in the war, have decided to contribute to the pressing need in the Army for sandbags. It was stated in a letter published in the Press that one regiment alone wag using between, 16,000 and 20,000 per diem. The Scouts set out to collect funds on a recent Saturday, and realised, with five boxes, the, sum of 9s. 6d.</p>
        <p>Undecided Question.—Albert Edward Wash- ington, of Castle4treet, Bolton, came before the Daresbury Bench on Tuesday for having driven a motor lurry along Tarporley-road, Stretton, on July 10th, at a speed exceeding 12 miles per hour, namely 17 miles per hour.—Acting- Sergeant Worth gave the evidence.—Defendant said he timed the distance (1 mile 80 yards) to be done in nine minutes; moreover, the motor lurry could not go at more than 12 miles per hour.—The fine was 53.</p>
        <p>Hot Day and Cigarette-.—Thomas Britland, labourer, of Hatton-lane, Hatton, was sum- moned on Tuesday to the Sessions for having, on July 26th, in Hatton-lane, not had proper control over two horses and a lurry, of which he was in charge.—Acting-Sergeant Worth said defendant was riding upon the lurry, but had not the reins. When stopped he said he had only just got on.—Defendant said it was a hat day, and he took off his coat and threw it upon the lurry. Presently he wanted a cigarette,' and just jumped on the shafts to get one from his coat pocket, when the officer saw him. Alto- gether he was not on for, ten yards. As for reins, he had never put reins on single horses in his life, and he had been driving for forty years.—The Chairman. said ,the fine would be 3s. 6d., and tlie g'man had better pot reins on another time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0177" n="art0177">
        <head>YEAR OF WAR.</head>
        <p>A YEAR OF WAR.</p>
        <p>At St. Thomas' Church on Sunday evening an intercession service to mark the completion of a year of war was conducted by the Vicar (Rev. J. Collins). There was a large congre- gation.</p>
        <p>7 The Vicar, writing in the current issue of the Parish Magazine, says:— " By the time this issue is published we shall have passed through a year of the stress and anxiety of war—a stress which is beconiing ever greater, and a trial increasingly acute, for our own village, as the weeks pass. " There are now some twelve homes in the parish which have given of their best in the country's hour of need, and ten of these have made the " great sacrifice" in defence of hearth, home and country. "It was thought that the time had come when we should hold our first service in memory of those who had passed away fighting in the cause of right and justice, and the large con- gregation, includ.ing representatives from the Forge, the Volunteer Corps, the Parish Coun- cil, Scouts and other bodies, was an eloquent testimony of the respect and appreciation,. as well as being a silent assurance of regret and sympathy at the loss of brave men, which is felt" by , the nation in general and Stockton heath in particular, for her own heKoes. "The Rev. T. T. Lee-Jones, vicar lof Dares.. bury, in his hopeful, inspiring address, paid touching tribute not only to our gallant men, but also to the often-unnoticed heroism of the English women at home.</p>
        <p>"We feel that his words of comfort and hope can only inspire us, in spite of the sad and terrible triaLs, we are passing through, to go on courageously so to struggle for the great ideals of life that we may share with those who have passed away, brave, pure, forgiven, in the glorious re-union hereafter. Those who have fallen in the service of their. King and country are</p>
        <p>"Torn Berry. John Livingstone, William Brown, Robert Gerrard, Victor H. Fletcher, Sidney Taylor, William Hy. Taylor, Robe 7:, Walton, Francis Bettles, John Clark,"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0178" n="art0178">
        <head>SOLDIER IN THE DOCti.</head>
        <p>SOLDIER IN THE DOCti.</p>
        <p>Alleged Absentee Who Visited Outhouse For Clothing.</p>
        <p>Private George Armitage, a soldier who was stated to be an absentee from his regiment, and of no settled abode, was -charged on Tuesday morning at the Sessions with having been on enclosed premises adjoining a house 'at Walton Inferior for •an unlawful purpose— that of stealing goods. Superintendent Owen applied for an ad- journment of eight days. Constable's Investigations.</p>
        <p>P.C. Worthington said that at 4.15 on the previous afternoon he received , information to the' effect that Armitage had been in the outhouse. He found that he had left those premises, and therefore mounted his bicycle and followed him. He stopped him in Stock- ton Heath, and questioned him. Prisoner replied that he went "for to see a party." Witness then asked him to go back with him to the holm, and this Armitage at firat re- fused to do, but ultimately did so. Witness learned: that Armitage had been seen by the maid to approach the, outhouse across', a field at the back, dpen the yard-gate, enter the outhouse and close the door after him. She summoned a man from the front of the house, and he looked through and saw the man turning some clothes over in a clothes-basket.l Armitage ma, his escape, and after walking the short distance to Walton-road started running. He made his way to Stockton Heath by Whitefield-roacf and London-road. When charged at the Police Station he made no I reply.</p>
        <p>the eight days' remand 'rag granted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0179" n="art0179">
        <head>BOGUS WAR BADGES.</head>
        <p>BOGUS WAR BADGES.</p>
        <p>Wearers Liable To A Fine 01 O.</p>
        <p>-- -- Mr. Lloyd George has determined to stop the scandal of the bogus war .service badge. Rules have been issued by him under Sec- tion 8 of the Munitions of War Act, one of which reads:—</p>
        <p>No person shall, except with the express authority of the Minister, make, sell, issue,. or wear any badge similar in form or appear- ance to any badge supplied or authorised by the Minister, or any colourable imitation thereof, or any badge or other distinctive mark calculated or intended to suggest that the wearer thereof is engaged on munitions work or other work for war purposes. Under the Act any person who acts in con- travention-of the real; is liable fin each offence to a fine not exceeding AO.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0180" n="art0180">
        <head>CONWAY MYSTERY.</head>
        <p>CONWAY MYSTERY.</p>
        <p>Bold Mother And Daughter</p>
        <p>Drcomned.</p>
        <p>A. regrettable accident to a mother and her child, belonging to a family well known in the Great Sankey and Penketh district, formed the subject of an inquest on Wesdnesday by Mr. Pentir Williams, the Carnarvonshire coroner, at Yspytty, a remote village in Conway Valley, on Elizabeth Hodgkinson, aged 51, and Dena Hodg- kinson, her daughter, aged 5. The Deputy Chief Constable of Carnarvonshire (Mr. G. T. Guest) was present on behalf of the police. It appears that Mrs. Hodgkinson, who was the -wife of Mr. Henry Hodgkinson, farmer,. Bold Hall Farm, Bold, was staying for the benefit of her health at a cottage with her daughter. On Monday morning she went for a walk accom- panied by her little one and by Mrs. Richards and her children, who were also 'staying at the same cottage. After going a little way Mrs. Hodgkinson complained of feeling tired, and said she would go back. Mrs. Richards went on, leaving Mrs. Hodgkinson and her child behind them. About one o'clock, an hour and a -half later, -Mrs. Richards returned and found that Mrs. Hodgkinson and her daughter had not come. A search was made, and in the opposite direction to that taken by Mrs. Richards, a few yards below the bridge crossing 'the Conway river, the body of a woman was seen, and about 70 yards lower down that of the child. They were taken cut of the water, on assistance being obtained, but both were dead. The jury found that there was not sufficient evidence to show how the woman and child came ,to be in the river, and returned an opeh verdict.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0181" n="art0181">
        <head>PATRIOTIC APPLETON.</head>
        <p>PATRIOTIC APPLETON.</p>
        <p>Successful Garden Fete For Troop-Comforts.</p>
        <p>COLONEL LYON AND WAR PRISONERS.</p>
        <p>No more appropriate place than Appleton Hall could have been found for the conspicu- ously successful garden sale and fete Which was held on Saturday afternoon to raise funds for satisfying the requirements of the troops. The grounds of the Hall were looking at their best for the occasion, and it was with general regret that the steady oncoming of the storm tended somewhat to mar the beauty. .BY many it had been feared that the fete might have to be postponed on account of the weather, but the promoters were not to be deterred—and the really splendid result of their efforts proves that they were right in following out the programme, although with an attendance somewhat impaired. The promoters were the Appleton Habita- tion of the Primrose League, although undue emphasis was naturally not laid upon this fact. Mrs. G. Ireland-Blackburne opened the proceedings in the unfortunate absence of Mrs. Henry Greenall; and there were present: Lieutenant-Colonel, Mrs. and Miss •Margaret Lyon, Captain James, Mr. Henry Roberts, Rev. H. V. and Mrs. Pigot, Rev. J. Collins, Mrs. Brereton Faircloug,h, Mrs. James Fair- dough, Mrs. Lionel Fairclough, Miss Garry, Mrs. J. Charlton Parr, Mr., Mrs. iind Miss Segar Owen, Mrs. Watts, Mrs. and the Misses Broadbent, Mr. and Mrs: F. W. Monks, Mrs. Roscoe Brunner, Mrs. Prince, Mr. and Mrs. H. Beresford Melville, Mr. and Mrs. Herbert Brocklehurst, Mr. Harold Brocklehurst, Mr. E. Brocklehurst, Mrs. Harold Joseph, Mr. and Mrs. Joseph Taylor, Mr. and Mrs. James Par- kinson, Mrs. Bower, Miss Lewis, Miss Parnell, Mr. and Mrs. Hewitt, Messrs. T. Ditchfield, Joseph Bailey, George Whitlow and Joseph Jones, and others—including a number of convalescent soldiers, who were conveyed thither from the local hospitals.</p>
        <p>CHESHIRE PRISONERS IN GERMANY. Colonel Lyon said the fete was being, held in place of ,the Habitation's usual summer outing, which had been abandoned owing to the war; and it had been thought desirable that the public should also have the oppor- tunity of rendering assistance, as the object was to supply the requirements of the troops. Again, the Habitation had for some time beeli asking for assistance for English prisoners in Germany; and materials had been collected and made up into garments. In Cheshire they were particularly interested in the prisoners, because from GOO to 700 men of the Cheshire- Regiment had been in the hands of the Ger- mans for nearly a year. Of course, this was- very unfortunate, and what the prisoners had gone through they did not know. It was te be hoped that they were all right. But they would, undoubtedly, be all the better for any comforts which could be sent to them.</p>
        <p>The Colonel concluded by reading a letter from Colonel Alan J. Sykes (Member for th• linutsford Division), in which he regretted his inability to be present owing to a previous engagement, but expressed his pleasure at ths Habitation's using its organisation in. such a manner. (Applause.) MRS BLACKBURNE'S WISH.</p>
        <p>Upon rising to open the fete, Mrs. Ireland- Blackburn) was handed a beautiful bouquet by Miss Elnith Segar Owen. Mrs. Blackburni then referred to their disappointment that Mrs. Greenall was not able to be with them. In her absence she had much pleasure in de daring the fete open, and, +loped it would ht a success, 4.f..Tite the riAttior being qlsv. •</p>
        <p>the ..motion of Mrs. Lyo7., koconded by Mr. Whitlow, a hearty oto of thanks was accorclod to lire, Bla4burrie for her 6errives.</p>
        <p>TRIBUTE TO COLONEL LYON.</p>
        <p>Mr. Segar Owen next proposed a similar vote to the Chairman (Colonel Lyon), without whom, he said, a ceremony such as the pre- sent would not be complete. They thanked him not only for praeiding, but also for allow- ing the use of his park. All knew that the reason why they so seldom saw the Colonel at present was that he was so busily engaged in attending to his military duties. But he was always welcome, and they were pleased to know that their Dame President (Mrs. Lyon) took such a deep interest in the work which was being done in connection with the League. Mr. Jones seconded, and the proposition was heartily carried, Colonel Lyon replying briefrY., On the 'motion of Mr. Ditchfield, a tribute was also paid to the Ladies' Committee for their services in connection with the fete. The committee consisted of Mrs. Lyon (dame president), Mrs. Segar Owen (hon. secretary), Miss Hatton (acting hon. treasurer, in place of her soldier-brother), Mesdames E..Brackle- burst, H. Brocklehurst, Bower, T. Ditchfiedd, Jones, Melling, James Parkinson, Joseph Pen- nington, Tickle, J. Whitlow, and the Misses Margaret Long and Prestoß; assisted by other ladies and gentlethen.</p>
        <p>The opening ceremony was followed by .11 highly enjoyable entertainment given in talented style in one of the marquees which had been erected. Items were given with equal success by Miss Gladys .Adams, who sang in humorous rein, and was accompanied by Miss Clarice Newall; Miss Margaret Lyon. who exhibited me skill in a pretty pianeforio solo; and Master Leslie Melville, in recitatiOn. ~The principal attraction was a' sketch entitled "The Rest Cure," in which the several parts were taken in a style cif strikingly high stand- ard by Mr. Harvey Adams (of Warrington) and Miss Doris Lloyd, both of the LiverPo°l Repertory Theatre; Miss Doris Mason Dean, Birkenhead Amatefir Dramatic Society; Mrs. Joel Dean, Manor Mummies Co.; and Mrs. Fitzgerald Davies. This really capital sketch by so expert a company earned particular commendation.</p>
        <p>In the evening the whole performance wva' repeated with equal success, an extra "turn being contributed by Mr. John Darbyshire's amusing monologue. Other attractions were silhouette photography, a flower sale, an last but not least—tea! By kind permission wifiegateden, and Royal' ri gave eeVera' elq of Colonel Lyon, the Band of the Ist County AP ri Itl ial fl ei nue , alended tzer the Bt nmiSiCal selections in true military style:</p>
        <p>THE PROCEEDS.</p>
        <p>.1!-- • The proceeds amounted to no less ,than, .27 a result on which the promoters are to IjV heartily cangratalated.</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>P</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0182" n="art0182">
        <head>JAIO</head>
