      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0183" n="art0183">
        <head>TRUNKS, DRESS J.</head>
        <p>TRUNKS, DRESS J.</p>
        <p>BASKETS, SUIT CASES, GARLAND,</p>
        <p>51, 53 &amp; 55, BRIDGE STREET, WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>)thers to the abitor</p>
        <p>DRINK IN WAR-TIME.</p>
        <p>.ir,—Would you kindly allow me space to Insert in your paper my words of unbounded a.ppreeiation to Corporal Tombs, V.C., for the wanner in which he stood faithful to the "King's Pledge" of Total Abstinence, when re- eel‘red by the authority of our borough at the Town Hall, a report of which appeared in your last issue; also for the manner in which this incident has brought before the citizens of this town the inconsistency of our worthy authority? On Monday last week a public meeting was • convened for the purpose Of impressing the necessity of "thrift" among families, with the -necessaries of life. Two ,days rater the "con- vbners" have a good,opportunity, of 'setting tlie, example by drinking the health of Corporal' Tombs, V.C., and of the King, in water, which would have been appropriate, considering the fact that the King has signed tie' temperance pledge, and Tombs has done lik'ewi'se. But, in- stead of this, they drank their healths in wine,. an "expensive 'luxury" in these hard times. ,When the worthy citizens of our town have the "thrifty" speeches of the authority fresh in their minds, and hear of their actions a few days later, the inconsistency of the counsel be- comes particularly noticeable. Trusting our re- tweeted Council will profit by the misadventure. ' "PRO BONO PUBLICO." August 6, 1915.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0184" n="art0184">
        <head>AWKWARD CUSTOMERS.</head>
        <p>AWKWARD CUSTOMERS.</p>
        <p>Sirs—lt is a frequent complaint of teetohilist advocates that landlords and their servants do not exercise sufficient caution in serving doubt- fully-sober customers, but these fault-finders should put themselves in the place of the man behind the bar to realise thoroughly the diffi- culties of . the situation. Not only does it require an expert to judge a man's condition at a glance, but a violent scene frequently results from refusal to serve a customer who has already "had enough," and the publican or- barman has to risk assault and ' battery in the execution of his duty. Such a case came up at the Lymm Police Court on Wednesday last. and is reported in Saturday's "Examiner," where a drunken man who had been refused resisted the landlady, and when outside brutally assaulted, another customer who had assisted in. ejecting him. The magistrates' dealt' very leniently with the case—only inflicting a small fine. A severer treatment of offenders of this class would doubtless act as a deterrent, and greatly assist the licensee in his' trying task, in which he merits the support of the Bench and the sympathy of the public. T. E. SIMPSON. Liverpool, August 7th, 1915.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0185" n="art0185">
        <head>TUBERCULOUS CHILDREN. Trades Council Supports Residential</head>
        <p>TUBERCULOUS CHILDREN. Trades Council Supports Residential</p>
        <p>Schdois.</p>
        <p>At a' fairly well-attended meeting of.; the Warrington Trades and Labour Council held on Tuesday evening Mr. F. Berry presided. Mr. A. Seabury was also present. The Secretary (Mr. C. Dukes) read a letter he had sent on July 14th to the local Educa- tion Committee:' "From reports we under- sta.nd that your committee: is favourably dis- posed towards the establishment of a residen- tial school for- • tuberculous children. We venture to express our approval of the scheme; and hope that the matter will be carried for- ward." The reply received was as -follows : "I am in receipt of your letter of yesterday's date. . . . I' have no doubt that the com- mittee will be pleased to hear that your Council supports them in the matter.—Yours faithfully, J. MOORE MURRAY." We have heard nothing further," added tb7?"t-ecretary. Member explained that the Education Itruittee had no meeting in August; they Wfte on holiday. , Ur. Dukes: Well, that is Worthy of note— that they can have a holiday, and yet you corn- -131011 or garden workmen cannot be spared for • da:Y :unless you are summoned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0186" n="art0186">
        <head>RENTS EN WAR-TIME.</head>
        <p>RENTS EN WAR-TIME.</p>
        <p>War Emergency Workers Raise Trades Council Discussion.</p>
        <p>The question of house, rents in war time was raised at Tuesday's meeting of the Trades and Labour Council by a letter from the War Emergency Workers' National Committee, 'which solicited the co-operation of that Council in the supply of information on the cost of, living. "There does not," wrote the committee, -` appear to be any specific policy adkpted by landlords generally; so we shall be glaNto hear, any facts that have come under the notice of your organisation. If these facts can be supplemented by the number of avail- able empty houses in your district we shall be obliged. These facts are frequently to be obtained from the local authority. Mr. J. Hamblett remarked there was a pre- vailing argument that the cost of living had not increased so much!</p>
        <p>The Secretary (Mr. C. Dukes) said he did -not know what increases in rent had taken place, but did not think there had been any- uniform movement in Warrington. But he thought there.would be one before long. A Delegate: I don't think you will find a couple of dozen houses in Warrington where rents haven't gone up. The Secretary: That reminds me that we have Housing Committee somewhere! (Laughter.) will write the Town Clerk for the informa- tion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0187" n="art0187">
        <head>NATIONAL GUILDS LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>NATIONAL GUILDS LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>Rev. Dr. Mellor And Mr. J. Dalby , Among Its Supporters. The Secretary (Mr. C. Dukes) read to the Trades and Labour Council on Tuesday a letter from a new organisation—the National Guilds - League,-sayingthey would like to send one of their lecturers to Warrington during the coming, season. The people, continued the Secretary, v'ere organising Trade Unionism on the basis of Industry versus Craft as it used to be in the Guilds. It really was a propaganda organisation and their ultimate -object was for National Guilds to absolutely control industry. As an educational body they desired that their view should be known as widely as possible to Labour bodies, and they would be open to criticism. Also they would help the Labour bodies so far as they could. Warrington might be able to get a man down from Manchester. The Rev. Dr. Mellor, late of Warrington, and brother to Mr. W. Mellor (0 the League Council) was one, and Mr. S. balby, late assistant master at the Grammar school, was another. , The,Chairman (Mr. Tz. Berry) said they en- dorsed this object so, far as. being willing .to invite a speaker down to Warrington with a view to deciding whether they could really support the movement or not. Mr. J. Hamblett mentioned that seeing their own Secretary knew so much about it he could have given them half-an-hour's address at one of their meetings. However, he moved that if it were possible to get a lecturer down from - Afanchester or Liverpool they might leave the arrangements with their Secretary.</p>
        <p>It Was proposed and seconded ,that further Information be sought in the matter before tile- Council attached itself or invited a Sneaker. This .was carried unanimously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0188" n="art0188">
        <head>LORD'S PRAYER.</head>
        <p>THE LORD'S PRAYER.</p>
        <p>Have The Christian Countries Lived Up To It?</p>
        <p>THE RECTOR ON A YEAR OF WAR.</p>
        <p>At a number of local churches the comple- tion of a year of war was marked on Sunday by special sermons. At the Parish Church, the Rector (Rev. Canon Willis) chose as his text: "And when he was come near, he beheld the city and wept over it, saying: If thou hadst known, even thou, at least in this thy day, the things which belong unto thy peace; trut now they are hid from thine eyes. For the days shall come upon thee, that thine enemies shall cast a trench about thee, and compass thee round, and keep thee in on every side. And ,shall lay thee even with. the ground and thy children within thee; and'shallknot leave-in thee one•stone upon an- other; because thou knewest not the time of thy visitation." The words (the Rector said) we're very appro- priate to the present day. The preacher pro- ceeded to emphasise the three main features of the theme: the Divine sorrow for Jerusalem, which was so dear to the heart of our Lord; the culpable ignorance of the Jews, who, after cen- turies of preparation for the coming of the Messiah, rejected Christ; and the just retribu- tion which overtook them in the destruction of Jerusalem by the Roman Army, and the dis- persal of the Jews all over the world. Alluding to the sufferings of the Jews since their exile, the Rector said he hoped that one of the results of the war would be that they would be treated very differently. The promises of the Czar had opened out a new prospect for Poland, which had such a large Jewish population, and the Russians now looked upon the Jews from a very different point of view from that which obtained before the war. How It Applies.</p>
        <p>• The first great question he wished the-conm- gation to think about' was: Does God care for all our sorrows at the present time, and on whose aide is He? God never changed; He was full of real love for all; we were made in His imaged and had been redeemed by .I",esus Christ, and we-ruight be sure that He did care for this terrible world-wide sorrow. Both the great forces opposed in the present strife claimed God to be on their side. The congrega- tion no doubt read the prayers, recently pub- lished, of the Kaiser and the Czar; he (the Rector) had read both and compared them. We might 'be quite sure that God was on the side of right. Who was to blame for the war? The Germans said they were being attacked on all sides, and that they started th# war for their town preservation. On the other hand, we said that our very life and existence were at stake, and we believed that we were fighting for jus- tice, liberty and religion. But, while we were convinced that we had right on our side, we</p>
        <p>had our faults. First there' was culpable ignor- ance.</p>
        <p>What Germany Was Doing.</p>
        <p>"I think," the Rector went on, "we ought to have known what Germany has been doing for the last 40 years. There were those who knew it, and they were not believed—notably, two soldiers, Lord Roberts and Robert Blatch- ford. I often read their utterances and thought over them. They knew why Germany was pre- paring that vast army and that powerful fleet, but most of us thought it did not matter in the least. I think that was very culpable ignor- ance." Everywhere, before the war broke out, people were thinking of little but pleasure, amusement and self-indulgence. There was no real religion—and there was not real religion at the present day amongst many of our. people. If there was, all our churches would be crowded. We thought of the Russians as a nation upon their knees before -God. Could we say the same of England? How very few came to the intercession services; how very few regular communicants there were amongst our Church people, and how small the congrega- tions were, as compared with what they should be.</p>
        <p>Two Thousand Years Since The Lord's Prayer.</p>
        <p>The preacher proceeded to allude to the divisions amongst Christians. Two thousand years had passed since our Lord's great prayer that we might be one, and still the Catholic Church was divided into two great branches— east and west. Then. there were the separation 'between the Roman Communion and the Church of England and further divisions be- tween Nonconformists and the Church. What must that be in the sight of Christ? "I am persuaded," the Rector averred, " that if people would read the Bible and Church history im- partially, and make it a matter of earnest prayer, that would be the means of bringing us together. We know that the Church of Eng- land is the one and only branch of Christ's Holy Catholic Church in this land.". In conelUsion, the Rector dealt with the questions: Is the war a deserved judgment? and Whet are we going to do when the war is over? He did not look upon it exactly as a punishment but as God intervening in love and mercy to make us think. It was a very awful state of things, and perhaps we had much more to go through, but if we would only receive it aright the nation would he purified. When this war was over we would think more about other nations than ourselves; we should not think only of making money and enjoying ourselves, but Would endeavour to follow in the footsteps of Jesus Christ and to make this world a heaven upon earth. Then we should find that the war had been a judg- ment in, mercy and love. preparing us for that ,Heavertly home which God wanted us to enjoy with Him for ever.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0189" n="art0189">
        <head>DEBT WE OWE.</head>
        <p>THE DEBT WE OWE.</p>
        <p>The preacher at the Parish Church at even- song, the Rev. T. Vickers, alluded to: the great debt which we owe to the men who have laid down their lives for us during the war, and urged.the.congregation to shew kindness to the _ .</p>
        <p>departed by prayer for their souls. He -CIO; as his text: "Blessed be he of the Lord who hath not left off his kindness to the living and to the dead." In French newspaper he read that "the French are very kind to the dead"; it seemed to suggest that we in England were not so kind. The preacher proceeded to refer to the marvellous way in which our military organisation cared for the Hying, especially, the splendid work of the Army Service Corps and of the medical services. He showed how dili- gently the chaplains sought the welfare of the souls of the soldiers amid the perils of the battlefield, and pointed out that death was but the gate of everlasting life, and that at the burial , service we prayed that the departed might have " their perfect consummation and bliss." The war had upset many preconceived notions, and he hoped that one which would be destroyed was that we had no more concern with the souls of men after death. We hon- oured the bodies of our departed heroes—as witness the reverent tributes paid at the graves in Flanders by our own King—but we could best show kindness to their souls, which lived for ever, by praying for them. It would be to or lasting shame if we allowed the heroes who- had given ,their lives for us to be forgott6. Nowhere in the Bible or in the Prayer Book were we forbidden to- pray for the departed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0190" n="art0190">
        <head>PRAY WITHOUT CEASING.</head>
        <p>PRAY WITHOUT CEASING.</p>
        <p>Special ,intercession was conducted at each of the services at St. Paul:s on Sunday. "Tray Without. Ceasing," was the text chosen by the Rev. E. Downham, who said that the nation 'was realising that behind all the material and mechanical was the spiritual and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0191" n="art0191">
        <head>MOTHER -- 9 BANISHES _ . _•.„.„ At ,r Tiq 1 ~ -,- • ii. t, -,.. . . ,SYRJPL : g 1.. f- 4 c .. d",,i: ;. ? fe..!:,"4 z.;:..1 ...... ''...,</head>
        <p>MOTHER -- 9 BANISHES _ . _•.„.„ At ,r Tiq 1 ~ -,- • ii. t, -,.. . . ,SYRJPL : g 1.. f- 4 c .. d",,i: ;. ? fe..!:,"4 z.;:..1 ...... ''...,</p>
        <p>L</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, AUGUST 14, 1915. THE WEEK'S WEDDINGS.</p>
        <p>eternal—in a word, God. They had seen that the leaders, of this mighty Empire would face the future in utter dependence on God. Throughout the Empire, from Anglican cathedral, from Mohammedan mosque, from Hindu temple, unceasing prayer had ascended to God. Let that encourage them to "Pray without ceasing?' In concluding, the preacher said.: " •11(-11.1 pray for victory; it is good to pray for peace. It is better to pray for justice, it is better to pray for liberty, it is better to pray for the triumph of right, for the victory of human freedom. Without shame pr regret we shall pray to God for victory over our enemies. We' shall pray that our nation may not bear the sword in vain against the evil doers. Some may question our attitude and remind us that Christ has pronounced the death sentence on war. We know that when Christianity is in the ascendancy war will cease. But we know too that Christ would never have us stand at one side while the weak are crushed, women outraged and, little chil- dren cruelly done to death. He would have us fight for the right, defend the weak, and suc- cour the women and children. Therefore. with a steadfast hope in God we shall face the future and 'Pray without ceasing.'"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0192" n="art0192">
        <head>"WE CAN GIVE THANKS."..</head>
        <p>"WE CAN GIVE THANKS."..</p>
        <p>Preaching at Christ Church, Padgate, on Sunday evening, the Rev. E. E. Chamberlain (curate-in-charge) said: A year has passed since the greatest war in •history began. That even the murder of the Archduke FranciS' Verdinand was ncit the real cause of the war, so far as it concerns England, is proved by General Botha's discovery of the Kaiser's telegram in 1913 offering to recognise a South African Republic provided the rebellion against the British Crown began immediately. The past year has been one in which Germany has put forth all her might to crush her foes. Nor can it be said without success. The cap- ture of Belgium, Poland and a good slice of France, tell their own tale., So far 'Finland has escaped with but little damage. What tale will .another year have to tell? That depends on what attitude the nation adopts. The point to remember is that God is proving us, whether we are worthy of the proud position we enjoy. We shall preserve the position only as we are true to our privileges, and with unanimity, avoiding all strikes as unthinkable in time of war, and which ought to be treated as identical with cowardice or desertion on the battlefield. If we are true to these principles we shall win through as a nation and as indi- viduals, not only in our battles with the enemy but more important still, we shall win the. battles in ourselves. "Greater is he that ruleth his spirit than he that taketh a city." Meanwhile we can thank God and take' courage for the glorious heroism of the men of all ranks of which our papers have been so full for some time past; thankful that we can drink the cup and endure.</p>
        <p>We can give thanks for all— First: Forothat supreme self-sacrifice' Which proved a little nation great, For those who knew the awful price, And made the choice, defying fate. The first to answer lionourN call. Give thanks for these: they gave their all.</p>
        <p>and then: For silent mothers, nroud and pale, For wives who smile a brave farewell; For women's hearts which never quail, Arnid the sights and sounds of Hell. For loving thought and steadfast hand, Give thanks to God, 0 Motherland.</p>
        <p>Truth from of old was England's choice, And duty was no empty phrase.</p>
        <p>Since Nelson cried. "England expects that EVERY. man," &amp;c.: We know the grave, authentic voice, We stand upon the ancient ways. We follow where our fathers trod, And, keeping troth, are true to God.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0193" n="art0193">
        <head>"601) AGAINST SATAN."</head>
        <p>"601) AGAINST SATAN."</p>
        <p>Father Whittle,. at. St. Alban's, Bewsey-street, on Sunday morning, said: "We have now concluded the first' year of the most terrible war in the world's history. More than, a million men have been killed so far, and double that number have been wounded, besides many innocent women and children, who had been Violated and slain. Whoever has, or have, caused this war, will have an awful account to give for these murders and outrages, and, what is worse, is that it is more than .probable that many more thousands,- perhaps millions, of lives will be lost before the end comes 7 I would remind you in a, few words of what I said soon, after the war broke out, namely, that on our part this is a just war, that we did not seek it, but were forced to draw the sword. And again I .remind you, as I did then, that We are fighting fou God 'against Satan, who has instigated this war. We are fighting for God. Empire against world- empire. We are fighting for King and country, for liberty against intended oppression and tyranny, for plighted faith against broken treaties. It is, therefore, a fight of right against might, a fight for a lasting peace against constant dread of war represented by huge standing armies. Ours is a fair fight as of sol- dier to soldier, not a wholesale massacre of civilians, women and children. You who can- not go to the Front, do your bit at home ,by continuous prayers in private, and come as often as you can to the daily public prayers said in this church. You can help also by your thrift, by leading sober and godly lives, thus inducing our merciful Lord to give victory to our arms, for only our victory can savour country and save the world. Pray also that our clear Warrington soldiers • may come safely through, sound in health and limbs."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0194" n="art0194">
        <head>CO-OPERATIVE GALA.</head>
        <p>CO-OPERATIVE GALA.</p>
        <p>Three Thousand Children Feted On A Wet Day.</p>
        <p>.The annu.ril ,children's .gala.:given...by. the ,War- rington Co-operative Society, as held on Saturday. Owing to the inclement weather, however, the usual proceedings were consider- ably curtailed, and instead of the fete being held on the Warrington Football Ground, as originally decided, the children—numbering 3,soo—adjourned to the Warrington Hippo- drome (which was kindly lent by Mr. Will A. Jackson, the manager) and to the-Co-operative Hall. At the former place, the children were amused by a cinematograph display, while the Penketh Tannery Prize Band rendered selec- tions of music during the afternoon. At the Co-operative Hall the Borough Band helped to pass away' the time, assisted by a troupe of gymnasts, and also by a company of enter- tainers headed by Professor W. Wallace, of Horwich. As the children left the hall, each received a large bag containing fruit and chocolate. ,great praise is due to the committee for the arrangements and the excellent way they were carried out,. despite the disadvantage arising out of the inclement weather.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0195" n="art0195">
        <head>SIR GILBERT GREENALL AND MILITARY HORSE SUPPLY.</head>
        <p>SIR GILBERT GREENALL AND MILITARY HORSE SUPPLY.</p>
        <p>A comn4ittee has been appointed by the President- of the Board' of Agriculture and Fisheries- (Lord Selbourne) tcr consider 'and. ad- vise_ the Board what steps shoilld he taken to secure the production and maintenance in Eng- land and Wales of a supply. of horses suitable and- sufficient for military purposes. Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., Walton Hall, has been selected a member of the c.ommittee, the other members being: Lord Middleton (chair- Man), the Right Hon. Henry Chaplin, M.P., the Right Hon. Sir Ailwyn Fellowes, the Hon. Alex- ander Parker, Major Sir Merrick Burrell, Bart., and Capt. M. S. Adye. The secretary of the committee is Mr. E. B. Wilson, of the Board of Agriculture and Fisheries.</p>
        <p>" To Celebrate Your Luck." Hawker: "Buy a flower, sir?" Billion: "No, thanks." "Buy one for your wife, sir." "Haven't one."' " For your sWeetheart., then." "Haven't one either." "Well, buy one to celebrate your luck."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0196" n="art0196">
        <head>DUNA BIN-BELL.</head>
        <p>DUNA BIN-BELL.</p>
        <p>On Monday morning, at the Wesleyan Chapel, Penketh, a marriage was solemnised .between Miss Annie Bell, daughter of, the late Mr. % T. Bell (for some years ono,of the trustees of the chapel) and Mrs. Bell, of Burmholm, Penketh, and Mr. John Dunabin, Hope House, Penketh. The Rev. E. J. Gawne, of -Formby, officiated. The bride was charmingly attired in a gown of white crepe-de-chene and hat of crepe-de- chene and straw, draped with lace, and she carried a bouquet of white sweet peas. Miss Gertrude Bell (sister of the bride) made a pretty bridesmaid, and was dressed in crepe- de-chene, with hat of blacksftund white Tagel straw. She carried a bouquet of pink sweet peas and wore a pearl and ruby pendant, the gift of the bridegroom. The bride was given away by her uncle (Mr. W. J. Winn), and Mr. Arthur Dennett fulfilled the duties of best man. A reception was held at the bride's home. During the afternoon the,ybung couple left for the Lake District, wherehe _honeymoon is to be spent. Very many valuable presents were received. The bridecake, a particularly fine one, made by Mrs. Ralitlelge„ was a present rrom the bridegroom's mother.-40.-,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0197" n="art0197">
        <head>TUCKER-OXLEY.</head>
        <p>TUCKER-OXLEY.</p>
        <p>At the Wesleyan Chapel, Penketh, on Monday, a marriage was soleninised betweenifiss Ethel Oxley, daughter of Mr. and Mrs. Fred Oxley, of Station-road, and Mr. William Pickton Tucker, son of Mr. and Mrs. Tucker, of Victoria-terrace, Station-road. The bride, whose father is the stationmaster at Fiddler's Ferry Station, was charmingly attired in white silk. She wore an embroidered tulle veil, with orange blossom wreath, and carried a shower bouquet of white- lilies and asters... A gold brooch, the gift of the bridegroom, completed the bridal toilet. Miss Oxley was attended by Miss Haigh, of Kirkburton, cousin of the bride. The bridesmaid's dress was of white voile, and she wore a black hat with pink roses and a gold brooch, the gift of the bridegroom, and carried a shower bouquet of sweet peas. Mrs. Oxley, mother of the bride, wore a dress cf blue silk with Oriental lace and hat to match. The best man was Mr. Lewis Tucker, brother of the . bridegroom—in place of his brother George, who has joined the Grenadier Guards, and was unable to obtain leave of absence. The bride was given away .by her fathsr. -The Rev. H. 'l'. Dixon and the Rev. R. Thompson, pastor of the Congrega- tional' Church at Great Lever, Bolton, the latter a cousin of the bride, officiated, and Miss Bates, at the organ, played "The voice that breathed o'er Eden," and "0, Perfect Love." After the ceremony a reception was held in the Wesleyan ...Schoolroom, and later the happy pair departed for Colwyn Bay amid a ,shower of hearty good wishes:- The bride's travelling dress was a navy blue costume with hat to match. Many presents were received.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0198" n="art0198">
        <head>SUNDAY SCHOOL TEACHERS WED.</head>
        <p>SUNDAY SCHOOL TEACHERS WED.</p>
        <p>At St Margaret's Church, Orford, last Satur- day week, a wedding was solemnised between Miss Alice Evans, daughter of Mr. William Evans, of 50, Chester-road, and Mr. Albert Mayer, son of Mr. William Mayer, of 80, Orford- avenue. Both bride and bridegroom are teachers at St. Margaret's Sunday school, and quite a number of friends attended the wedding. The bride, who was tastefully attired in cream silk, and carried a bouquet of white heather, sweet peas and smilax, was attended b two bridesmaids, the Misses May Evans and Fanny Mayer, prettily dressed in -white silk and voile, with hats to match, and carrying bouquets of sweet peas and smilax•. The duties of best man were discharged by Mr. Gilbert Simcock, and the groomsman was Mr. J. Rudge. The/ bride was given away by her father. The ceremony was conducted by the Rev. G. Vale Owen, and the organist was Mr. Brownbill (deputy organist of the church). After the wedding a reception was held at the home-of -the bride's parents. About forty guests attended. The bridal pair subsequently left for Southport. the bride travel- ling in a fawn costume, with hat to match.</p>
        <p>REV. OGDEN AT CHATHAM. "A Sample" Of A Day's Work In Camp. The Rev. H. Ogden, who is spending a month with the troops at Chatham, Under the auspices of the Y.M.C.A., ,writes to say that he is very busy. "There is no place for a slacker in the work of the Y.M.C.A.'," he says. "Here is a sample diary: 9.0 a.m. commenced work in the Barracks. Visited the men who were in the Guard-room Prison; spent half-an-hour talking to the guards off duty about 'Divine things'; 11 a.m. to 1 p.m., sold stamps, postal orders and tobacco to the men; 2 p.m., went the round of blocks where the men lived distributing literature; 5.30 p.m., opened the evening pro- gramme, 'concert, with a brief manly service, the assistants meanwhile supplying refreshment from ginger'beer to Wild Woodbines; 9 p.m., the day was closed with prayer; 9 to 10 p.m., clearing up the room; 10 to 11 p.m., supper and hallowed intercourse with tired but joyful workers. A fair day that for a holiday day."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0199" n="art0199">
        <head>BULGARIA'S PRICE.</head>
        <p>BULGARIA'S PRICE.</p>
        <p>Wants Serbian Macedonia In Return</p>
        <p>For Co-operation. M. Radoslavoff, the Premier of 'Bulgaria, has in an interview with an American journalist declared his country's readiness to join in the -war if her terms are granted. "When the' Triple Entente," he says, "can assure us that this territory (i.e., Serbian Macedonia) will be returned to, Bulgaria and our minor claims in Grecian Macedonia and elsewhere, realised, they will find us ready and waiting to fight with them; but these guarantees, must. be real and absolute—no mere paper ones can. be-accepted. Only ~c.e.x*,l#l. cao: induce Bul- garians again to pour out their blood." M. Radoslavoff hints that without Bulgarian help Turkey cannot be conquered. Against the authoritative Bulgarian view may be set the less influential opinion of the Serbian Minister in Rome. This diplomat argues that it is useless to purchase Bulgaria's help, because whatever terms she 'may be granted she will always find some excuse for doing nothing in the field. Only an 14kustro-German attempt to burst a way through ,the Balkans to Turkey would bring Bulgaria into line as•an effectual combatant</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0200" n="art0200">
        <head>HELP FOR BELGIAN FARMERS.</head>
        <p>HELP FOR BELGIAN FARMERS.</p>
        <p>Altrincham Agricultural Society's Novel Scheme.</p>
        <p>A novel scheme to relieve the distress among farmers in Belgium and Northern France has been adopted •by .the Altrincham Agricultural Society. The committee boasts of a Membership of over one hundred members; and these are to be exempted from subscribing to the society for two years, and are invited to .pay a similar amount to the fund. Farmers and others are also to, be invited to send-hcirticultural, agricul- tural and dairy produce to an exhibition to be held on September, 22nd, for which prizes will be awarded.. These exhibits will afterwards .be sold, and the entire proceeds,- which are to be supplemented by a donation. from. the society's invested capital.. are. to go to the benefit of the Agricultural Relief of Allies Committee- It is, expected that the exhibits will be of a large and very attractive character.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0201" n="art0201">
        <head>MR. REDMOND AND 11W POPE'S APPEAL.</head>
        <p>MR. REDMOND AND 11W POPE'S APPEAL.</p>
        <p>. Refusal To Press The Government. Mr. Redmond, in reply to Bishop O'Dwyer's letter supporting the Pope's appeal for peace, and soliciting his influence to move the British Government to take the initiative, writes that the latter step is not calculated to promOte the cause of peace, and he would not be justified in endeavouring to bring pressure to bear on the Government to enter into peace negotiations when the German Powers, the aggressors in this war, show no sign of any disposition to repair the wrongs inflicted on Belgium and our other Allies.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0202" n="art0202">
        <head>FARM AND GARDEN.</head>
        <p>FARM AND GARDEN.</p>
        <p>NCREASING THE HOME PRODUCTION OF FOOO. ,</p>
        <p>The committee presided over by Lord Milner, which was Appointed by the Board of Agricul- ture and Fisheries to consider what steps should 'be taken by - legislation or otherwise for 'the' purpose of maintaining and, if possible, in- creasing the present production of food in Eng- land and Wales, have now drawn up an in- terim report, embodying recommendations which the 'committee suggest'ought to be -put into effect in time to be realised in the harvest of 1916. These recommendations are briefly:— __ . . .</p>
        <p>The conversion into arable land of inferior pasture land; and the Compensation, where necessary, of farmers for expenses incurred in making the change. There• are also provisions for an agricultural maximum wage in certain circumstances and for the protection of the consumer.</p>
        <p>In the interests of farmers who will require considerable notice in order to alter the rotational crops, it is important that the recommendations of the committee should be decided upon as soon as possible to ensure the results hoped for at the harvest of 1916. For this purpose an Act of Parliament will be required. and it is most important tliat the matter should come before the House "Of,Com- mons at the earlies,t possible date.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0203" n="art0203">
        <head>HORSE BREEDING PROSPECTS.</head>
        <p>HORSE BREEDING PROSPECTS.</p>
        <p>Horse-breeding offers one of the most promis- ing,of the many bright prospects that confront -the farming industry. .The ,termination of the war, whether it comes" soon or late, will leave the belligerent and neutral countries exhausted of their horse supply. The wastage in horse- flesh, even if cavalry have not played the con- spicuous part in the conflict that was expected, has been enormous, and in heavy horses par- ticularly has exceeded all calculations. The country may consider itself fortunate which can retain a substantial nucleus from which to restore its normal strength in draught and saddle animals. The United Kingdom has every chance of being as favourably situated in this respect as any other country, neutral or belligerent, and it will be its duty as well as its privilege to buckle, without delay, to the task of repairing the impoverished supplies. It is not improbable that many of the industrial firms and business houses whose animals have been impressed will replace their teams with motor vehicles, but, making full allowance for the accelleration that will thus result in the process of superseding the horse, it is a po4tive certainty that the demand will exceed the supply for many years to come, and that those who breed a good class of draught or saddle animal will be assured of a handsome reward. At any rate, it is in this belief thAf the re- sources that remain in the country are being so fully utilised for reproductive purposes, and the fact that stallions of all breeds have ex- perienced so busy a season testifies to the wealth of the country 'in eligible mares.— Field"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0204" n="art0204">
        <head>DEBATABLE QUESTION ABOUT PASTURE.</head>
        <p>A DEBATABLE QUESTION ABOUT PASTURE.</p>
        <p>It may be observed that in grass land farm- ing there are districts in which it is customary to mow the same meadows year after year, while pastures are always kept for grazing and are never mown. In another part, however, grass fields are grazed and mown alternately and the advocate of one method never succeeds in persuading the other that he is wrong My vote is given in favour of the first mentioned. The object of the meadow is to get good crops of hay, and by moving land year after xear the tall and vigorous .types of grass that are, best for the purpose are encouraged. On the other hand the continued grazing of a pasture encourages the development of dwarf and tender herbage, which animals appreciate, and as the years ncro on the field becomes a carpet of the kind of forage that is desirable. I make these remarks after careful observation of the results which have followed, under both systems.—" Agricultural Economist and Horti- cultural Review."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0205" n="art0205">
        <head>IN THE GARDEN.</head>
        <p>IN THE GARDEN.</p>
        <p>/ EARLY POTATOES.</p>
        <p>Those who have breadths of early potatoes would be well advised to lift them, as in many cases the foliage is quite yellow and the recent heavy rains will cause the tubers to form a second growth.</p>
        <p>A GOOD PLANT FOR WINDOW-BOXES. One of the finest plants for the front of a window-box' is the white harebell (Campanula isophylla alba), now coming into bloom. As a trailing plant it is one of the gems of the family, the flowers being large and of the purest white. Such an easily grown plant should be used for window-boxes, and with a background of Ampelopsis Veitchii is very effective when in bloom. It is one of the most free-flowering of the harebells, and is an excellent own plant. The great thing in its culture is to keep it well watered and occasionally to give it weak doses of some artificial manure.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0206" n="art0206">
        <head>TUFTED PANS:ES.</head>
        <p>TUFTED PANS:ES.</p>
        <p>Now that the beauty of these is on the wane, steps should be taken for increasing the stock, and a bed may be got ready at any time. A rough frame is of great benefit, as this can ho easily shaded if, as often is the case, the weather is very bright after the cuttings have been put in. When the flowers are past their best, cut off the long, straggling shoots which have flowered. In the centre of the clump will be found vigorous growths that make ideal cut- tings, these in many cases being well rooted. It is not advisable 'to grow a great number of varieties, as a good effect can be had with one really good sort in the various shades of colour now to be had. A friend of ours sent us this year the white miniature form, which has been for some weeks a mass of bloom, and if the weather remains moist will keep up, a succes- sion for some time to come. This section of the tufted pansy deserves attention, as the plants, owing to their drawf creeping habit, form huge elumps in the course of a season. and always flower freely for many years if they are given a little attention in the way of mix- ing some well-rotten manure and loamy soil in the centre of the plants..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0207" n="art0207">
        <head>SWEET PEAS.</head>
        <p>SWEET PEAS.</p>
        <p>No flowers bear qr pay for cutting freely more than sweet peas, fbr the more the flowers, are cut the better and more continuous will be the crop of blgom. When flowers and not seed- saving are the primary' consideration, all the seed-pods and faded flowers should be removed before the swelling of the seeds has had time to weaken or exhaust the bine. If this be done, twice the amount of flowers will be forthcom- ing, provided attention is paid to watering with an occasional dose of liquid manure allowed to</p>
        <p>percolate through a mulch of well-rotted horse or cow dung.—Wick, in "Farm and Home."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0208" n="art0208">
        <head>CRICKET.</head>
        <p>CRICKET.</p>
        <p>RYLANDS'S RECS. v. MR. HAWTHORN'S • TEAM.</p>
        <p>The match, Rylands's Rees. v. Mr. It Haw- thorn's XI., should have been played at Gorsey, lane on Saturday, but was postponed owing to inclement weather. It will be played at Gamy- lane on. Saturday, September 4th.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0209" n="art0209">
        <head>TO-DAYS TEAMS.</head>
        <p>TO-DAYS TEAMS.</p>
        <p>RYLANDS'S RECS. v. RUNCORN METHODISTS.</p>
        <p>To be played at Runcorn; start 3 p.m. Rylailds's Recs. will be selected from the fol- lowing players: E. P. Sanders (captain),, S. Carter, T. Knight, F. Horrocks, G. Whitfield, W. H. Bell, J. Constable, G. -Duckworth, W. Bennett, G. Woodcock, F. Lemon, G. McGowan, 3. Wrench, 3. Ashton and C. Kenworthy. Tram to ineet at 1.45 p.m. at Bank Quay Station. Train leaves 1.55 p.m.</p>
        <p>When a roan loses his heart hiss head has to do double work.</p>
        <p>Men with blue or grey eyes are almost in. variably the best shots.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0210" n="art0210">
        <head>!WITH THE WOODS</head>
        <p>!WITH THE WOODS</p>
        <p>WORKS' ASSOCIATION,</p>
        <p>A Tie In The Semi-Final Stage.</p>
        <p>The semi-finals of the "I inlafson " Cup Conapetition in connection with thl Warring- ton and district Works 11:o.vling les( ciation were played at headquarters, the Horse and Jociley, Winwiek-road, on Saturday, in the- presence of a large numher of enthusiasts. Two very interesting games were witnessed. Great excitement prevailed during the contest be- tween Monks, Hall and Pearson and Knowles' (Bewsey Forge). In the last game Bowering, of Pearson and Knowles', was seven ahead before his opponent, Baker, had scored. Baker, how- ever, subsequently pulled up in sensational fashion and scored the requisite fifteen while Bowering registered nothing. Baker's achieve- ment was very remarkable. Before he started scoring, his opponent needed only one point to make the game safe for his side. So finely did the Monks. Hall man play that the one point necessary for a Pearson and Knowles' victory was not forthcoming, and ,the match ended in a draw of 1443 all. The replay takes place to- day, starting at 2.30. The final, between the winuer:of this game and crosfield's (Cheshire), will commence at four o'clock. The Mayoress will present the cup and medals at the con- clusion of the game. Scores Monks, Hail. s P. and K. (Bevesey). E. Baker (-6) W. Thomason, (2) 8 W. Littlefair (6) .... 15 J. Timmins (4) J. ;Barrett .(7) A. Ellison (4) , 15 J. Probert (5) T. Jefferson (6) ...... 15 W. Blackledge (3) ll G. Hubble (4) B. Higham (4) E. Jones (6) C. Trantei (4) 15 J. Coyne (6) P. Gleave (7) ....... 15 J. Bradshaw (3)' 3 W. Pritchard (6) —.. 7 P. Cowell (3) - 15</p>
        <p>Cresbekl's (Cheshire). Assurance Agents, No. 1. A. Gleave, son. (2)... 15 T. Gorton (6) .• lO A. Yates (5) ........ 15 S. Robinson (4) l3 A. Gleave, jun. (3)... 15 R. Squires (5) D. High= (4) S. Taylor (5) H. Cook (2) J. Archer (5) W. Unsworth (4) .... 15 'E. H. Mann (G) ll E. Appleby (4) J. Middleton (3) ..... 15 J. Mellor's (5) .8. Green (4) A. Payne (5) l5 F. Seed (5)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0211" n="art0211">
        <head>LANCASHIRE COUNTY AMATEUR ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE COUNTY AMATEUR ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>Second Team Championship.</p>
        <p>FINAL TIE.</p>
        <p>The final tie of the Second Team Champion- ship of the Lancashire County Amateur Associa- tion was decided at the West Leigh Conserva- tive Club on Saturday. The competing teams were Warrington Cemetery and the Co-opera- tive Wholesale Society, Irlam, the former team finally recording a fine win. After the first six games had been decided, the Irlamites were leading by one point, but eventually the Ceme- tery made a fine rally, and victory was secured when J. Plant gained a magnificent win over J. Povah. The Cemetery Hotel won by 32.. The following were the scores : Want</p>
        <p>Cemetery Hotel. R. Warburton</p>
        <p>. 15 J. Mills</p>
