        <p>• 33 .. 21</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0212" n="art0212">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION.</head>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION.</p>
        <p>Friendly Matches Arranged— But Warrington Stand Firm.</p>
        <p>"The Veteran," writing in .the "Daily Dis- patch," says:— The Northern 'Union. League clubs are taking the bull by the horns. The ruling body at their annual meeting in Manchester decided to have no competitive football except for youths under military age, but they did not debar clubs from arranging friendly matphes. These, however, will have to be played without pay- ment to professionals. As I have previously pointed out, the Nor- thern 'Union game is not so very popular that it can afford; to keep out even for one season, and the professional Rugby code will very pro- bably have a lot to thank its clubs and players for when the wat is'over. With very few exceptions—Wakefield Trinity, Warrington, and Widnes are all I have yet heard of—the clubs in Lancashire and York- shire are doing their best to keep the game before the public, and I was particulatly pleased to note that at their recent meeting the Yorkshire clubs decided to publish -a weekly table of results. This is one way to make Rugby in the coming season more popular than it would be if friendly matches 'were played without any re- cord being kept, and the tables will sustain the public interest. I presume the clubs in Lan- cashire will do the same thing., If Warrington, Wakefield, and Widnes desire to stop out—their grounds for so doing may seem somewhat peculiar to many people—they will make room for clubs that have been struggling valiantly for years to get in the first flight of football, and I hope they will not' be forgotten- when times-become normal again.' Clubs that remain faithful to the League now are doing a usefur.work. It is recognised that outdoor recreation is required by the workers more than ever it was, and more harm than good would be done were the curtain to be closed down on adult football altogether. I . trust Rugby in the North will pay its way. The fixtures of the Yorkshire clubs have been practically completed, and among those who have fixed up matches are Halifax, York, Hud- dersfield, Dewsbury, Bradford, Hull, Hull Kingston Rovers, Brighouse Rangers, Batley, Bramley, Leeds, and Hunslet. Many of the Yorkshire clubs have also fixed up with teams from Lancashire, and -vice versa, and altogether the outlook is very promising..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0213" n="art0213">
        <head>WARRINGTON'S DECISION.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON'S DECISION.</p>
        <p>We have on several occasions explainea the reasons why Warrington will not entertain the idea of playing senior football this year, and those reasons still stand. We shall be enter- tained by ,the juniors, .and for the remainder concentrate upon the prosecution of the war both in the works and in the field.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0214" n="art0214">
        <head>INTERNED/ATE RUGBY</head>
        <p>INTERNED/ATE RUGBY</p>
        <p>LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>The above League will ,be carried on next .seation under fjmilar rules as last season. The age-ree will, mot probably, be as before —players must be under 17 on the Ist of September. A meeting will be held on Thursday next in the Pavilion, Wilderspool, at 7.30. All who are interested are invited to attend. Applications for membership of thp League can be made at once by post to Mr. E. JACESON ItisE, Hon. Sec., Mersey View, Penketh.</p>
        <p>The Hon. Secretary will willingly ansiver any further inquiries and would be glad to receive the names of those who would like to be plated on the referees' list.</p>
