      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0215" n="art0215">
        <head>RUNCORN; GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN; GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Board of Guardians was held at Dutton Work- house on Tuesday, the chair being ocetpied by the Chairman (Mr. C. E. Linaker, jun.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0216" n="art0216">
        <head>CONDOLENCE.</head>
        <p>CONDOLENCE.</p>
        <p>The Chairman moved that a letter ef sym- pathy be sent to Mr. Eaton Bretherton, who has recently lost- his wife. Mr. Linalrer re- marked that Mr. Bretherton had been associated with the Board longer than most members could remember, and they all regretted to hear of his sorrow. The proposition was carried in silence, the members standing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0217" n="art0217">
        <head>CHILDREN'S DAY OUT.</head>
        <p>CHILDREN'S DAY OUT.</p>
        <p>The Children's Home Committee reported that the children had an enjoyable, day at Overton Hill, where they were conveyed in vehicles lent by Messrs. Baxter and Fowles. It was agreed that these two gentlemen and Mr. Hallows (who entertained the yvangsters) be cordially thanked for their kindness.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0218" n="art0218">
        <head>INMATES.</head>
        <p>INMATES.</p>
        <p>The Master reported that the number of in- mates last week was 124.,as compared with over 130 in the corresponding period of last year. The number 014 vagrants was 95, as compared with 69.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0219" n="art0219">
        <head>RUNCORN RURAL COUNCIL</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL COUNCIL</p>
        <p>Economy And The Outicok</p>
        <p>For The Future.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of this Council was held on Tuesday, Mr. T. Antwis presiding.</p>
        <p>MR. BRETHERTON'S LOSS.</p>
        <p>On the motion of the Chairman, a vote of condolence was passed with Mr. Eaton Brether- ton on the loss of his wife.</p>
        <p>HALTON CASTLE WATER SUPPLY.</p>
        <p>Mr. Jas. Wilding wrote stating that con- tinued attention had been given to the Castle Hotel water supply, which was now satis- factory.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0220" n="art0220">
        <head>NOTIFICATION OF BIRTHS ACT.</head>
        <p>NOTIFICATION OF BIRTHS ACT.</p>
        <p>A circular letter was read from the Local Government Board explaining the provision of the Notification of Births (Extension) Act, 1915, which brings into force the Notification of Births Act, 1907, making its adoption com- compulsory in areas in whick it is not already adopted. The Acting Clerk (Mr. W. E. Hough) said that this matter had been before the Board previously and was referred to the Medical Officer prior to his taking up military duty. As some expenditure would be involved, it was necessary that the matter should be con- sidered by a committee. The matter was referred to the Finance Com- mittee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0221" n="art0221">
        <head>CALL TO ECONOMISE.</head>
        <p>A CALL TO ECONOMISE.</p>
        <p>A circular letter was read from the Local Government Board drawing the attention of the Council to the need for the organisation of labour and the restrictions of borrowing, with the consequent postponement of many.. desir- able works. The need for increased imperial taxation rendered the question of local rates one of very pressing importance, and empha- sised the necessity for local authorities adopt- ing a different attitude towards expenditure to assist the individual to meet the increased calls in respect of imperial taxation. It was ex- tremely important that every effort should be made to reduce the local rates; and local authori- ties should, as far as possible, refrain from • requiring the execution of work, the cost of which had to be borne by private individuals, unless the work was urgently necessary. " Bled Almost to Death." . Mr. Linaker, sen., said this was one of the most sensible circulars ever sent out by the Local Government Board. Unfortunately there had been legislation in the past which had not asked local authorities carefully to consider matters and exercise their powers. wisely, but had compelled them to spend money in order to comply with Acts of Parliament. It was- like food not properly prepared being forced upon a person and resulting in chronic indigestion, which only time could cure. In the last ten years public expenditure had gone up from 87 millions to 134 millions; and a good deal of expenditure had been forced upon local authorities who moved rapidly -where they would have moved cautiously but for the force t of, the Board. With regard to the closing clause, the private individual had been bled almost to death, and it was now thought that the only way to save him was by stopping the bleeding. The Council should try to reduce its expenditure in order to save its rates. Mr. Woodward said that he thought"that the District Council had foreseen the necessity for such a recommendation as that, and had really prepared itself for the step. They had ahnost carried out the requirements of the letter be- fore the letter arrived. They knew that there had beea a large expenditure in the past. He did not agree with all Mr. Linaker had said, because there had been necessity_ and oppor-</p>
        <p>tunities for_ improving ‘their social conditions, though be admitted that some things might have been carried too far. Mr. Linaker : That is just my point. Mr.:Woodward, continuing, said that if public a*horities had not done as much as they had done their sanitary conditions would be much worse than they were to-clay. All they could do now was to maintain ,the status quo at as low a cost as was compatible with efficiency. A Serious Outlook. The outlook for the future from the stand- point of taxation and rating was, in his opiniory a most serious one, and he thought the meneyithey would in the ordinary course have been using for improvements would be needed for the national exchequer. They had before them a time such as none of them ever remem- bered, and he thought economy would have to be observed not only in public life, but in the private life of the individual. Mr. King suggested that the Council might pass a- resolution expressing its approval of the circular.</p>
        <p>Lina.ker, jun.: Don't flatter them too much. (Laughter.) Mr. Woodward sugge.sted that the best way of showing appreciation would be to appoint a general committee to take the oversight of the Council's financial situation, and assist the other committees in deciding what expenditure was necessary. _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. Linal:er, sen., said that such a com- mittee would control the functions of the otter committees.</p>
        <p>Linaker, jun., suggested that the Finance Committee was the proper authority to under- take the duty. After some discussion the consideration of the matter was adjourned until the next meet- ing.</p>
        <p>;</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0222" n="art0222">
        <head>WIGAN FOOTBALL CLUB FIXTURES.</head>
        <p>WIGAN FOOTBALL CLUB FIXTURES.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the members of the Wigan Rugby Football Club on Thursday evening approved of the action of the committee in arranging fixtures with all Lancashire clubs .in,..nkcanbe.rs•hip with. the Northern League, the fixtures to be friendly ones, and .to be on a strictly amateur basis. It was announced that the majority of the available players .had agreed -to play on condition that their, travelling.- ox- and meals were allowed.</p>
