      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0227" n="art0227">
        <head>Education:</head>
        <p>Education:</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, AUGUST 14, 1915.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0228" n="art0228">
        <head>TWO WARRINGTON V.C.'S.</head>
        <p>TWO WARRINGTON V.C.'S.</p>
        <p>Tombs' Predecessor Helped To Capture Arpley Cannons</p>
        <p>FIFTY YEARS AGO.</p>
        <p>Just at present, when enthusiasm runs rife throughout the town at Corporal Tombs hav- ing gained • that highest and most-coveted military honour, the Victoria Cross, it should be noted that the Corporal is not the first Warringtonian to obtain it. Our first Victoria Cross, in fact, was brought to the town no less than fifty-six years ago. The gallant and pnly soldier who gained for his native town this rare distinction was Private William Norman, of the 7th. Royal Fusiliers, and he did so during the Crimean. War. Also he served through the Indian ' Mutiny. This V.C.'s wife, whose age is now 78, lives in Manchester.</p>
        <p>These and the following particulars we have obtained from the V.O.'s only sister, Mrs. Sarah Rowland, of 28, School-brow—who was but ten years of age at the time of the Crimean War. Mrs. Rowlands, whose husband is a ilagger, slater and general repairer, has now three sons in the Army—Privates William, Daniel and Albert Rowland. Of these, the Ara and third are members of the 4th South Lancashire Territorials, whilst the second be- longs t 4), the National Reserve. Private Rowland was recently wounded and gassed (as reported in "The Examiner ") and on June 16th underwent the experience, some- what unusual even at the Front, of being blown up into the air and afterwards buried in the trench, out of which he had to be dug.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0229" n="art0229">
        <head>BORN IN BUTTERMARKET.</head>
        <p>BORN IN BUTTERMARKET.</p>
        <p>Private William Norman was born in 1832 in Naylor-street, off Battermarket-street, being third of the six sons of Mr. and Mrs. Ralph Norman. In his youth and early manhood he worked on the Sankey Canal and the River. Mersey as a waterman 'under his father, who was captain of a flat. Presently, however, a patriotic regard of his country and his young Queen caused him to relinquish this work and join the regiment stated. This example, inci- dentally, was shortly followed by his younger brother, Edward. William had not been in the Army many years when the Crimean War broke out like a thunderclap, with startling suddenness. At 'that time he had reached the age of 27 years, and his home was with his mother in Factory- yard, Bank Quay. Along with all other sec- tions of the Army, the 7th Royal Fusiliers were, of course, called up for service, and in a very short time found themselves embarked upon the war in Russia.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0230" n="art0230">
        <head>WOUNDED IN WINTER CAMPAIGN.</head>
        <p>WOUNDED IN WINTER CAMPAIGN.</p>
        <p>The rigours of that terrible winter cam- paign in the Crimea are still vividly—only too vividly—renkembered in thousands of fami- lies, but by none more vividly than that of Private Norman. Yet he went through it with a cheerful courage—and his hardihood was noticeable even when, after some months of this dual fighting against the Russians and the 'natural conditions, he was forced to give up for a time on account of having been wounded in the right leg. The bullet which wounded him went in at one side of his leg, and occupied many weeks in working out at the other side. Indeed, at one time it was thought his limb would have to be amputated; but Norman told the doctors that "if he had to die, he would die with both legs on." Luckily he recovered, and realised his desire to return to the regi- ment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0231" n="art0231">
        <head>FOOTSTEPS IN THE NIGHT.</head>
        <p>FOOTSTEPS IN THE NIGHT.</p>
        <p>It was shortly after this return that Norman gained the V.C. on the Heights of Inkerman by. saving the regiment as a result of. cap- turing thee Russians. It was night-time, and he was on sentry-duty outside the barracks where all his regiment was sleeping. Pre- sently he thought he heard footsteps, and quietly laying his gun on the ground -threw himself -down and put his ear to the soil. Now he could distinctly hear the apprirwn of three of the enemy—whose mission, it was afterwards found, was to blow up the bar- racks and_.all in it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0232" n="art0232">
        <head>SAVING THE REGIMENT.</head>
        <p>SAVING THE REGIMENT.</p>
        <p>Norman, in a manner not altogether devoici of humour, suddenly sprang up from his con- cealment, shouting "Up, boys, and s•ts 'era!" The surprised Russians, thinking they lad walked into the midst of a number of guards, at once threw down their arms, n Lerettpon the sentry rushed at them, disabling one in an instant and taking charge of the other .wo, whom he conducted to prison. Shortly, be returned for, and dealt with, the third ore. In this way he saved some hundreds of com- rades from terrible agony and delith. THE SEBASTOPOL CANNON.</p>
        <p>It is also noteworthy, and of considerably greater local interest, that Private Norman took part on September 9th, 1855, in the capturing of the cannon at the Fortress of Sebastopol, which now form so imposing a memorial in front of Arpley Station. "As a memorial of the suffer- ings and of the triumphs of the British Army in the Crimea—Alma, Inkerman, Bamarsund and Sebastopol,'" these were presented to the town by her Majesty's Government, the late Mr. Gil- bert Greenall bearing the expense of their mounting. At the great function, held ia 185.7, in 'celebration, of their erection here—at which thousands of enthusiastic Warringtonians at- tended—all sorts of speeches were made upoa the war, and several of the speakers—notably the late Mr. Benjamin. Pierpoint—referred in full detail to Private Norman's gallantry.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0233" n="art0233">
        <head>CROSS OF THE QUEEN.</head>
        <p>THE CROSS OF THE QUEEN.</p>
        <p>That, of course, was after the war, but Norman's deed had been recognised long before that, for it was only a short time after the Cap- ture that Norman *got the news that he 4, had been awarded t great distinction, along with the usual pen . Thereafter he served to the end of the wait, nd on his return to Eng- land was summoned to Londoil, where Queen Victoria herself pinned the cross upon his breast at Buckingham Palace. He was not, howeve,r, able to visit his native town, as, his regiment was once again hurriedly required this time to go out to the Indian Mutiny, through which the V.C. also served, gaining another medal of honour. Altogether Norman gained during his eleven years' mili- tary career the V.C., three medals and four clasps—a,performance rarely equalled in so short a time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0234" n="art0234">
        <head>MARRIAGE AND DEPARTURE.</head>
        <p>MARRIAGE AND DEPARTURE.</p>
        <p>Five years after this Private rman's period of service expired, and he left the Army to marry Miss Elizabeth Green, of Buttermarket- street. Thenceforth he was employed for a niimber of years as an ironworker by Mr. Roger Irons at High-street, off School Brow. His home was in Gerrard's-row (opposite How- ley-lane, now the site of Messrs. Rylands' works), and later in Eldon-street. Eventually the popular hero—who still maintained his con- nection with the Warrington Volunteers—took up his abode in Hulme, Manchester, where he remained until the end of his life. In the course of time his V.C. pension was more than doubled; but he only lived three years to enjoy this increase„passing away at the age of sixty- four, on Malin 13th, 1898. Thus he lived for 37 years to enjoy the honour he so gallantly earned, and now lies at rest in the cemetery at Hu tate. " BLIND HERO'S" TRIBUTE.</p>
        <p>C. H. Bartell, the "blind Crimean hero." Nwhe lost his sight in the campaign which•roade Neuman similarly fathous (only in a more pleasant manner), and the "royal poet and the author of 'Flags Of All Nations ' " was a devoted -comrade of our first T:C., and attended his funeral at Hulme. Immediately after- wards he wrote the following poem. -which was printed and sold in aid of the widow. If only to afford :an example of the "blind hero's" work and of work which enjoyed the acknow- ledgment of every member of the Royal Family, tkis poem is worthy of reproduction:—</p>
        <p>I Another hero'a dead and- gene, He sleeps in peace to wake no Burro, Angels his spirit they have born* To that bright and golden- shore. , William Norman was his name, He left his home and friends so dear In fifty-four to cross the main, He fought with me through the Crimea. He joined the Seventh Royal Fusiliera, And,if you'll read that famous story, All on the Heights of Inkerman,</p>
        <p>Continued in Next Column.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0235" n="art0235">
        <head>PUBLIC AMUSEMENTS.</head>
        <p>PUBLIC AMUSEMENTS.</p>
        <p>'EVERYTHING IN THE GARDEN'S-' AT THE ROYAL COURT. .</p>
        <p>A merry and bright revue, with the un- finished title of "Everything in the Garden's -, is the fare at the Royal Court 'theatre this week, and good business has been &amp;me. Rollicking fun, inspiriting music, criginal songs, together with pretty dresses and artistic and elaborate scenery and effects, go to make up a most entertaining show. The capable cast, includes Miss Marguerite Ismay and Mr. Gus Ern, both of whom are very successful in their respective parts. Mdlle. Tina Aguzzi (dancer) and the Six Cairo Girls and an ani- mated and captivating chorus contribute admirably to the enjoyment of appreciative audiences.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0236" n="art0236">
        <head>"The Rosary" Next Week.</head>
        <p>"The Rosary" Next Week.</p>
        <p>Habitues of the Royal Court Theatre will welcome the return visit of Mr. John Glen- dinning and his talented company in "The Rosary." Unlike most plays based upon a religious subject, ' The Rosary" has an abund- ance of humorous situations and characters. It is not sectarian, as the chief character, Father Brian Kelly, is what gvery clergyman tries to be—a man thoroughly' human and a servant of</p>
        <p>Divine truth. Mr. John Glendinning will appear in his great creation, Father Kelly— and Miss Jessie Millward will appear in the dual role of Vera Wilton and Alice Marsh (twin sisters). Theatregoers should note that this play should not be confused with any other play bearing a somewhat similar title. This is the original play of "The Rosary," and was brought from America by 'Mr. John Glendin- ning, and produced at the Elephant and Castle Theatre, London, in 1913. The beautiful song, "The Rosary," will be rendered during the last scene.</p>
        <p>--,.4 '',•*;,,tir;, " •-,-,4,''', -4,,,,,r. ,4111,;:- -7:;40tt1,f,4 tm.,-, -5. v., 445..i.,,,,,;:g5t5,,,,„„., .i, • -,- :44 ~4,,.*, _7,4,, •,..ziwgztr,,,,-„,,;,,ii.. '',it',4t:',-1,-4,6;427,r,-,..z - 4-"%, ::,,,,,,i'r .t.- ',' V?. '?,,•"':11,4„, ":. 4.4.,..5::",„re, , , :7'4' l','-'.. '. • y',4lA--'.4'" -,,:-4,,,,,,,,,wyr- ,,,,,,,..,-., .. "-e-lt: - :cf.: ',,y,*,,,',..-!k''..• Pe '' ~t':*":- ••-‘..l,':ll4f;:t.;',*e- 44.,,,,4i'''l ....k• .;*,*4.,sitt?"s4*.-P-:"..itV2.i):-. - . 1,4,4 -4....c-tiT,T, .. • -"!,,,,,,,:..1.=,,,,,piee...1.;„:: ~, , v.' ... - -..e4- - - -,t, . 4:,*(,,q. ,2 ',.• ~', . t ..• : 4 ~, a i Th, airtT,Vt, ~, •+% _t,... _l, _ ~: i ~, .;•;' ..1--C '.,,,,..1',,,.' 1 :,- , ..,,„,x.T.:5"-tt,','..vk. -1 : L.,-,;,=-Ati..4.t.i •_,,,g-ii.--.&lt;4. ,• -•-• i --,,ff-V44-.7....e..v ').-i, Vs. - - .yi-`O,- ..,L,.-•••.,----, , '-'-f ~Tir ~ .:'.,v,,, v,.,...t.</p>
        <p>MR. JOHN GLENDINNING as Father Brian Kelly.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0237" n="art0237">
        <head>COMEDY SPECIALIST AT TIIE HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>A COMEDY SPECIALIST AT TIIE HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>The comedy man who goes in for mimicry generally presents a variety show; that is to say, he does not confine himself to imitating one thing or one type of individual, but rings the changes and gives a series of impersona- tions that sneak well for his versatility. This is a day, however, of specialisation. It is in- controvertible that to specialise nowadays is ono of the principal means of rising to the height of one's avocation, an'd the truth was never, perhaps, more vividly emphasised than at the Hippodrome this week, where large audiences have been delighted by the "turn" given by Vivian FoSter, the clerical comedian. He confines himself to an impression of the character indicated in his description, and he does it as -we do not remember to have seen it done before. The audiences have laughed till they could laugh no more. "Yes, I think so!" This turn in only one of several *first- rate contributions. Indeed, the whole pro- gramme- is an excellent one. Charles Mott, the well-known baritone,' who has had the honour On several occasions of, appearing be- fore the King, sings finely. The Mandos give a wonderful and sensational acrobatic act, which fairly .makes the spectators hold their breath. A novel and breezy musical act is Presented by the Four Royal Scots. Laurie and Elsie, vocalists and dancers, introduce Bunny and Bogie, two juvenile comediennes and dancers; the "turn" is a very acceptable one, and the same may be said of the contri- bution of Eddie Mostyn, comedian and dancer, and Maudie Gray, chorus comedienne. The Ga,umont Graphic completes a thoroughly entertaining bill. Good Bi!I Next Week.</p>
        <p>Next week's show promises to be excellent. Viviano and Co., appear in "The Toyshop," in which Mark Lupino is the " star " performer. There are comedians, gymnasts, singers and dances to make up a strong bilL</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0238" n="art0238">
        <head>MISS JENNIE ARMSTRONG AT THE STAR KINEMA.</head>
        <p>MISS JENNIE ARMSTRONG AT THE STAR KINEMA.</p>
        <p>• "Some" thrill has been the order at the Star Kinema this week. For, although of a touch- ing character, "Little Grey Home in the West" —which was the first principal film—contained many exciting episodes and attracted large audiences. That local favourite, Miss Jennie Armstrong—always in much demand—appeared at all houses, and sang the well-known ballad bearing the same title as the film. Naturally, she met with a fine reception. Another "grip- ping" drama was "The Black Mask." "Pathe's Animated Gazette," as usual, came out as a " tip-top " war edition; whilst "With the Heavy Guns in France" directed attention to the fine work being done behind the French firing line. "In Oran (Algeria)" proved to be a beautiful scenic film. Oa the humorous side the all-conquering (in one sense only) Charlie Chaplin tries an Elopement; the results are better seen than described - To-day's programmes are greatly attractive, for the chief drama is "The Painted World," and has a good lesson to teach.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0239" n="art0239">
        <head>Next Week.</head>
        <p>Next Week.</p>
        <p>For Monday, Tuesday and Wednesday the principal film will feature Charlie Chaplin in "Charlie by the Sea," and on Thursday, Friday and Saturday "The Girl of the Golden West" will be shown.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0240" n="art0240">
        <head>TWO WARRINGTON V.C.'s.—Continued.</head>
        <p>TWO WARRINGTON V.C.'s.—Continued.</p>
        <p>How they carved their way to glory. Shot and shell was madly flying, Still brave Norman he was there. Surrounded by his comrades dying, Face to face with the Russian Bear. On the dastard foe came dashing, In the midst of all the fray, Sword and bayonet they came crashing, Still the foe he kept at bay. Dreadful whilst the battle lasted, Heavy was Old England's loss; Norman he three Russians mastered, For which he gained the Victoria Cross. To the Indian Mutiny then he went, There his courage did not lack. Like as before, true to the core, He fought again for the Union Jack. The fatal battle he has fought, And to the front he's had to come, It was a struggle ithere for life or death, But God above the victory won. He leaves behind a widow kind, One noble 'son to mourn his loss, At the age of Sixty-Four we find No more to gain a "Victoria Cross." May his two daughters dear, Their widowed mother long protect, And do their best her heart to cheer, And to her wants ne'er neglect. Full Military Honours has been done</p>
        <p>To that Hero once so brave, The *Dead March" and the "Muffled Drum" With the volley fired o'er his grave. The "Union Jack" it was his pall,</p>
        <p>A thousand on the scene appears, Relations, too, so kind and true, And the 23rd Royal Welsh Fusiliers. Hark! it is the angels' trumpet-call, The golden gates are open wide, To Tit you know both one and all, That Norman he has passed, inside.</p>
        <p>Within those heavenly -realm's aboYe He's free from grief and pain. I pray that we again shall see In Heaven, that Hero Norman again.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0241" n="art0241">
        <head>STORIES FROM THE TRENCHES,</head>
        <p>STORIES FROM THE TRENCHES,</p>
        <p>DON'T FORGET " KITCHENER'S."</p>
        <p>An Appealing Message From Soldiers</p>
        <p>In The Desert.</p>
        <p>We are in receipt of a letter from four War- rington members of Kitchener's Army in the Mediterranean Expeditionary Force. We pub- lish it in full:—</p>
        <p>"A Battery, 69th Brigade R.F.A., 13th Division British Mediterranean Expeditionary Force, July 22nd, 1915. "Dear Editor,—l now write thanking you for the space you allowed me in The Examiner' of July 3rd, 1915. We received the paper with very great pleasure. The lads of Warrington enjoyed reading it as we have had no news of what is happening in the good old Lancashire town. By special request I am now writing to your paper, hoping you will allow us—l mean the 'Lads of Kitchener's. Army— a space in one of the columns. "We are situated on a desert, with sand surrounding us for miles around; and as for the heat of the sun it is very nearly roasting. ." All we lads are enjoying the very best of health considering the hardships we are facing. Each of us is thinking of our warm-hearted friends left behind and wishes to be remem- bered to the townspeople of Warrington, though we are doing our bit in a far distant land. "_We should be greatly pleased if a kind lady or gentleman would answer our, call by send- ing the 'Lads of Kitchener's Army' a gift of the old 'Woodbine cigarettes,' for we cannot get a smoke for love or money. The gift would be welcomed here by the lads. , "The Warrington Territorials are well spoken of in your paper, but we don't see anything mentioned of the Warrington \ lads in Kitchener's Army, R.F.A., who are doing their share in the British Mediterranean Forces. "Well, I must now conclude, hoping you will put our small item in your paper, which Kitchener's Lads' of Warrington hope to read very shortly. "Signed, "Bombardier P. BROADHURST, "Driver G. W. ISFIERWOOD, "Driver W. COUSANS, "Driver T. HILL, "The Lancashire Lads."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0242" n="art0242">
        <head>"I COULD HAVE CRIED."</head>
        <p>"I COULD HAVE CRIED."</p>
        <p>" Tommy " lio Was Much Affected</p>
        <p>By Colonel's Death.</p>
        <p>Private Thomas Dawson Braman, son of Mr. John Bramell, of 61, Dudley-street, Warring- ton, who until he joined the 9th Battalion King's Royal Rifles in November was employed at Garton's seed warehouse, writes home under date August 4th. He states that he is out of the trenches, and proceeds:— " I suppose you have read the week-end paper ! The sky was a flame of fire. Our battalion made a great charge and captured a lost trench. I am sorry to say I have lost some more ,of my comrades. Jack Andrews got wounded, but only slightly. What a shock I had when I learned that our Colonel had got killed! I could have cried. Poor old chap; he was a father to us. The gallant 9th gets praised on all sides. One of our officers was wounded three times in the head, but "stood it" till the end: He has been recommended for the Military ‘Cross. I Parapets of Dead.</p>
        <p>"For three days it was a tornado of fire. Hell was not the name' for it. I shall never forget it as long as I live. When our stretcher- bearers brought out the wounded the Germans opened fire on them. It was a ghastly sight to see, but we paid them back for it. The Germans were building parapets of their dead. They poured liquid fire on to a regi- ment who lost the trench; and then we came up and reeaptured the trench. We had fifty hours' incessant shell and rifle fire, also a number of bomb attacks. The Germans counter-attacked twice, but we repulsed them It must have been complete butchery to the German troops; and they deserved it Our Colonel died a gallant death. He was one of the finest in the British Army."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0243" n="art0243">
        <head>SLEEPING AMONGST TOMBSTONES.</head>
        <p>SLEEPING AMONGST TOMB- STONES.</p>
        <p>Graphic Leiter From An Orford "Tommy." -</p>
        <p>"There is a cemetery a few yards away, where we have spent -three nights among the tomb- stones." This is an extract from a letter penned by, a local soldier at the Front. His interesting narrative is published in the August issae of the Orford Parish Magazine. We quote fur- ther : " If the people at home will only work hard, and ensure us an unlimited supply of shells, the war will be brought to a speedier conclu- sion, as when the Germans begin to bonaba-d any position occupied by us it simply rains shells for the time. . . I hear Lady Greenall is here, close to the hospital. She was to come and have a look at me a couple of days ago, but I have not seen her yet. The nurses and doctors are very good to us here. They are all American people.</p>
        <p>An Untouched, Statue.</p>
        <p>"I would like to be home, but we have a duty to do, both to God and our dear King and coun- try; so we ,must not think too much of home, although dear old Orford is always in my memory. . . . We go through a village named every time wd go into the trenches, and it is heartrending to see the people's houses nothing but ruins. There is—or rather was—a beautiful church, with what seems to be the vicarage by the side; but both are in ruins. On the altar, which is still standing, is a statue of our Lord, full. size. It is 'beautiful, and, although the church has no roof and no win- dows, the statue is untouched, except for a few scratches, caused apparently by falling debris. There is a cemetery a few yards away, where we have spent three nights among the tomb- stones. It is absolutely untouched. An Exciting Night Experienceh</p>
        <p>"I have a little experience to tell you about while in the trenches from June 15th to the 21st. For about two hours they were very quiet, never sending any Star Shells up, so we got ready to man the parapet. It is not a nice job—it makes one shake a bit. Just then our listening patrol came in and reported that they had a working party on our barbed wire, so you Pan see the reason that they were so quiet. The officer. asked for volunteers to go out and scatter them if possible. We generally have a few rounds rapid, but we were all done up after the excitement coming in, when we were shelled and lost one man, so we tried another way. Four men—one N.C.O. and myself volunteered to go—so over the parapet we went, crawling over our barbed wire into the long grass between ours and their lines. We got within fifty yards of their barbed \wire, and there were between ten and twenty men hard at work, 'so we crawled in a direct line back to our trenches, and gave the exact position of the workers. You can guess what happened. We did not fire, but we sent two rifle grenades right in amongst them, as we saw by the star-shell that we sent up at the same time; and I am sure that he was a lucky man that es,caped, as it went right in amongst them. Anyhow, our listening post at daybreak said that they heard the stretcher-bearers at work; so we must have done some damage. But the sensa- tion going out between the two lines isn't up to much, although it is all right as long as there are a few of you; but you must not breathe scarcely, as the least sound would give you away, and then the game would be up. The captain of. our company said it was a good and daring piece of work: that is all we have heard about it so far.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0244" n="art0244">
        <head>Service Stopped by Guns.</head>
        <p>Service Stopped by Guns.</p>
        <p>"Last Sunday we were having service in a field at the back of our billet. Our minister had got part of the way through the service when the great guns, only 50 yards at the back of us, began to fire. The noise was so great that the service was; stopped; but Only for a few minutes, and then it went on as if there had been no interruption at all. It sometimes makes one wonder,, as our mbiister told us he has been asked many times, why the Lord still lets the war go on The other day _ _ _ _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>one s-niper was caught. IT. bad his rifle and even his face and hands painted green; so you see to what extent the Germaits will</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0245" n="art0245">
        <head>"NEVER FORGET THE,Mic"</head>
        <p>"NEVER FORGET THE,Mic"</p>
        <p>" Bullets Seem To Be Like Peas To Major Crosileld."</p>
        <p>We cull the following from an interesting letter writttp by one of Warrington's Terri- torials andiPublished in the current issun of the Orford Parish Magazine:— "We were told three days before what the job was, and all the boys were glad to get the chance we had been waiting for so long. I think we made good use of it. Not only was the position taken, but it was held against several counter-attacks, and still remains in our possession. The German trenches were bombarded, for three hours, and if men at home were present when a bombardment is going on they would soon realise why so many shells are required to bring this war to a close. The Warrington 'Terriers' came out with flying colours, and will de the same again when the time cornea I got</p>
        <p>hit in the right knee 'with a piece of siren whilst in support for the charge a week last Wednesday. We were billeted about eight miles from the trenches, and were sur- prised when we heard about the charge.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0246" n="art0246">
        <head>Four Lines Taken.</head>
        <p>Four Lines Taken.</p>
        <p>However, we got put in position ia a newly-dug trench just behind our reserve trench and about 460 yards from the Germans. 'We had hardly been in five minutes when they started to send their tram-cars over, dropping all around us. After about quarter-hour's bombarding by our own guns we saw our lads running towards the Germans' first lino of trenches, which they took quite easily. Shortly afterwards they made another run and took three more lines; they had to retire out of the fourth line, but managed to take it the follow- ing-night. I managed to last till 10 o'clock, and then I came in contact with a piece of shell. I managed to walk to our dressing- station, which was about a mile from the lines, to be dressed, and I had to walk another two miles through --- to another hospital, where we. got on motor 'buses and were taken to a Canadian Hospital on the coast ef France just near the sea. " My Hat Goes Off to Major Crosfield."</p>
        <p>"I am getting on fine and m 7 wound is heal- ing champion, and, I can tell you, it is a pleasure to be out of the sounding of the big guns." "We had a good scrap at -, and our boys did not falter. They went into it Al, and we would have gone farther had we been allowed to. My hat goes off to Major Gros- field. He is everywhere where there is fight- ing. Bullets seem to be like peas to him. He deserves every praise. It does your eyes good to see him walking about amidst shot and shell as if there is nothing up. He deServes every honour they can bestow on him. Good luck to him." " Thank you for the magazine. I spent a very pleasant hour reading th‘ extracts from the' letters from the lads and I also very §m(uch enjoyed one of the articles in it as regards parents and sweethearts standing in the way of them doing their bit out here. I expect there are lots of such cases, but the sooner they realise what is at stake the better. We have sofnething to thank God for that this war is not taking place in England. I have just got up from writing to look at the effects of a shell they have sent over not half a minute ago. They have knocked in part of a. cottage about 400 yards away. What would that mean at home? We are not without a bit of humour at times. When we are close to the wires you will hear a German shout in broken English, Stop snipe Anglish,' and say to each other, 'l'll bet that's the Jocks in r</p>
        <p>"There are fields of corn growing round about; but I fear they will be set on fire, when they are ripe, with shell fire." Orford " Doing Its Whack,"</p>
        <p>[Dated from the Convalescent Base, Bonen]: "I am ready to go back now and have another smack at them-7-but the last one was a taster, I can tell You; and some don't look forward to going back. I'd rather it was all over, but we have got to do our duty, and that's the end of it. I think little Orford is doing its whack for the country."-" I shall not forget June 16th and 17th as long as I live. Our battalion was in reserve, and the bombardment started at 2.50 a.m. and lastd three-quarters of an hour, and -inside half-an- hour we had captured their three lines of trenches with hardly a casualty, thanks to our artillery. It was grand. They smashed all their trenches to atoms, and, somehow or other, we, got mixed up with other regiments. In spite of .their heavy shelling we stuck to the trenches until we were relieved next morning. That night they tried their new method of war- fare, and started shelling us with gas shells. But still we stuck to them. At last, about nine or ten o'clock, it overcame *me, and I remem- ber nothing more until one of ,the reliefs roused me and another fellow who was in the same traverse and had shared the same fate. They took us down to the hospital. There I was kept for two days, and I Must say it is sur- prising how comfortable they make you under the circumstances, and how kind they all are, and I am ever grateful for all they did for me durint my short stay."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0247" n="art0247">
        <head>WAR HARVEST IN FRANCE. Strikinu Tribute To Our Allies'</head>
        <p>WAR HARVEST IN FRANCE. Strikinu Tribute To Our Allies'</p>
        <p>Children.</p>
        <p>Recalled to the colours at the outbreak of war, an Inspector of the .N.S.P.C.C.. serving as Battery Sergeant-Major "somewhere in France," sends home a very interesting letter. Both sides," -he writes, "are very strongly entrenched and each side is waiting for the other to attack. The talked-of dash for Calais has not been attempted yet. When it does take place I think we shall give the Germans a good thumping. All the troops here are wonderfully fit; they leek nothing in the way of supplies. At present there is talk of another winter cam- paign. If it is so we shall profit by last year's experience, and consequently we shall be made more comfortable. . . _ . . It is surprising _</p>
        <p>what little one knows of what is taking place, although you are on the spot. In a few weeks from now everybody hero will be harvesting the crops. There are splendid fields of wheat, oats, peas, beans, beetroot, plenty of ,potatoes, also maize. Every inch is under oultivation, most of it done by the women and children, a few old men and " unfits" thrown in; also a few men who have six or more children, who have been sent out of the firing-line brthe French. " Taking into consideration the shortage of labour it is simply wonderful the way things are kept going. Everybody in the farming line have to do their share, even the dogs; they draw carts, churn the butter, and cut up the food for the cattle, most of whom are stall-fed, there being very, little grazing except in the orchards.</p>
        <p>"It is surprising what a fine lot of children you meet here. I have not seen a single case of what we Chould call neglect. No matter how poor the family, or the size of it, the children are always clean, well shod and clothed. They feed them well and most of them drink beer for their supper. The last farm I was billeted at I. often saw a boy of seven years eat two and sometimes three eggs, with a couple of glasses of beer to wash them down; hi addition, he always had two slices of bread and butter, and one must remember that a slice off a loaf, which. is usually 18 inches in diameter, is some' slice. This little chap no doubt had a wonderful appetite. Another thing I have noticed, they all have splendid teeth. What the children are like in -the•towns I cannot say, as I have only been in a town of any, size once, and then only for a couple of hours. Comparing the children of the agricultural districts of our countyy with France, the children here are far better eared for.</p>
        <p>Unfortunately there are very few schools left standing, and education is at a standstill. Of course, the youngsters are not worrying about it!</p>
        <p>"Speaking of the 'crops, many of them were sown by men, women and children when under shell-fire, and they go on attending them under the same conditions, even now. We shall all be helping to get then in shortly-. "The people in this oountry have suffered terribly; no one can realise it unless they have seen it for themselves. They never know from one minute to another whether the farm will be shelled and flattened out. It is quite a common thing to pass a farm almost intact and the next day find it'a heap of ruins. the men of England could only realise what would happen to one country if the Ger- mane got a footing, there would be no difficulty in getting recruits."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0248" n="art0248">
        <head>in,</head>
        <p>in,</p>
        <p>-N,</p>
        <p>RAILWAY FARE ALLOWED.</p>
        <p>TO-MORROW'S REGISTER.</p>
        <p>How The Resources Of The Country Will Be Recorded.</p>
        <p>THE WAY TO ANSWER.</p>
        <p>Every man and woman must help the Govern' merit to take stock of the country's resources this week-end by filling up as correctly and carefully as possible the National Register form. We give below• some instructions as to how all can help the-Governmont to carrY out this important work.</p>
        <p>Keep your form of questions neat and cleaß. Do not tear or spoil 'it, and, if possible, avoid creasing or folding it. Write the answers plainly in the spaeto provided. Write your Airname at the head of the form in large letters. They. are easier to read.</p>
        <p>If. you have doubts a&amp; to how to answer any question. the enumerator who ,letti-es the forme at your house and collects them will help you if you a 4. him.</p>
        <p>If a form has not been left for you at the place where you sleep on the night of Sunday, August 15th, you should obtain one on. Moe - day and fill it up as soon as possible. if you are travelling on Sunday night arJ have not received a form before startinn• should Obtain one and fill it -up at the plf where ydti arrive on Monday morning. If yet have received your form don't leave it bebitio you. If you leave home after receiving a forrO; but before August 15th, take it with you aril harnd it, when filled up, to the enumerate. who calls at the address where you are temper' arily etopping. The same applies if you .are returning home after a temporary absence. • , You are asked to give your permanent postsi address. By this is meant the address where you can usually be found and to which lettero to you can be sent. If you ail, an employe "living in" (Yr, example, a domestic servant or shop assistant/ give your place of work and residence as yeol. permanent postal address. • After you have been registered yon will receive a certificate which you must sign alw keep carefully. Do not forget that. If Y°ll change your address and leave your hole! (otherwise than temporarily), you must send! your certificate to the Clerk of the Council el the district where your new home is, with rill. new address Written on the back (see instruction, on the hack of the certificate). The simPle: way is to hand the certificate in at the rot 19ffice nearest your new home.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-08-14_WidnesExaminer_art0249" n="art0249">
