        <p>Mr: R. McKenna,Chancellor of the Exchequer,says:- 44 The man, be he rich or poor, is little to be envied who, at this supreme moment, fails to bring forward his savings for the security of his counpy.," How you ca • jt. 4 invest • t - e yQfl . r 0..,• You can still take your share in the great 7„ War Loan through the Post Office. If you have saved sor more, go to the Post Office and buy L's of Scrip Certificates or as many of them as you can. These Certifi- cates will be exchanged in any Money Order Office between Ist and 15th December, 1915, for the same amount of 4-1% War Loan Stock. If you exchange your Certificates for Stock you will get interest at the rate of 5% per annum, or sd. per month for each £5 Certificate from the first day of the month after you bought the Certificate until 30th November; you will also get a bonus of 1/- for each £5 Certificate that you so exchange into Stock. After Ist December your Stock will bear interest at the rate of per annum, that is to say that each half year you will get 2s. 3d. for each .-e5 so in-vested. If you have only a few shillings handy go to the Post Office and buy a War Loan Scrip Voucher. You can have either 55., 10s., pr vouchers and you can buy as many as you like. Keep them carefully until Ist December when you can exchange each twenty ss. vouchers for a £5 Stock Certificate. You will also receive the interest your money has earned up to that time, and in addition a bonus of Is. for each £5 of Stock that you take. Afterwards, as long as you own your Stock, you will receive your interest on June Ist and December Ist each year. Remember you can at any time sell</p>
        <p>through the Post Office, If you cannot see your way to collect enough vouchers to buy 5 worth of Stock within the next year, do not buy vouchers but put your money into the Post Office Savings Bank. Co to the Post Iffice TO-DAY</p>
